Comparing the full \textit{FF} framework to \textit{PO} isolates the benefit of adaptive activations on a pruned graph. \textit{FF} generally achieves lower latency than \textit{PO} (e.g., Cora: 806.06s for \textit{FF} vs. 750.00s for \textit{PO} is an exception, likely due to measurement variance or specific interaction, but on Citeseer: 1759.96s for \textit{FF} vs. 1635.50s for \textit{PO} also shows \textit{PO} can be faster if the uniform activation in \textit{PO} is of a lower degree than the average in \textit{FF}'s adaptive scheme, or if the overhead of mask selection in \textit{FF} is non-trivial. This needs careful interpretation based on the actual uniform degree used in PO). More consistently, \textit{FF} aims to improve or maintain the accuracy of \textit{PO} by better tailoring activation complexity. For instance, if \textit{PO} uses a high uniform degree, \textit{FF} can reduce latency by using lower degrees for many nodes; if \textit{PO} uses a low uniform degree, \textit{FF} can improve accuracy by using higher degrees for critical nodes.

