In summary, both the pruning component and the adaptive activation component contribute to the overall performance of DESIGN (\textit{FF}). Pruning provides the most significant latency reduction, while adaptive activation primarily helps in preserving or even enhancing accuracy by intelligently allocating computational resources for non-linearities. The combined effect in \textit{FF} generally yields the best balance of substantially reduced latency while maintaining competitive accuracy compared to applying either optimization in isolation or using a baseline FHE GNN.

