\begin{abstract}
We study non-terminating graph rewriting models, whose local rules are applied non-deterministically---and yet enjoy a strong form of determinism, namely space-time determinism. In the case of terminating computation, it is well-known that the property of confluence may ensure a deterministic end result. In the context of distributed, non-terminating computation however, confluence alone is too weak a property. 
Here we provide sufficient conditions so that asynchronous local rule applications produce well-determined events in the space-time unfolding of the graph, regardless of their application orders. Our first two examples are asynchronous simulation of a dynamical systems. Our third example features time dilation, in the spirit of general relativity.
\end{abstract}
