The negative data values in Fig.~\ref{fig:4.8} require a positive $\cos(3\gamma)$, implying $\gamma_{\mathrm {U}}<30^{\circ}$. Since the Glauber model shows similar agreement to the full hydrodynamic model for $R_{v_2^2\delta \pT}$, it can provide high-precision estimates of $\beta_{2\mathrm U}$ and $\gamma_{\mathrm U}$. Right panels of Fig.~\ref{fig:4.8} show the Glauber model's prediction of the $\beta_2^{3}$ dependence of $R_{v_2^2\delta \pT}$ for different $\gamma_{\rm U}$ values. The calculations exhibit a linear dependence on $\beta_2^3$, independent of the $\gamma$.  As the Glauber model does not simulate $\pT$ dependence, we can only extract a broad constraint on $\gamma_{\rm U}$. The extracted values are $\gamma_{\mathrm{U}}\lesssim 17^{\circ}$ (0--2\% centrality) and $\gamma_{\mathrm{U}}\lesssim 13^{\circ}$ (0--5\% centrality) assuming $\beta_{2\mathrm{U}}=0.28$.

The ratio of $\rho_2$ can also constrain nuclear deformation. However, this observable does not have a simple linear dependence on $\cos(3\gamma)\beta_2^3$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:4.9}), due to additional $\beta_2^2$ dependence introduced by $\lr{v_2^2}$ and $\lr{v_2^2\delta \pT}$ in the denominator of $\rho_2$ (see Eq.~\eqref{eq:13}). Therefore, the ratio of $\rho_2$ is not as robust for extracting nuclear deformations.

