$$v(x_1)-\varphi(x_1)\geq 0,$$ but $v<\varphi$ with equality only at $x_0$ hence $x_1=x_0$. We conclude that every convergent subsequence of $y_{n}$ converges to $x_0$, and hence by compactness $y_n\to x_0$ as $n\to \infty$. From the argument above, we get also that
%$$-\dfrac{1}{n}\leq M_n\leq v(y_n)-\varphi(y_n),$$
%and hence
$$\lim_{n\to \infty} M_n=0.$$
Now, we complete the proof of the proposition. We define $\varphi_n=\varphi+M_n$. Notice that $$u_n=(u_n-\varphi)+\varphi\leq M_n+\varphi=\varphi_n,$$
with equality at $y_n$. Then, since $u_n\in \mathcal F$, we get
$$0\geq -L[\varphi_n](y_n)+f(\varphi_n(y_n))\geq -L[M_n+\varphi](y_n)+f(M_n+\varphi(y_n))=-L[\varphi](y_n)+f(M_n+\varphi(y_n)).$$
Hence, thanks to Lemma \eqref{lem:continuity}, the claim and the continuity of $f$, we conclude that
$-L[\varphi](x_0)+f(\varphi(x_0))\leq 0$,
a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}\label{rmk:stability super}\rm
 From Remark \ref{rmk:super}, we obtain a similar stability result for supersolutions, that is, if $\mathcal G$ is a family of supersolutions to $L[u]=f(u)$ and if $w(x):=\inf_{u\in \mathcal G}u(x)$ is continuous, then $w$ is aslo a supersolution.
\end{remark}

