{\it{\color{blue}Introduction.-}}
Phase transition, spontaneous symmetry breaking (SSB) and universality class of criticality are the core concepts in condensed matter and statistical physics~\cite{sachdev1999quantum,wannier1987statistical,girvin2019modern}.  For an unknown phase transition, we need to comprehensively consider its symmetry, dimension and other information to analyze its possible universality class of phase transitions. However,
it is a challenge to identify the universality of a phase transition, since the low-energy effective theory of a Hamiltonian can have a different symmetry from the Hamiltonian's. Higher symmetry may emerge at a first-order phase transition point~\cite{BWZhao2019,yan2021widely,Sun_2021}, continuous phase transition point~\cite{ding2016emergent}, multicritical point~\cite{Zhou_2025} and deconfined quantum criticality (DQC)~\cite{senthilDeconfined2004, senthilQuantum2004,sandvikEvidence2007,nahumDeconfined2015,qinDuality2017,wangDeconfined2017,senthilDeconfined2023}.

The symmetries associated with phases proximate to the deconfined quantum critical point (DQCP) are incompatible with the Landau-Ginzburg-Wilson (LGW) framework of phase transition. Such DQCP-scenario phase transitions by definition are continuous, which require the emergence of higher symmetries~\cite{nahumEmergent2015,tanaka2005many,senthil2006competing,liu2024emergent,huang2019emergent,wang2017deconfined,ma2019role,senthil2024deconfined,d2024entanglement}. Thus DQCP provides a novel paradigm beyond LGW phase transition theory, which has stimulated significant theoretical advances, including its connection to higher-dimensional symmetry protected topological states and 't Hooft anomaly~\cite{ashvinsenthil}, and fractionalized degrees of freedom~\cite{senthilfisher,nahumEmergent2015,maRole2019,sreejithEmergent2019,maDynamics2018}. Extensive numerical ~\cite{haradaPossibility2013, sandvikEvidence2007, louvbsneel2009, liuSuperconductivity2019, liaoDiracI2022, shaoQuantum2016, maDynamics2018,zhang2018continuous,liu2024deconfined,liu2022emergence} and experimental ~\cite{jimenezquantum2021, zayed4spin2017, guoQuantum2020, sunEmergent2021, cuiProximate2023,guoDeconfined2023,cui2025deconfined} progresses concerning the topic of DQCP have been reported during the past decades. 

Despite the extensive debate regarding the DQCP mechanism~\cite{takahashi2024so,zhaoScaling2022,deng2024diagnosing,song2023deconfined,zhou2024mathrmso5}, our focus in the present work lies in the determination of the intrinsic symmetries at the phase transition points. For instance, by analyzing the order parameters dimensionality in N\'eel phase and valence bond solid (VBS) phase of (2+1)D quantum systems combined with field theory analysis, an emergent $SO(5)$ symmetry can be theoretically conjectured and numerically verified~\cite{nahumEmergent2015}. However, a {\it priori} assumption of an order parameter may overlook some hidden dimensions, e.g., when a system possesses $O(4)$ symmetry, we can still obtain a $O(3)$-symmetric order parameter histogram even though we incorrectly assume only a three-dimensional order parameter, which will then lead us to an incorrect conclusion of a $O(3)$-symmetric system.
Here we aim to establish an unbiased approach to probe the underlying (emergent) symmetry at a critical point---without prior knowledge of its low-energy effective field theory---even in large-scale or/and high-dimensional quantum many-body systems.

A desirable approach would involve spontaneous continuous symmetry breaking, whereby its Anderson tower of state (TOS) {\it even in small finite system sizes} will then help identify the type of the broken continuous symmetry~\cite{anderson1952approximate,lhuillier2005frustratedquantummagnets,wietek2017studying}. 
However, on a second thought, we find this approach would be impractical for a quantum phase transition with emergent symmetry, e.g., DQCP. 
On the one hand, when directly probing the low-lying spectrum at the critical point, although the emergent symmetry is preserved, the spectrum is dominated by critical modes, e.g., a conformal field theory (CFT) tower~\footnote{A CFT tower applies if the critical point's conformal field theory is established; for the (2+1)D $J$-$Q$ model DQCP, this remains controversial}  is otherwise yield other than an Anderson TOS. 
On the other hand, by tuning the Hamiltonian parameters to shift the system away from the criticality and to potentially achieve SSB, we may concurrently hinder the higher symmetry to emerge.

The entanglement spectrum (ES) provides a solution. Similarly to the energy spectrum, the ES is also expected to exhibit an Anderson TOS when continuous symmetry breaking occurs in the ground state~\cite{Alba2013entanglement,Kolley2013entanglement,mao2025sampling}. 
However, we will show that, in addition to a ground states with broken symmetry, the ES displays a TOS even at a phase transition point and the TOS reflects the (emergent) symmetry of the phase transition point. The reason is that an entanglement Hamiltonian (EH) naturally introduces an effective temperature $T_E$~\cite{chandran2013how,Poilblanc2010entanglement,zyan2021entanglement,song2023different,li2023relevant,wu2023classical}. The ES actually describes the zero temperature physics of EH which can be treated as cooling from the phase transition point and spontaneously breaks the symmetry there. 

{\it{\color{blue} A general phase diagram of entanglement Hamiltonian.-}} A quantum system can be separated bipartionally into the subsystem $A$ and environment $B$. Therefore, we can define the reduced density matrix (RDM) as $\rho_A=\Tr_B(\rho)$. The EH was further defined as $H_E=-\ln(\rho_A)$ and its eigenvalue spectrum is ES~\cite{Li2008entangle,Poilblanc2010entanglement,XLQi2012}. According to the definition of EH and ES, $\rho_A=e^{-H_E}$ can be treated as a Gibbs mixed state of EH at $T_E=1$, where $T_E$ is the effective temperature for EH~\cite{zyan2021entanglement,song2023different}. 

In this way, a general phase diagram of the EH is proposed as shown in Fig.\ref{fig1}. The $J$ is a tunable parameter of the original Hamiltonian (OH). The dashed line $T_E=1$ is not only a line in the $T_E-J$ phase diagram of EH, but also the ground-state phase diagram of OH. The yellow point is the quantum critical point for OH and the thermal phase transition point for EH at $J_c$. Note that the quantum phase transition of OH becomes a finite temperature phase transition point of EH. 
In this way, the EH naturally introduces an extra temperature space $T_E$, SSB will happen through cooling the $T_E$ from the upper critical curves or point. Because the ES reflects the SSB of ground state at $T_E=0$, the different symmetries ($S_1$, $S_2$, $S_3$) in finite $T_E$ phase transitions can be detected, as shown in Fig.\ref{fig1}. 

However, whether the SSB induced by cooling temperature can reflect an emergent symmetry of the finite temperature phase transition (the yellow point in Fig.\ref{fig1}) is still an open question. Our numerical results later will demonstrate it. 

