{\it{\color{blue} Tower of states.-}}
To be more specific, let's consider the ground state of antiferromagnetic (AFM) Heisenberg model in 2D square lattice, which spontaneously breaks SO(3) spin-rotation symmetry to the O(2) subgroup. In the thermodynamic limit, the states are infinitely degenerate and the ground state manifold is a 2-dimensional sphere. However, in a finite volume calculation, the symmetry is always restored and instead we have a unique ground state and a “tower” of excited state~\cite{anderson1952approximate,lhuillier2005frustratedquantummagnets,wietek2017studying}. The tower of states can be described by an effective Hamiltonian~\cite{metlitski2015entanglement}, 
\begin{equation}
H_\mathrm{tower} = \frac{c^2 \mathbf{S}^2}{2\rho_S L^d},
\end{equation}
with $c$ being the spin wave velocity, $\mathbf{S}$ being the total spin of the system, $\rho_S$ being the spin stiffness, and $V$ the volume. For more general cases~\cite{Penc2003PRBSU4,Toth2010PRLSU3,Corboz2011PRLSU4}, it has been shown in 2D square-lattice SU($N$) Heisenberg model, the square of total spin $\mathbf{S}^2$ should be generalized to the quadratic Casimir operator $C_2 = \mathbf{J}^2$, where the components of $\mathbf{J}$ are the generators of the Lie algebra $\mathfrak{su}(N)$.
Such generalization also holds for the SO($N$) group and $\mathfrak{so}(N)$ algebra, and we find that the low-lying levels correspond to the fully symmetric tensor representation labeled by the 1-row Young diagram in which the $C_2$ eigenvalues can be evaluate by $\mathcal{J}(\mathcal{J}+N-2)$, where $\mathcal{J}$ is the number of boxes in the 1-row Young diagram and physically corresponds to the total SO($N$)-spin of the considered level. To sum up, we then arrive the general form of the TOS~\cite{Hasenfratz1993} in SO($N$)-SSB states,  
\begin{equation}
E_\mathcal{J}(L)-E_0(L)=\frac{c^2 \mathbf{J}^2}{2\rho_\mathcal{J} L^d}=\frac{c^2 \mathcal{J}(\mathcal{J}+N-2)}{2\rho_\mathcal{J} L^d},
\label{eq3}
\end{equation}
with $\rho_\mathcal{J}$ the SO($N$) spin stiffness. Note that the structure of TOS contains the $N$ of related group, thus it probes the symmetry.

{\it{\color{blue} ES of (2+1)D $O(3)$ criticality.-}}
To demonstrate that the TOS of ES can reflect the symmetry of critical point through cooling $T_E$, we firstly use the spin-1/2 columnar dimerized Heisenberg model on square lattice with (2+1)D $O(3)$ criticality to display our idea. The Hamiltonian is given by 
\begin{equation}
H=J_1\sum_{\langle ij \rangle}S_iS_j+J_2\sum_{\langle ij \rangle}S_iS_j
\label{HJ1J2}
\end{equation}
where $\langle ij \rangle$ denotes the nearest-neighbor bonds; $J_1$ and $J_2$ are the coupling strengths of the thin and thick bonds, respectively, as shown in Fig.\ref{Fig2} (a). Its ground-state phase diagram (Fig.\ref{Fig2} (c)) has been accurately determined by previous studies \cite{Matsumoto2001,ding2018engineering}. 

As shown in Fig.\ref{fig1}, $J=J_2/J_1$ is the tunable parameter of the OH in Eq.(\ref{HJ1J2}). The red region is N\'eel phase with $O(3)$ symmetry breaking and blue is the dimer phase. The yellow point here is a (2+1)D $O(3)$ critical point. The ES will reflect the continuous $O(3)$ SSB at $J=J_c,~T_E=0$. Here we choose a 1D chain needing few computer memory as the subsystem $A$ in a 2D lattice with periodic boundary condition (PBC) as Fig.\ref{Fig2} (a) displays. Through the newly proposed quantum Monte Carlo (QMC) method~\cite{mao2025sampling}, the RDM $\rho_A$ has been sampled and then diagonalized to obtain the ES with few computational resource. As Fig.\ref{fig:J1J2_spec} shows, we find that the ES at both $J=J_c=1.90951$ and $J=1$ display a structure of $O(3)$ SSB in which the tower is proportional to $S(S+1)$, where $S$ is the total spin. It is totally different from the energy spectrum at a critical point which is a CFT tower~\cite{francesco2012conformal,zhu2023uncovering}.
You may concern whether a finite size effect makes the ES at $J_c$ seem like the one in N\'eel phase nearby. Later we will show that if the symmetry of critical point is different with others, the TOS only reflects the SSB of the critical point. In other words, the TOS at $J_c$ exactly probes the symmetry of phase transition point.


