{\it{\color{blue} SO(3) spin $S$ reveals the TOS of the SO(N) superspin $\mathcal{J}$.-}} 
For more general SO($N$) TOS, in practice, we can only access part of the degrees of freedom and the corresponding quantum numbers of the subgroup. For example, the total spin $S$ of the SO(3) subgroup is the real spin we can measure directly. 
In these cases, the irreducible representations (IREP) of the original SO($N$) group will usually become reducible in the present of SO(3) subgroup. We can then still identify the SO($N$) IREP in the SO(3) spin sectors [c.f. TABLE~\ref{tab:tab1} for the cases of SO(5) IREPs decomposed into direct sums of SO(3) IREPs]. Alternatively, we offer a {\it Poorman's approach} by simply replacing the SO(N) spin $\mathcal{J}$ with SO(3) spin $S$ in the formula Eq.(~\ref{eq3}), i.e., 
\begin{equation}
E_S(L)-E_0(L)\propto S(S+N-2).
\label{eq4}
\end{equation}
It means the proportional relation still holds in a subgroup (real spin).
This can be justified by the fact that, the maximal $S$ a state can reach is $\mathcal{J}$, and the maximal-$S$ levels in the energy spectrum will then locate at the precise location of the corresponding SO($N$) IREPs.


