{\it{\color{blue} ES in $J-Q_3$ model with emergent $SO(5)$ symmetry.-}}
Directly, we explore the ES of 2D square lattice $J-Q_3$ model with emergent $SO(5)$ symmetry via measuring real spins with $SO(3)$ symmetry (see Fig.\ref{Fig2} (b)). Its ground-state phase diagram (Fig.\ref{Fig2} (d)) has been accurately determined by previous studies \cite{Lou2009,wangScaling2022}. 
As shown in Fig.\ref{Fig2} (b), the subsystem is a linear subregion labeled as $A$ and the remaining part is environment $B$. We still sample RDM via QMC and diagonalize the matrix through ED technique as we did in the dimerized Heisenberg model.
The $J-Q_3$ Hamiltonian  on the square lattice can be written as~\cite{Sandvik2007}
\begin{equation}
	\begin{split}
		H& = -J \sum_{\langle ij\rangle}P_{ij}-Q \sum_{\langle ijklmn\rangle}P_{ij}P_{kl}P_{mn}
	\end{split}
\end{equation}
where $P_{ij}=\frac{1}{4}-\mathbf{S}_i\cdot \mathbf{S}_j$.

It's worth noting that the $S$ for calculating the $SO(5)$ symmetry breaking should be the related $S$ operator in an $SO(5)$ group. However, the extra symmetry except the $SO(3)$ symmetry of real spins is emerged by the original $Z_4$ VBS order at the DQCP of $J-Q_3$ model to reconstruct the $SO(5)$ symmetry. Therefore, we can not measure the $S$ of $SO(5)$ directly, instead, we used the $S$ of the $SO(3)$
spin here, i.e., the real spin, as mentioned in the upper section.

The critical point between the N\'eel and VBS phases is around $Q/J=1.49153$~\cite{takahashi2024so,wangScaling2022,zhaoScaling2022}. According to the knowledge of TOS, the ES is proportional to $S(S+N-2)$ when the ground state breaks $O(N)$ symmetry. As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:JQ_spec}, the transverse axis of subfigures in the upper row are labeled as $S(S+1)$. When the $Q=0$, the groud state is a AFM N\'eel order with $O(3)$ SSB, the ES displays a perfect linear dispersion with $S(S+1)$. Meanwhile, we set the transverse axis in below row as $S(S+3)$, the dispersion of ES would be linear if the ground state is $SO(5)$ SSB. The subfigures show that the ES around the DQCP becomes linear, which reveals the emergent $SO(5)$ symmetry at the DQCP. 

We note that the $SO(5)$ DQCP is considered as a weakly first-order phase transition recently through many indications~\cite{takahashi2024so,zhaoScaling2022,deng2024diagnosing,zhou2024mathrmso5,song2023deconfined,wangScaling2022}, but our method still works even if there is an approximate DQCP with emergent symmetry.


