{\it{\color{blue} Checker-board $J-Q$ model with emergent $O(4)$ symmetry.-}}
Similarly, The Hamiltonian of chequerboard $J$–$Q$ model (CBJQ) on the 2D square lattice using singlet projection operators $P_{ij}=1/4-S_{i}S_{j}$ can be defined as~\cite{BWZhao2019,wang2024probing}
\begin{equation}
H=-J\sum_{\langle ij \rangle}P_{ij}-Q\sum_{ijkl\in \Box^{\prime}}P_{ij}P_{kl}
\label{eq:cbjq}
\end{equation}
where all indicated site pairs comprise nearest neighbors, $\Box^{\prime}$denotes same-colored squares on the black and white checkerboard. 

The ground-state phase diagram has been determined accurately by the quantum Monte Carlo method~\cite{BWZhao2019}. For $Q\to \infty$ the model is in the two-fold degenerate plaquette-singlet solid phase, and for $Q\to 0$ the system is in the Néel phase. At the first-order phase transition point $Q/J\sim 4.598$~\cite{BWZhao2019}, the model is ordered by breaking emergent $O(4)$ symmetry~\cite{BWZhao2019}.

At the first-order phase transition point with emergent $O(4)$ SSB, we also calculate the ES and draw it versus $S(S+1)$, $S(S+2)$ and $S(S+3)$, where the $S$ is the real spin with $SO(3)$ symmetry. As Fig.\ref{fig:cbjq_spec} shows, the TOS of ES clearly supports the emergent $O(4)$ symmetry in CBJQ model.

\begin{figure}[h!]
    \centering
    \begin{minipage}[c]{0.45\textwidth}
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_cbjq_L8.pdf}
    \end{minipage}
    \caption{Entanglement spectrum of checker-board $J-Q$ model with $L_x=L_y=8$ at the first-order phase transition point, subsystem is chosen as a chain with size $L_x^{sub}=8$. The linear relation $S(S+2)$ probes the $O(4)$ SSB here.}
	\label{fig:cbjq_spec}
\end{figure}

{\it{\color{blue} Conclusion.-}}
We have proposed a general method to detect the underlying symmetry of phase transition point numerically without any prior knowledge about its low-energy effective field theory. 
Using the extra temperature space introduced by EH, we calculate the ES of the system whose TOS actually reflects the SSB of related symmetry at critical point through cooling the entanglement temperature.
The good properties of TOS are perfectly preserved at finite size, making it possible to probe the SSB intuitively with little computational resource even when the correlation length of the system is large. For emergent symmetry, our scheme does not require to measure the related superspin but only observes the real spin to demarcate the symmetry because of the projection principle in group theory.
The numerical scheme is easily doable in both QMC and tensor network without searching in a huge parameter space~\cite{yang2023detecting}, several (2+1)D quantum phase transitions have been explored as examples.
This low-cost and low-technical-threshold method will greatly assist us in studying quantum criticalities of unknown systems and help construct their low-energy effective field theories.

