\begin{abstract}
Despite centuries of investigation, exact solutions describing nonlinear pendulum motion in the frequency domain have remained elusive. Existing formulations, while mathematically exact in the time domain via Jacobi elliptic functions, do not yield tractable, closed-form expressions for the frequency content of the motion, limiting both theoretical understanding and practical applications. This work presents the first complete, exact, and closed-form frequency-domain solutions for energy-conserving nonlinear pendulum motion across all regimes: swinging, stopping, and spinning. These solutions, expressed in terms of elementary functions and derived through an angular velocity frequency analysis, resolve a longstanding analytical gap in nonlinear dynamics. The derived expressions enable a consistent and rigorous treatment of all motion classes within a unified mathematical framework. Numerical validations confirm agreement to machine precision, and the simplicity of the formalism facilitates both theoretical exploration and potential applications in nonlinear physics and engineering.
\end{abstract}

\medskip
Keywords: nonlinear dynamics, nonlinear pendulum,  mathematical physics



\section{Introduction}

Despite its long history and being built upon the work of legendary mathematicians such as Euler in the 18th century and Jacobi in the 19th century, solutions for an energy-conserving nonlinear pendulum remain elusive and incomplete in the frequency domain. The lack thereof can be explained with the following arguments.

An energy-conserving pendulum is governed by the following equation:

\begin{equation}
	\frac{d^2}{dt^2}\theta(t) + \frac{g}{L} \sin \theta = 0 \label{eq_pendulum_eq}
\end{equation}

Although exact solutions of the nonlinear pendulum have been known for more than 100 years \cite{Belndez2007, Ochs2011}, they are limited to the time domain, involving a complicated special function called Jacobi elliptic sine function, $\text{sn}(z, k)$. It is true that an exact Fourier series of $\text{sn}(z, k)$ is also known \cite[{22.11.E1}]{NIST:DLMF}. In fact, the series has been used by Lima as an intermediate step to compute $\theta(t)$ \cite{Lima2018}. However, the form of the exact $\theta(t)$ is such that the Jacobi elliptic sine is wrapped inside an arcsine function. For example, $\theta(t) = 2 \arcsin[ k \, \text{sn}(\sqrt{\frac{g}{L}} t + K ,\, k)]$ \cite{Lima2018,Belndez2007}. Unless a clever mathematical manipulation is performed to unwrap it,  knowing the exact Fourier series of $\text{sn}(z, k)$ does not constitute an exact Fourier series of $\theta(t)$.

Studies of the nonlinear pendulum have employed numerical methods \cite{Singh2018,Simon1979} or sophisticated theoretical schemes \cite{Belndez2012,Johannessen2011,Mndez2010} to compute the Fourier coefficients of $\theta(t)$, with increasingly complicated expressions for higher harmonics. It is therefore reasonable to conclude that an exact closed-form expression allowing direct computation of the Fourier coefficients does not exist to begin with.

Specifically, the Fourier series for a  pendulum with  large amplitudes has also been revisited \cite{Gil2008, Hinrichsen2020}. These studies  meticulously confirms with experimental measurements that the motion indeed consists of odd harmonics. However, the theoretical Fourier coefficients are obtained from a perturbation method  where $\sin \theta$ in Eq.~(\ref{eq_pendulum_eq}) is replaced by a power series \cite{Fulcher1976, Zilio1982}. Harmonics then appear in the solution for each order of the perturbation. Collecting and regrouping the harmonics result in the Fourier coefficients being a power series in the amplitude, $\theta_0$. The series is truncated  because the equation for each order of the perturbation is increasingly more complex. Therefore, the Fourier coefficients are practically an approximation.

Surprisingly, despite its long history since Galileo's work in the 16th century \cite{Matthews2004} and being fundamentally periodic, an exact solution for a pendulum in the frequency domain remains evidently unknown.

The nonlinear pendulum is an active area of research \cite{Moatimid2022,Hinrichsen2023a,Hinrichsen2023b,Zhang2024,Kontomaris2024} with  modern applications in science and engineering  such as alternative energy source and artificial intelligence \cite{Marszal2017, Fu2023,Feng2024,Krishnapriyan2023}. Specifically, a pendulum structure embedded in a wearable device can harvest energy from human movement \cite{Fu2023}. A marine monitoring device can harvest energy from ocean waves using pendulum and piezoelectric coupling \cite{Feng2024}. A machine learning model can recognize continuous dynamics using an analytical solution of nonlinear pendulum as one of its training sets \cite{Krishnapriyan2023}. As such, a simple and exact solution for nonlinear pendulum motion may further accelerate developments in diverse areas of research.

For the class of motion in which the pendulum swings with a finite amplitude, the period is proportional to the complete elliptic integral of the first kind, $K(k)$ \cite{Marion1995}. Because there is no known closed-form expression for $K(k)$, the period must be computed numerically \cite{Abramowitz1965}. Numerous attempts have been made to derive simple formulae that describe the period of the nonlinear pendulum, and a wide variety of analytical approximations have been published \cite{Hinrichsen2021}. For example, frequently cited simple formulae for the period of a pendulum are those by Ganley \cite{Ganley1985} and Lima \cite{Lima2008}. Specifically, the Lima formula was the first attempt to address the full range of amplitudes. In contrast to the period, obtaining a complete expression for the pendulum trajectory, $\theta(t)$, is mathematically more challenging and is often omitted from intermediate classical mechanics textbooks \cite{Marion1995,Kibble2004,Taylor2004}.

The lack of an exact closed-form expression for the Fourier coefficients suggests that frequency-domain analysis of the motion has remained a long-standing problem. Simple and exact nonlinear solutions for all three classes of motion are still lacking; thus, the purpose of this research is to address this gap in the literature.

\section{Results}

The nonlinear motions of an energy-conserving pendulum can be divided into three classes: Swinging, Stopping, and Spinning. These names are coined in this work to enhance intuitive interpretation. In addition to the familiar case in which the pendulum is initially at rest, it may also possess an initial angular velocity, $\omega_0$. Consider the case where the pendulum starts at the bottom, $\theta_0 = 0$, with only $\omega_0$ to initiate the motion. Due to conservation of energy, the angular speed at the bottom is always maximal; thus, $\omega_m$ will be used to denote the angular speed at the bottom.

If $\omega_m$ that initiates the motion is too low, the pendulum will not reach the top and will inevitably fall back down. This is referred to as \enquote{Swinging} motion. If $\omega_m$ is just sufficient for the pendulum to reach the top, it will approach the top but never quite get there. This is because conservation of energy dictates that the closer the pendulum gets to the top, the slower it moves. In practice, the pendulum comes to a halt as it approaches the top. This is referred to as \enquote{Stopping} motion. Finally, if $\omega_m$ is too large, the pendulum will continue to rotate in one direction. This is referred to as \enquote{Spinning} motion.

There exists a critical value of angular speed, $\omega_c$, which separates the motion into three distinct classes. Using conservation of energy, it is straightforward to show that

\begin{equation}
\omega_c = \sqrt{4 g / L}
\end{equation}

