\subsection{Complete Pendulum Trajectory}

While the exact solution for the angular displacement, $\theta(t)$, is typically written in terms of Jacobi elliptic functions, its exact frequency content is not accessible in this form. We present the exact solution not only as a much simpler series involving only elementary functions such as linear, sine, and hyperbolic functions, but also as an exact and direct representation of the frequency content of the energy-conserving nonlinear pendulum. The exact expressions are as follow.

\begin{align}
	\text{Swinging: } \omega_m < \omega_c && \; \theta(t) & = \sum_{n \text{ odd}}  a_n \sin( n \Omega_0 t   + n \delta) \label{eq_swinging} \\
	\text{Stopping: }  \omega_m = \omega_c && \; \theta(t) &   =  2\arcsin \! \big[  \tanh [  \sqrt{\tfrac{g}{L}} (t + \delta) \, ] \, \big]        \label{eq_stopping} \\
	&& \; \theta(t) & = \int_0^\infty \!\!\! d\Omega \, B(\Omega) \sin\big(\Omega (t+\delta)\big) \label{eq_stopping_freq} \\
	\text{Spinning: }  \omega_m > \omega_c && \; \theta(t) & = \Omega_0 t + \delta +   \sum_{n \ge 1} b_n \sin(n \Omega_0 t + n \delta) \label{eq_spinning} 
\end{align}

For simplicity, a closed-form solution for the stopping motion is shown in Eq.~(\ref{eq_stopping}). For uniformity, however, an integral form can also be written as Eq.~(\ref{eq_stopping_freq}). In this form, all three classes of motion are now on the same footing, using the frequency domain.

The phase constant $\delta$ in the derived exact solutions plays a simple role of shifting the time so that other initial conditions can be satisfied. If  $\delta = 0$, the pendulum starts at the bottom ($\theta_0=0$) with an initial angular velocity $\omega_0 = \omega_m$. Changing the initial angle $\theta_0$ is amount to shifting the phase constant $\delta$.

The coefficients $\{a_n, b_n, B\}$, fundamental frequency $\Omega_0 \equiv 2\pi/T$, and  period $T$ are explicit functions of  angular speed at the bottom, $\omega_m$. Let us define $k = \omega_m/\omega_c$,

\begin{align}
	 \text{Swinging: }   & a_n   =     \frac{4} {n\, \cosh(\kappa n \pi /2) }, && \kappa = \frac{K(\sqrt{1-k^2})}{K(k)}  && T = \sqrt{\frac{L}{g}} 4 K(k) \label{eq_swinging_coef}\\
	 \text{Stopping: }  & B  = \frac{2}{\Omega \, \cosh(\kappa \Omega \pi /2 )}, &&  \kappa = \sqrt{\frac{L}{g}} && T \rightarrow \infty \label{eq_stopping_coef}\\
	 \text{Spinning: }   & b_n  = \frac{2}{n\,\cosh(\kappa n \pi)}, && \kappa = \frac{K(\sqrt{ 1-1/k^2 })}{K(1/k)} && T = \sqrt{\frac{L}{g}}\frac{2}{k} K(1/k) \label{eq_spinning_coef}
\end{align}


Fig.\ref{fig_3S} shows examples of $\theta(t)$ from this work (solid lines) using $\delta = 0$. The $\omega_m$ used for the plots are  $(\omega_c - 0.0001,\, \omega_c,\, \omega_c + 0.0001)$ respectively. As clearly seen, the pendulum starts at the bottom and continues to rise up, but eventually branches off into three distinctive patterns.

For other initial conditions $(\theta_0, \omega_0)$, $\omega_m$ can be determined using conservation of energy: 

\begin{equation}
	\omega_m = \sqrt{  \omega_0^2 - \frac{2g}{L}\cos(\theta_0) + \frac{2g}{L}  }
\end{equation}


Generally, it is not easy to formulate a simple solution that covers all quadrants of initial condtions $(\pm\theta_0, \pm \omega_0)$. For example, a sign function denoted by $\text{sgn}(x)$ has to be used in a traditional nonlinear pendulum solution \cite{Ochs2011}.

In this work, the forms of the derived exact solutions are preserved by the use of the phase constant $\delta$. In other words, $\delta$ can be chosen to reflect both plus and minus  sign of initial angle $\theta_0$.

For plus and minus sign of $\omega_0$, the forms are  also preserved by the use of mirror-like symmetry of pendulum motion. 

\begin{equation}
 \theta(t;\; \pm\theta_0, -\omega_0) = -\theta(t;\, \mp\theta_0, +\omega_0)
 \label{eq_mirror_symmetry}
\end{equation}

That is, if an initial condition $\omega_0 < 0$ is encountered, the solutions for positive $\omega_0$ with sign-reversed $\theta_0$ is used instead. Once, the solution $\theta(t)$ is calculated, an additional minus signed is added at the end. In short, for an initial condition $\omega_0 < 0$, the right-hand-side of Eq.~({\ref{eq_mirror_symmetry}}) is used instead.

Therefore, the motion for all possible initial conditions are explained by the set of 3+1 solutions given in the Eq.~(\ref{eq_swinging})-(\ref{eq_spinning}) in this work. Its unique simplicity serves as  a fundamental  basis  in which tractable theoretical frameworks can be built  upon. For example, angular velocity and acceleration can be readily evaluated. Adaptation to a physical pendulum can be done easily. Torque, angular momentum, and other physical properties can be computed and utilized in many areas of research.




\subsection{Phase Constant $\delta$}

Once the initial condition $(\theta_0, \omega_0)$ are given, the phase constant $\delta$ can be calculated. Then, the general angular displacement $\theta(t)$ in this work will be completely defined.

For the swinging motion, i) $\delta = 0$ when the pendulum starts at the bottom with an angular velocity to initiate the motion, \,  ii) $\delta = +\frac{\pi}{2}$ when the pendulum is initially at rest with $\theta_0>0$, \, and iii) $\delta = -\frac{\pi}{2}$ when the pendulum is initially at rest with $\theta_0 < 0$. For other initial conditions $(\theta_0, \omega_0)$,  consider when $t=0$, Eq.(\ref{eq_swinging}) becomes

\begin{equation}
	\theta_0 -   \sum_{n \, \text{odd}} a_n \sin ( n \delta) = 0
	\label{eq_phase_swinging}
\end{equation}

The above condition  can be used to numerically determine $\delta$ using a simple Newton root finding method. The phase constant for the stopping and spinning motions can be determined in a similar fashion. Namely, for the stopping motion

\begin{equation}
	\theta_0 - 2\arcsin \! \big[\tanh[\sqrt{\tfrac{g}{L}}\delta] \big] = 0 \quad \rightarrow \quad \delta = \frac{1}{2} \sqrt{\tfrac{L}{g}} \ln\Big(\frac{1+\sin \frac{\theta_0}{2}}{1-\sin\frac{\theta_0}{2}} \Big)
	\label{eq_phase_stopping}
\end{equation}

and for the spinning motion

\begin{equation}
	\theta_0 - \delta -  \sum_{n \ge 1} b_n \sin(n\delta) = 0
	\label{eq_phase_spinning}
\end{equation}

\begin{figure}[H]
	\begin{center}
	\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_phase_shift.pdf}
	\caption{\label{fig_phaseshift} Example of motions with illustrative initial conditions}
	\end{center}
\end{figure}


Fig. \ref{fig_phaseshift}a) shows examples of motions with three set of initial conditions, all of which  starts on the left side of the peak, with $\theta_0 \approx -\pi$, or more specifically, $-179^\circ$ degrees angle. With an initial angular speed $\omega_0 > 0$, the pendulum starts to move counter-clockwise and momentarily reaches the bottom. At the bottom, the pendulum has maximum angular speed $\omega_m$. For the three cases, the $\omega_0$ are chosen such that $\omega_m$ deviate from the critical value by $(-0.0001,\,0,\,+0.0001)$ respectively. As a result, three classes of motion are seen.

The role of the phase constant $\delta$ can clearly be seen when comparing Fig.~\ref{fig_3S} and Fig.~\ref{fig_phaseshift}a). The stopping motions from the two graphs, for example, share the same values of $\omega_m$. The only difference is the phase constant $\delta$. The $\tanh$-like feature in Fig.~\ref{fig_3S} simply shifts along the time axis, and becomes the graph in Fig.~\ref{fig_phaseshift}a).

Fig. \ref{fig_phaseshift}b) and c) shows the mirror-like symmetry as previously mentioned in Eq.~(\ref{eq_mirror_symmetry}). b) is the case where $\theta_0 = +\pi/2$, and $\omega_0 > 0$. The motion starts on the right side and begins to move counter-clockwise. On the other hand, c)  starts from the left side, $\theta_0 = -\pi/2$, with the same magnitude of $\omega_0$ but minus sign. In other words, c) is the mirror image of b). The mirror-like nature continues  throughout the motions.


\section{Discussions}

A literature search dating back to the 1970s \cite{Fulcher1976} and extending to recent studies in the 2020s \cite{Hinrichsen2020} indicates that the body of knowledge is limited to approximate Fourier series for swinging motions. Therefore, it is evident that the derived solutions in this work constitute the first exact and complete description of all three classes of motion in the frequency domain reported in the literature. These solutions represent a significant advancement in the understanding of the pendulum, a crucial prototype model in the field of nonlinear dynamics.

The derived exact solutions provide both qualitative and quantitative insights into the nonlinear behavior of the pendulum. Qualitatively, the nonlinear motions can be understood through a Fourier-like series, thereby treating all classes of motion on the same theoretical footing. A Fourier series is expected for the swinging motion because $\theta(t)$ is strictly periodic. For the spinning motion, however, $\theta(t)$ is unbounded. It comes as a surprise that the solution can be separated into two parts: (i) the simple non-periodic function $\Omega_0 t$, and (ii) a Fourier series containing an infinite number of harmonics, whose fundamental frequency is $\Omega_0 = 2\pi/T$.

Quantitatively, the exact numerical values obtained using solutions in the form of Jacobi elliptic functions \cite{SciPy2020} are also plotted in Fig.~\ref{fig_3S}. The agreement is found to be within 14 significant digits, a typical limit of computer precision. Therefore, the solutions in this work are also exact.

It is instructive to analyze the harmonic frequencies of an energy-conserving nonlinear pendulum motion and to examine whether Eq.~(\ref{eq_swinging_coef}) and Eq.~(\ref{eq_spinning_coef}) can be validated, in addition to the given mathematical proofs, using numerical simulations of the dynamical system.

\begin{figure}[p]
	\begin{center}
		\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_frequencies.pdf}
		\caption{\label{fig_frequencies} Frequencies analysis of swinging and spinning motions}
	\end{center}
\end{figure}

Fig.~\ref{fig_frequencies} shows frequencies analysis of swinging and spinning motion from a dynamical simulation using velocity verlet integrator. The initial angle is $\theta_0 = 0$, and initial angular velocity $\omega_0 = \omega_c  \pm 0.0001\,\text{rad/sec}$, resulting in the two classes of motion. $2\pi\sqrt{L/g} = 1\,\text{second}$; and $m = 1\,\text{kilogram}$. The simulation uses $dt=0.0001\,\text{ second}$ and runs for $200\text{ seconds}$. Throughout the simulation, the total energy is found to be very stable, with only $10^{-6}\,\text{Joule}$ fluctuation. Fourier transforms are done on the simulated $\theta(t)$ and $\omega(t)$. The frequencies shown on the horizontal axis are normalized by the linear (natural) angular frequency of a pendulum, $\Omega_L = \sqrt{g/L}$.

For the swinging motion in Fig.~\ref{fig_frequencies}a), the peaks are in agreement with the theoretical values from Eq.~(\ref{eq_swinging_coef}). The frequency spectrum of $\omega(t)$, however, resolves more clearly than that of $\theta(t)$, especially in the higher frequency range.

For the spinning motion in Fig.~\ref{fig_frequencies}b), frequency spectrum of $\theta(t)$ does not resolve at all. As explained earlier, because $\theta(t)$ is unbound, its frequency domain has no meaningful interpretation. On the other hand, the frequency spectrum of $\omega(t)$ shows clear peaks, in agreement with the theoretical values from Eq.~(\ref{eq_spinning_coef}).

When studying nonlinear motions in general, one can learn from the example of nonlinear pendulum motion shown in Fig.~\ref{fig_frequencies}. If a study involves driving a system with a specific frequency to observe its response, the frequency domain of the motion trajectory must be analyzed. However, even in the simple energy-conserving case presented in this report, the frequency domain of $\theta(t)$ is  drowned by its linear term, $\Omega_0 t$, in the spinning motion. Therefore, one should also consider the frequency domain of the velocity to fully understand the system's frequency response behavior.

The only special function needed to evaluate the derived exact solutions is $K(k)$, as it appears in Eq.~(\ref{eq_swinging_coef}) and Eq.~(\ref{eq_spinning_coef}). The exact numerical value from a Python package, SciPy \cite{SciPy2020}, is used in this report. Alternatively, in a classroom environment where advanced mathematical topics are not yet accessible to students, a simple analytical approximation for $K(k)$ is recommended \cite{Chachiyo2025saa}. Composed entirely of elementary functions, the closed-form expressions should facilitate discussions of nonlinear pendulum motion in an introductory physics classroom.


\section{Theoretical Method}

\subsection{Swinging Solution}

A brief derivation of Eq.~(\ref{eq_swinging}) is outlined here. Traditionally, pendulum motion is formulated in such a way that the pendulum starts at rest at an amplitude $\theta_0$, after which it undergoes a swinging motion. In this perspective, a stopping motion can be somewhat realized if the pendulum starts exactly at the top, in which case it remains at rest thereafter. A spinning motion cannot be realized unless sufficient initial velocity is provided. Transitions between the three classes of motion depend on $\theta_0$ and $\omega_0$, and are mathematically entangled. However, the three classes of motion can be treated within the same framework if one considers the motion from a different perspective. That is, considering the pendulum starting at the bottom with an initial angular velocity $\omega_m$. This perspective naturally allows the use of $\omega_m$ as a characteristic parameter, enabling continuous transitions between all classes of pendulum motion.

In addition, as previously noted, the challenge in finding the exact Fourier coefficients arises from the fact that the Jacobi elliptic sine function is embedded within the $\arcsin$ function. However, the $\arcsin[\cdots]$ dependence of $\theta(t)$ can be avoided if one initially focuses on  the  angular velocity $\omega(t)$. Once its frequency domain is obtained, one gains access to the frequency domain solution of $\theta(t)$ via simple integration.

Starting with $\omega(t)$, adapted from a comprehensive and notable study of nonlinear energy-conserving pendulum by Ochs \cite[Eq.27]{Ochs2011},

\begin{equation}
	\omega(t) = \omega_m \, \text{cn}(\sqrt{\frac{g}{L}}t, k), \qquad \{\theta_0, \omega_0\} = \{0, \omega_m\}
\label{eq_swinging_exact_velocity}
\end{equation}

Here, $k = \sin\frac{\theta_0}{2}$, but with conservation of energy also $k = \omega_m/\omega_c$. $\text{cn}(z, k)$ is Jacobi elliptic cosine function, whose Fourier series is also known \cite[{Eq.22.11.2}]{NIST:DLMF}.

\begin{equation}
\text{cn}(z,k) = \frac{2\pi}{K k }\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{ q^{n+\frac{1}{2}}\cos ((2n+1)\zeta)}{1+q^{2n+1}}  \label{DLMF_sd_fourier}
\end{equation}

The auxiliary variables defined in the reference are: $k' = \sqrt{1-k^2}, q = e^{-\pi K(k')/K(k)}, \zeta = \frac{\pi z}{2K(k)}$. Before going any further, it is convenient to adjust the form of the summation index $n$. Note that $(2n+1)$ is always an odd integer, so we define an odd integer $m = (2n+1)$. The above Fourier expansion becomes
\begin{equation}
\text{cn}(z,k) = \frac{2\pi}{K k }\sum_{m\,\text{odd}} \frac{ q^{\frac{m}{2}}\cos (\frac{m\pi}{2K} z )}{1+q^m} = \frac{2\pi}{K k}\sum_{m\,\text{odd}} \frac{ \cos (\frac{m\pi}{2K} z) }{2\cosh(\kappa m \pi/2)} 
\end{equation}

Here,  $\kappa \equiv \frac{K(\sqrt{1-k^2})}{K(k)}$. Substituting the Fourier series using $z = \sqrt{\frac{g}{L}}t$ into Eq.(\ref{eq_swinging_exact_velocity}) and renaming the index $m$ back to $n$, the angular velocity becomes

\begin{equation}
\omega(t) = \omega_m \frac{2\pi}{K k} \sum_{n\,\text{odd}} \frac{ \cos (\frac{n \pi}{2K} \sqrt{\frac{g}{L}} t) }{2  \cosh(\kappa n \pi/2)} 
\end{equation}

Using $T = 4 K \sqrt{L/g}$, and integrating with respect to time; one arrives at the Eq.(\ref{eq_swinging}) in this work.

\subsection{Stopping Solution}

For $\omega_m = \omega_c$, it follows that $k = 1$. $\theta(t)$ for a pendulum with sufficient energy to flip at the top can be used as a starting point \cite{Ochs2011}. Then, the limit $k \rightarrow 1$ can lead to a simple solution. When $k = 1$, the solution is

\begin{equation}
	\theta(t) = 2 \arcsin\left[  \text{sn}(\sqrt{\frac{g}{L}} t  ,\, 1) \right]
	\label{eq_stopping_exact_displacement}
\end{equation}

Using a known limit of Jacobi elliptic sine function $\text{sn}(z,1) = \tanh(z)$\cite{Ochs2011}, the Eq.~(\ref{eq_stopping}) readily follows.

The integral form of the stopping solution in Eq.~(\ref{eq_stopping_freq})  can be derived from the spinning solution with the phase $\delta = 0$ and in the limit where $k \rightarrow 1$. In this limit, the period $T \rightarrow \infty$; and fundamental frequency $\Omega_0 = 2\pi/T \rightarrow 0$, the linear term $\Omega_0 t$ can be ignored. Inside the summation, the spacing between $n\Omega_0$ is so small, we can identify $\Omega = n\Omega_0$ as a continuous variable, which is a typical argument for turning a Fourier series into a Fourier transform. Therefore,

\begin{equation}
\theta(t) = \frac{1}{2\pi}\int_0^\infty \!\! d\Omega \, T b_n \sin(\Omega t)
\end{equation}

The term  $T b_n / (2 \pi)$ can be written as a function of $\Omega$, as denoted by $B(\Omega)$ in Eq.~(\ref{eq_stopping_coef}). The phase constant $\delta$ can be added at the end to shift the time axis, yielding the integral form of  the stopping solution in Eq.~(\ref{eq_stopping_freq}).

\subsection{Spinning Solution}

For $\omega_m > \omega_c$, it is apparent that $\theta(t)$ can not be a Fourier series because the solution is unbounded. But from angular velocity $\omega(t)$ perspective, conservation of energy dictates that when a pendulum makes a complete revolution, the angular velocity must come to the same value. In other words, Fourier series of $\omega(t)$ is possible, from which $\theta(t)$ can be subsequently integrated.

The period $T$ and  $\omega(t)$ when a pendulum has sufficient energy to flip at the top are known \cite{Ochs2011}.

\begin{equation}
T = \sqrt{ \frac{L}{g} }\frac{2}{k}K(1/k),\quad \omega(t) = \omega_m \text{dn}(k\sqrt{\frac{g}{L}}t,\, 1/k)
\end{equation}

Fourier series of the Jacobi elliptic function $\text{dn}(z, 1/k)$ is \cite[{Eq.22.11.3}]{NIST:DLMF} available. It involves cosine functions and a constant. Upon integration, this particular constant then gives rise to the linear term $\Omega_0 t$ in Eq.~(\ref{eq_spinning}).

\section{Conclusion}

In this work, we have presented the first complete, exact, closed-form frequency-domain solutions for the nonlinear pendulum applicable to all energy-conserving initial conditions. By deriving explicit expressions for swinging, stopping, and spinning motion, this work addresses a longstanding analytical gap in the study of this canonical nonlinear system.

The solutions, expressed in terms of elementary functions and formulated through an angular velocity frequency analysis, provide a unified and tractable analytical framework for describing the nonlinear pendulum's dynamics across all motion regimes. Their simplicity and mathematical exactness overcome the limitations of previous frequency-domain approaches, which have been restricted to perturbative, approximate, or numerically intensive methods.

Beyond resolving a classical problem, these results establish a foundation for further theoretical exploration in nonlinear dynamics. The exact frequency-domain perspective introduced here enables both improved analytical understanding and practical applications where precise characterization of nonlinear oscillators is critical, including areas such as energy harvesting, vibration control, and nonlinear system modeling.

We believe these solutions contribute a significant theoretical advancement to the study of nonlinear oscillatory systems and provide a framework for future analytical and applied research in the broader field of nonlinear science.


