\subsubsection{Stanford WILDS/Wild-Time Benchmarks}
The Stanford WILDS benchmark~\cite{koh2021wilds}\footnote{\url{https://wilds.stanford.edu/}} evaluates model robustness under real-world distribution shifts across domains like healthcare and ecology. %It covers domain generalization and subpopulation shifts, providing standardized datasets, models, and evaluation protocols. 
Results show significant performance drops from in-distribution to out-of-distribution settings, emphasizing the challenge of robust deployment.
%
Extending this, Wild-Time~\cite{yao2022wild}\footnote{\url{https://wild-time.github.io}} focuses on temporal shifts using five real-world datasets, including MIMIC-IV for clinical prediction tasks. %It introduces Eval-Fix and Eval-Stream strategies and offers a Python package for reproducible evaluation. 
Wild-Time reports ~20\% performance drops over time, highlighting the difficulty of achieving temporal robustness.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsubsection{TableShift Benchmark}
TableShift~\cite{gardner2024benchmarking}\footnote{\url{https://tableshift.org}} is a benchmark designed to evaluate distribution shift robustness of machine learning on tabular data, an area that, despite its widespread use, has lacked standardized benchmarks compared to text and vision.
%
This is especially important in healthcare, where structured EHR data is common. TableShift includes clinical tasks such as ICU mortality, length of stay, and sepsis prediction.

It supports a range of tabular models, including state-of-the-art architectures like FT-Transformer~\cite{gorishniy2021revisiting} and Tabular ResNet~\cite{borisov2022deep}, and provides Python APIs for streamlined data processing and evaluation.
