\section{Challenges and Future Directions}\label{Discussion}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{Major Challenges}
\subsubsection{Missing or Delayed Ground-Truth Labels for AI Monitoring}
\iffalse
Ideally, real-time monitoring of AI models enables rapid identification of performance degradation, allowing healthcare staff to take prompt corrective actions. Ground-truth labels represent the most direct and reliable means of evaluating AI model performance. However, obtaining such labels quickly is challenging in practical healthcare settings. Firstly, generating labels usually involves significant time, labor, and financial costs. For instance, annotating radiology images, such as chest X-rays for lung conditions, demands considerable expert effort from clinicians, making the process slow and expensive. Secondly, there is often a substantial delay in obtaining clinical outcomes, which serve as the ultimate ground truth for evaluating AI predictions. Examples include predictive AI models for clinical deterioration or diagnostic models where endpoints, such as patient mortality or disease progression, are not immediately observable. Such delays in label availability hinder timely identification of AI performance degradation, complicating real-time monitoring and prompt intervention by healthcare providers. \fi

Ideally, real-time monitoring of AI models allows for the early detection of performance degradation, enabling healthcare staff to respond quickly. 
However, obtaining ground-truth labels, the gold standard for performance evaluation, is often delayed or infeasible in clinical settings. 
Label generation typically requires significant time, labor, and cost, such as clinician annotation of chest X-rays for pulmonary diagnoses. Moreover, definitive clinical outcomes, such as mortality or disease profession, often become available only after significant delays. These limitations impede timely performance assessment and delay corrective interventions, particularly for predictive and diagnostic models targeting long-term outcomes.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsubsection{Human-Centric Requirement Monitoring}
\iffalse
Health AI monitoring is an emergent and rapidly growing research topic, with most existing work primarily focusing on performance metrics such as diagnosis accuracy, F1-score, and AUC. However, recent advances in AI research emphasize the increasing importance of human-centric requirements~\cite{naveed2024towards}, including privacy protection, bias and fairness, and alignment with human values.
%
This shift highlights that solely focusing on performance metrics is insufficient for health AI systems. For instance, a system may maintain stable overall diagnostic accuracy while exhibiting worsening performance for an underrepresented group, such as certain ethnic minorities. Such issues must also be identified, flagged, and addressed to ensure equitable and trustworthy AI systems.

Therefore, in addition to designing performance monitors, future efforts should prioritize complementary monitors such as Bias Detectors, Privacy Detectors, and other tools that assess human-centric factors. These additions are essential for building comprehensive monitoring frameworks that uphold both technical reliability and societal accountability in health AI systems. \fi

Health AI monitoring is a rapidly evolving field, traditionally focused on performance metrics like diagnostic accuracy, F1-score, and AUC. However, recent research underscores the need to address human-centric concerns such as privacy, fairness, and alignment with human values~\cite{naveed2024towards}. Performance metrics alone are insufficient. For example, a system may show stable accuracy overall while underperforming for certain ethnic groups. To ensure equitable and trustworthy AI, monitoring must also detect and address such disparities. Future efforts should incorporate tools like bias and privacy detectors to complement performance monitors and support both technical reliability and societal accountability.
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsubsection{Stability-Plasticity Dilemma}
\iffalse
One of the fundamental challenges in updating AI models after detecting performance degradation is the stability-plasticity dilemma~\cite{mermillod2013stability}, \ie, the trade-off between retaining previously learned knowledge (stability) and adapting to new information (plasticity). In healthcare AI, where models are deployed in dynamic clinical environments, addressing this dilemma is critical to ensuring long-term reliability.

\textbf{Stability}: AI models must maintain previously learned decision boundaries to ensure consistency in clinical decision support. Frequent or aggressive updates can lead to catastrophic forgetting~\cite{kirkpatrick2017overcoming,kemker2018measuring}, where previously reliable predictions deteriorate due to excessive adaptation to new data.

\textbf{Plasticity}: AI systems must remain flexible to incorporate emerging medical knowledge, changes in disease prevalence, and updates in clinical guidelines. Without sufficient adaptability, models risk becoming obsolete and misaligned with current patient populations.

Balancing stability and plasticity remains a significant challenge in real-world practice, with no clear guidelines on optimizing this trade-off. Over-reliance on retraining can introduce instability, while insufficient updates may cause performance decay. Developing effective adaptive strategies is essential to ensuring AI models remain robust and clinically relevant over time. \fi

A key challenge in updating AI models after performance degradation is the stability-plasticity dilemma~\cite{mermillod2013stability}, the trade-off between preserving prior knowledge (stability) and adapting to new information (plasticity). 
%
\textbf{Stability}: Models must retain learned decision boundaries to maintain consistent clinical support. Frequent updates risk catastrophic forgetting~\cite{kirkpatrick2017overcoming,kemker2018measuring}, compromising previously reliable predictions.
%
\textbf{Plasticity}: Models must adapt to new medical knowledge, shifts in disease prevalence, and evolving clinical guidelines. Without adaptability, they become outdated and misaligned with current patient needs.
Balancing these forces remains difficult in practice. Excessive retraining can destabilize models, while infrequent updates lead to performance decay. Robust adaptive strategies are essential to keep AI models both reliable and relevant.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsubsection{System Complexity and Maintenance Challenges}

Real-world AI systems are increasingly complex, comprising feedback loops, dynamic data pipelines, and interdependent modules. Unlike rule-based software, their behavior is data-driven, making them sensitive to input shifts and operational changes.
%
Such complexity increases the risk of silent degradation. Feedback loops may reinforce bias, and changes in one component can unintentionally destabilize others, accumulating what is often referred to as technical debt~\cite{2015Hidden}.
%
These challenges are magnified in large models like LLMs and VLMs, whose black-box architectures and emergent behaviors (unintended capabilities arising post-training) make them difficult to interpret or monitor~\cite{yampolskiy2024monitorability}.

Manual oversight is no longer sufficient. Ensuring long-term safety and reliability demands automated, adaptive monitoring systems that can detect hidden failures, track behavioral shifts, and improve model transparency.
