\subsection{Future Directions}
\subsubsection{Label-Free Estimation}
\if false
In healthcare AI, ground-truth labels are typically delayed, costly, or impractical to obtain in clinical settings. As a result, traditional retrospective validation is inadequate for timely model assessment in dynamic environments. To address this, label-free performance estimation has emerged as a promising direction, enabling AI systems to self-assess without requiring labeled target data.

One potential solution is the use of surrogate/pseudo labels, where predictions from a reference model or the model itself, serving as stand-ins for ground truth. While this can provide rough performance estimates, it may propagate bias if surrogate/pseudo labels are inaccurate or unstable.

Another widely adopted technique leverages the model’s output confidence, such as softmax probabilities, to estimate performance. For instance, the Average Thresholded Confidence (ATC) method~\cite{gargleveraging} learns a confidence threshold from labeled source data and applies it to target data to predict model accuracy. This approach is particularly useful when labeled data is unavailable but model confidence remains informative.

