\subsubsection{Benign vs. Harmful Shift}
\iffalse
Not all distribution shifts lead to significant performance degradation, making it important to distinguish between benign and harmful shifts. A benign shift refers to changes in the data distribution that do not substantially impact a model’s performance, whereas a harmful shift results in notable performance degradation that requires intervention. In practice, benign shifts can often be ignored, as they do not warrant retraining or human intervention. Raising unnecessary alarms in such cases can lead to operational inefficiencies, increased deployment costs, and potential system deactivation~\cite{finlayson2021clinician}.

Differentiating between benign and harmful shifts can enhance the robustness of AI systems by reducing false alarms and focusing on meaningful interventions. However, most existing research focuses on detecting shifts and triggering alarms without accounting for the impact on model performance. Some recent studies have started addressing this distinction by developing methods to track and categorize shift severity~\cite{podkopaev2022tracking}, providing a more robust approach to managing AI systems in dynamic environments. \fi

Not all distribution shifts cause significant performance degradation, so distinguishing between benign and harmful shifts is essential. Benign shifts have minimal impact and usually require no action, while harmful shifts degrade performance and demand intervention. Misidentifying benign shifts can lead to false alarms, inefficiencies, higher costs, and even system deactivation~\cite{finlayson2021clinician}.

Differentiating shift types improves AI system robustness by reducing false positives and focusing resources on meaningful interventions. However, most research emphasizes shift detection without assessing performance impact. Recent studies~\cite{podkopaev2022tracking} have begun addressing this by categorizing shift severity, offering a more practical approach for managing AI in dynamic environments.
