\subsection{Computational Efficiency}
The optimization process successfully balanced performance and computational cost. Figure \ref{fig:time_vs_dcs} plots the final $DCS$ achieved against the total training time for each of the 100 trials. No significant correlation was found between longer training times and better final $DCS$ ($r = -0.01$). The best model, BO-QGAN, trained efficiently ($<$17 minutes on test hardware, 2140 iterations). Training time in simulation was primarily driven by quantum circuit width (qubits), consistent with the exponential scaling of simulation cost with \(N\). The finding that shallower, narrower circuits perform best aligns well with computational tractability.

