To extract the microscopic activation energy, we analyse the temperature dependence of the rearrangement probability for fixed SOP values. Specifically, we fit $P_{R}(\beta\phi)$ as a function of inverse temperature $1/T$ for several values of $(\beta\phi)$ using the Arrhenius form:
\begin{equation}
  P_{R}(\beta\phi) = P_0(\beta\phi) \exp\left[-\frac{\Delta E(\beta\phi)}{T}\right],
\label{prs}
\end{equation}
where $\Delta E(\beta\phi)$ is interpreted as the microscopic activation energy required for rearrangement and depends on the local structure via $(\beta\phi)$. This is shown in Fig.\ref{Fig_calculation}(b), which demonstrates the increasing structural control over dynamics at lower temperatures.
This method thus establishes a direct quantitative link between local structural features and the energy barrier for particle rearrangements.\\
