\title{Chirality-dependent terahertz topological resonance and inertial dynamics of magnetic skyrmions}

\author{X. D. Wang}
\affiliation{School of Materials and Physics, China University of Mining and Technology, Xuzhou 221116, P. R. China}

\author{Y. F. Duan}
\email{yifeng@cumt.edu.cn}
\affiliation{School of Materials and Physics, China University of Mining and Technology, Xuzhou 221116, P. R. China}

\author{H. M. Dong}
\email{hmdong@cumt.edu.cn}
\affiliation{School of Materials and Physics, China University of Mining and Technology, Xuzhou 221116, P. R. China}

\author{Kai Chang}
\email{kchang@zju.edu.cn}
\affiliation{Center for Quantum Matter, School of Physics, Zhejiang University, Hangzhou 310027, P. R. China}

\date{\today}

\begin{abstract}
We present a theoretical investigation of the terahertz (THz) optical response of magnetic skyrmions governed by an inertial-modified Thiele equation. By incorporating the inertial mass term, we derive analytical expressions for the THz absorption spectrum of skyrmions, revealing a topological resonance phenomenon tunable via the skyrmion's topological charge $Q$, damping factor $\alpha$, and effective mass $m$. The resonance frequency $\omega_0 \propto Q/m$ emerges from the interplay between gyroscopic coupling and inertial mass, enabling precise control over skyrmion dynamics through circularly polarized THz fields. Our results demonstrate that the helical motion trajectories and velocity components of skyrmions depend on the chirality of light and the topological charge $Q$, with critical damping $\alpha_c$ marking the transition to overdamped regimes. Our findings establish an optical method for designing tunable THz devices, such as topological filters and detectors, by exploiting the interplay between magnetic skyrmion topology and the chirality of THz light coupling.  
\end{abstract}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Magnetic skyrmions are topologically protected spin textures that are robust against external disturbances, such as electric or magnetic fields, and can be efficiently manipulated and controlled. They have attracted significant attention in the scientific community due to their unique dynamic responses to electromagnetic fields \cite{reich2022}. These intriguing spin structures exhibit fascinating properties derived from their inherent topology, making them promising candidates for various technological applications, such as data storage and information processing. It has been demonstrated that skyrmions can be controlled and driven by spin-polarized currents \cite{Iwasaki2013}, electric fields \cite{Ba2021}, spin waves \cite{Rozsa_2020}, thermal gradient fields \cite{Wangth2020}, and so on. Recently, optical methods for manipulating skyrmions have demonstrated significant advantages and potential applications \cite{lepad2023, AlSaidi2024}. Especially, the spin precession modes and the rotation of skyrmions, due to the inverse Faraday effect (IFE), have been found by ultrafast all-optical spin wave spectroscopy experiments \cite{Kato2023}. Optical techniques' main benefits in manipulating magnetic skyrmions include non-contact operation, ultra-fast response, spin selectivity, and control of various dimensions \cite{jun2024, Kazemi2024}. These qualities address the limitations of traditional methods and introduce entirely new physical mechanisms based on the interaction between light and spin \cite{fang2021}. Therefore, the primary focus of this investigation is to elucidate the mechanisms associated with the IEF in magnetic skyrmions.

The process acts directly on magnetic materials through spin-photon coupling driven by intense light fields, generating a torque that drives the movement of skyrmions \cite{VinasBostrom2022, Lixin18}. Femtosecond laser pulses can induce ultrafast demagnetization of magnetic moments through multi-photon absorption, creating or annihilating skyrmions \cite{serban2020}. Additionally, vortex beams, such as Laguerre-Gaussian beams, carry orbital angular momentum that interacts with the topological charge of skyrmions via the Zeeman effect, leading to their helical motion \cite{fu054421, guansh2023}. Furthermore, spin waves excited by light can achieve quantum coherent coupling with skyrmions through magnetic dipole interactions \cite{Chen2021a, leemu2022}. Importantly, utilizing the spin angular momentum carried by circularly polarized light, spin angular momentum transfer can occur through the IFE, allowing for the simultaneous detection and manipulation of skyrmions. Mie resonances can create highly nonuniform effective magnetic fields due to the IFE, which can be utilized for generating magnetic skyrmions \cite{Krichevsky2024}. In particular, recent experimental studies have provided compelling evidence of a direct coupling between light and skyrmions, based on the IFE \cite{stanci2007}. This suggests the possibility of manipulating these chiral magnetic textures using chiral optical stimuli \cite{VinasBostrom2022}. The observed interactions between chiral spin textures and chiral light fields open intriguing opportunities to develop novel optoelectronic devices and explore fundamental light-matter interactions at the nanoscale \cite{Peng2023}.

However, despite these promising findings, a comprehensive quantitative theory that explicitly connects the well-established Thiele dynamics of particle-like skyrmions to their optical absorption characteristics remains elusive. Thiele dynamics describe the motion of skyrmions under external forces and are crucial for understanding the transport and control of these spin structures. Bridging the gap between the improved Thiele equation and the optical response of skyrmions is essential for unlocking their full potential in practical applications. In the meantime, the modulation of the magneto-optical response at terahertz (THz) frequencies has been realized for spins \cite{Subkhangulov2016}. THz emission of ferromagnetic Ni-Fe thin films can be excited by ultrafast laser pulses \cite{Shen_2012}. Tunable THz oscillations are observed based on the nontrivial topological structures in chiral magnets \cite{Phys033006}. Sub-THz spin precession frequencies are observed in van der Waals ferromagnets \cite{Zhang2024b}. Notably, it has experimentally been shown that skyrmions possess inertial mass, particularly at gigahertz and THz bands, similar to classical particles \cite{Buttner2015, Wang3M2022a, Makhfudz2012}. The inertial mass leads to skyrmion motion in circular or spiral trajectories and the breathing mode, describing the oscillatory variation in the skyrmion radius. Moreover, the optical spin-orbit torque induced by a circularly polarized laser can drive free decaying oscillations within 2 THz in metallic antiferromagnetic \cite{Huang2024}. It has been demonstrated that the transport of skyrmions can be steered via THz electric pulses \cite{Wangxi2023}. These studies highlight the significant role of spin and spin topological structures in the THz field. However, the theoretical investigation of the THz responses of skyrmions has yet to be systematically explored. The response to time-dependent magnetic forces and movements depends strongly on a large effective mass (e.g., a typical mass scale of 10$^{-25}$ kg) \cite{schutte2014}. Moreover, the mass of skyrmions can be determined from the frequency dependence of the damping kernel, offering a microscopic view of the kinematic properties of skyrmions \cite{Psaroudaki2016ikn}. By coupling between skyrmion motion and the corresponding excited magnons, a dynamical mass for the skyrmions can be identified directly \cite{Wangma2022b}. However, micromagnetic simulations and experiments fail to effectively incorporate this inertial effect at present \cite{Jiang2017, Makhfudz2012}. Therefore, including an inertial mass term in the Thiele equation is necessary to fully understand the THz optical properties of skyrmions \cite{Yangoe2018}.

An accurate comprehension of the interaction between chiral skyrmions and chiral light is essential for advancing this field. A fundamental issue is how left- and right-handed circularly polarized light couples to chiral skyrmions. Extensive research efforts are currently underway to address this challenge. These efforts combine advanced theoretical frameworks, sophisticated experimental techniques, and computational modeling to elucidate the interplay between topological dynamics and optical properties of magnetic skyrmions \cite{lepad2023, VinasBostrom2022, Kato2023}. Developing a detailed and predictive theory in this area will enhance our fundamental understanding of the fascinating nontrivial spin textures, pave the way for innovative skyrmion-based optoelectronic devices, and explore novel physical phenomena at the intersection of magnetism and photonics.

In this theoretical work, we have built upon the improved Thiele equation to incorporate crucial inertial effects that are highly relevant to the dynamic response of magnetic skyrmions at THz frequencies. Through a systematic and rigorous mathematical derivation, we have obtained explicit analytical expressions that describe the THz absorption spectrum of the topological spin textures. The intricate helical motion of the magnetic skyrmions is governed by two key parameters: the topological charge $Q$, which encodes the skyrmion's topological properties, and the chirality of the incident THz circularly polarized light. Our comprehensive theoretical analysis has revealed the existence of a remarkable topological resonance phenomenon that can be precisely tuned and controlled by choosing the skyrmion's topological charge $Q$, the damping factor $\alpha$, and the effective mass $m$. This discovery highlights the immense potential and versatility of magnetic skyrmions for practical applications in the burgeoning field of THz technology. By harnessing the unique topological features of these quasiparticles, we envision a new generation of high-performance THz devices, such as tunable filters, modulators, and detectors. The ability to engineer the dynamic ultra-fast response of skyrmions at THz frequencies represents a significant advancement, paving the way for integrating topological spin textures.

\section{Theoretical Model}
The right- and left-handed circularly polarized light can interact with spins via the IFE \cite{kiril2010}. The dynamics of a magnetic skyrmion under time-harmonic optical excitation are governed by the modified Thiele equation \cite{Makhfudz2012, Buttner2015}, which is
\begin{equation}
m\dot{\boldsymbol v} + {\boldsymbol G} \times {\boldsymbol v} - \alpha D {\boldsymbol v} = {\boldsymbol F}(t). \label{Thiele}
\end{equation}  
Here $\dot{\boldsymbol{v}}=d\boldsymbol{v}/dt$. $\boldsymbol{v} = d\boldsymbol{R}/dt$ is the velocity for the center of skyrmion' mass $\boldsymbol{R}(t)$ with the time $t$, and $m$ is the effective mass of skyrmions. $\boldsymbol{G} = G_z \hat{z} = 4\pi Q M_s\hat{z}a/\gamma$ is the gyroscopic coupling vector with the saturation magnetization $M_s$ and the gyromagnetic ratio $\gamma=1.76 \times 10^{10}$A$\cdot$s$\cdot$kg$^{-1}$, resulting from the skyrmion's topology \cite{Liu2023}. $a$ is the skyrmion lattice constant. $Q = \frac{1}{4\pi} \iint \boldsymbol{n} \cdot (\partial_x \boldsymbol{n} \times \partial_y \boldsymbol{n}) dxdy$ is the topological charge with ${\boldsymbol n}$ being the normalized magnetization vector. $D=4\pi M_s\eta a/\gamma$ is the dissipation tensor, and $\alpha$ is the Gilbert damping factor \cite{hong094429}. $\eta$ is the shape factor obtained based on the skyrmion profile as follows \cite{Liu2023}. $\boldsymbol{F}(t) = \boldsymbol{F}_0 e^{-i\omega t}$ is the optical force with the intensity $\boldsymbol{F}_0$ and $\chi$ being the magneto-optical susceptibility due to the optomagnetic effect \cite{kiril2010}, which is derived in detail in this work. $I_0$ and $\omega$ are the intensity and frequency of the circularly polarized light. $M_s$ is the saturation magnetization of the material. The inertial term $m\dot{\boldsymbol{v}}$ becomes significant at THz frequencies with $ m\sim 10^{-26}-10^{-23} \, \mathrm{kg}$ \cite{Buttner2015, lin2017}. 

The shape factor of skyrmions can be written as 
\begin{equation}
\eta=\frac{1}{4\pi}\iint(\partial_x\boldsymbol{n}\times\partial_y\boldsymbol{n})dxdy,  \label{eta1}
\end{equation}
which is obtained based on the skyrmion profile \cite{romming2015, Jiang2017}. $\boldsymbol{n}(r)=\boldsymbol{n}(\theta,\phi)=(\sin\theta\cos\phi, \sin\theta\sin\phi, \cos\theta)$ is the normalized magnetization vector in the planar space. We perform integral calculations by incorporating the spin structure of a Néel-type skyrmion. For an axisymmetric Néel-type skyrmion with $\phi=0$, the linear approximation of the domain wall is utilized. In this case, the polar angle $\theta(r)$ varies linearly within the domain wall region, and its derivative remains constant. Skyrmions can be viewed as a type of 360$^{\circ}$ domain wall that is highly localized and features a closed spin texture \cite{romming2015}. The spin structure is represented in cylindrical coordinates $\boldsymbol{r} = (r, \phi, z)$ as
\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{n} = \sin\theta(r) \hat{r} + \cos\theta(r) \hat{z},
\end{equation}
where 
\begin{equation}
\theta(r) = 
\begin{cases} 
\pi, & r - P < -\dfrac{r_{\text{dw}}}{2}, \\
\dfrac{\pi}{2} - \dfrac{r - P}{r_{\text{dw}}}\pi, & -\dfrac{r_{\text{dw}}}{2} \leq r - P \leq \dfrac{r_{\text{dw}}}{2}, \\
0, & r - P > \dfrac{r_{\text{dw}}}{2}.   \label{theta3}
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
within the domain wall region ($r \in [P - r_{\text{dw}}/2, P + r_{\text{dw}}/2]$), $d\theta/dr = -\pi/r_{\text{dw}}$, and $P = d/2$ with $d$ the diameter of the skyrmion \cite{Jiang2017, Liu2023}. $P$ and $r_{\text{dw}}$ denote the position and width of the domain wall.

Due to the axisymmetry of the spin structure, after transforming to the cylindrical coordinate system $(r, \phi)$, $\frac{\partial \boldsymbol{n}}{\partial x}$ and $\frac{\partial \boldsymbol{n}}{\partial y}$ can be decomposed as
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial \boldsymbol{n}}{\partial x} = \frac{\partial \boldsymbol{n}}{\partial r} \cos\phi, \quad \frac{\partial \boldsymbol{n}}{\partial y} = \frac{\partial \boldsymbol{n}}{\partial r} \sin\phi.
\end{equation}
Therefore, the sum of their squares is
\begin{equation}
\left(\frac{\partial \boldsymbol{n}}{\partial x}\right)^2 + \left(\frac{\partial \boldsymbol{n}}{\partial y}\right)^2 = \left(\frac{\partial \boldsymbol{n}}{\partial r}\right)^2.
\end{equation}
Furthermore, we can calculate $\frac{\partial \boldsymbol{n}}{\partial r}$, which is
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial \boldsymbol{n}}{\partial r} = \cos\theta \frac{d\theta}{dr} \hat{r} - \sin\theta \frac{d\theta}{dr} \hat{z}.
\end{equation}

By substituting the above results into equation \ref{eta1}, we have
\begin{equation}
\eta = \frac{1}{4\pi} \iint \left(\frac{d\theta}{dr}\right)^2 \, d^2\boldsymbol {r}.
\end{equation}
In the cylindrical coordinate system, the integration interval is the domain wall region $r \in [d/2 - r_{\text{dw}}/2, d/2 + r_{\text{dw}}/2]$ by equation \ref{theta3}. We have $d\theta/dr = -\pi/r_{\text{dw}}$ and the size $r \approx d/2$ for $r_{\text{dw}} \ll d$. Finally, we obtain
\begin{equation}
\eta = \frac{\pi^2 d}{8r_{\text{dw}}}.
\end{equation}
This result shows that $\eta$ is proportional to the diameter $d$ of the skyrmion and inversely proportional to the domain wall width $r_{\text{dw}}=\sqrt{A/(K_u-\frac{1}{2}\mu_0M_s^2)}$. $\mu_0$ is the magnetic permeability of free space. For example, we choose the typical skyrmion diameter $d=100$ nm, the exchange constant $A=16\times10^{-12}$ J/m, the Dzyaloshinskii-Moriya interaction (DMI) constant $D_0=4\times10^{-3}$ J/m$^2$, the anisotropy constant $K_u=0.51\times10^6$ J/m$^3$, and the saturation magnetization intensity $M_s=5\times10^5$ A/m for a typical skyrmion \cite{wangSize2018, dong2023, finocchio2016}. Thus, we have $r_{\text{dw}}\approx6.73$ nm, and $\eta\approx18.32$.

Due to the IFE, a magnetization can be induced without magnetic fields \cite{VanDerZiel1965}. This phenomenon describes generating a permanent magnetic field in magnetic materials induced by circularly polarized light \cite{pershan1966, stanci2007}. The fundamental equation that governs this effect is
\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{H}_{\text{eff}} = \chi(\boldsymbol{E} \times \boldsymbol{E}^*)
\end{equation}
where $\boldsymbol{H}_{\text{eff}}$ is the light-induced effective magnetic field \cite{kiril2010}. $\boldsymbol{E}$ and $\boldsymbol{E}^*$ are the electric field of the light field and its complex conjugate, respectively. $\chi$ is the magneto-optical susceptibility tensor via the IFE again, describing the material's response to circularly polarized light to induce magnetization. The coefficient $\chi$ is nonzero when time reversal symmetry is broken while retaining lattice symmetry \cite{Holleis2021}. It shows that right- and left-handed circularly polarized waves can act as magnetic fields of opposite signs.

For circularly polarized light with the amplitude $E_0$ and the frequency $\omega$, the electric field can be expressed as:
\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol {E} = E_0 (\hat{x} \pm i\hat{y}) e^{-i\omega t},
\end{equation}
where $\pm$ corresponds to left-handed/right-handed polarization. Taking right-handed circularly polarized light as an example, its electric field components are
\begin{equation}
E_x = E_0 e^{-i\omega t}, \quad E_y = i E_0 e^{-i\omega t}.
\end{equation}
We consider the isotropic media, the non-zero components of $\chi$ can be simplified as
$\chi_{xyz} = \chi_{yxz} = \cdots = \chi$, and the induced magnetization $M_z$ in the skyrmion layer. The direction is along the light propagation direction in the $z$-axis. As a result, we have \cite{kiril2010}
\begin{equation}
H_{\text{eff},z} = \chi E_i\cdot E_j^*.
\end{equation}

The effective magnetic field $H_{\text{eff},z}$ induces variations in the magnetization $\boldsymbol{M}$ \cite{jun2024}. The improved Thiele equation describes the motion equation of the skyrmion, and the optical force ${\boldsymbol F}$ comes from the gradient of the effective field per volume $\boldsymbol{f} = -\left(\nabla  \boldsymbol {M} \right)\cdot \boldsymbol {H}_{\text{eff} }$.
For a uniform circularly polarized light within a skyrmion area $\mathcal{A}$, the gradient mainly comes from the spatial variation of the spin structure of the skyrmion \cite{Jiang2017, Fert2017, Buttner2015}, which is
\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{F} = -t_0 \iint [(\nabla \boldsymbol{M}) \cdot \boldsymbol{H}_{\text{eff}}] \, d\mathcal{A},
\end{equation}
The topological charge of the skyrmion $Q$ can lead to a non-zero optical force $\boldsymbol{F}={\boldsymbol F}_0e^{-i\omega t}$. Magnetic skyrmions are two-dimensional spin structures, and it is necessary to introduce the skyrmion's effective thickness $t_0\sim 1$ nm for calculations. Moreover, considering the geometric factor of skyrmions and the light intensity $I_0$, the amplitude of the driving force of the circularly polarized light is
\begin{equation}
{\boldsymbol F}_0 = \frac{4\pi M_s \chi}{\mu_0 c} Q t_0 I_0(\hat{x}\pm i\hat{y})\propto Q I_0.
\end{equation}
The direction of the optic field force depends on whether the light polarization is left-handed or right-handed due to IFE. $c$ is the speed of light in a vacuum. The magneto-optical susceptibility $\chi$ is directly related to the IFE and describes the magnetic susceptibility of a material's magneto-optical susceptibility response to an optical field. For common magneto-optical materials, such as ferrites and magnetic topological insulators, $\chi$ is about in the range of $10^{-5}-10^{-10}\, \mathrm{m^2/A}$ due to the optomagnetic effect \cite{Holleis2021, Pershan1963, Pershan:1966zz}, which can be measured by using high harmonic detections, the magneto-optical effect, and the Raman resonance enhancement method \cite{cheng2020, Mugiraneza2022, Basini2024}. By measuring the variation of the Faraday rotation angle $\theta_{\text{F}}$ with the optical intensity $I_0$ and combining it with the material parameters, such as the Verdet constant $\mathcal{V}$ and the sample thickness $t_s$, the magneto-optical susceptibility $\chi$ can be experimentally obtained by \cite{Salmon2020, dmitriev2013handbook}
\begin{equation}
\chi = \frac{\theta_{\text{F}} \cdot c^2 \epsilon_0}{2 \mathcal{V} \mu_0 t_s I_0}.
\end{equation}
The light intensity $I_0$ refers to the power density of incident circularly polarized light. 
Moreover, the measurable quantity is a magnetization $\mathcal{M}=\lambda_0\mathcal{V}(2\pi c)^{-1}[I_R-I_L]$ due to the IEF \cite{VanDerZiel1965}. $I_R$ and $I_L$ are the intensities of right and left circularly polarized light with the wavelength $\lambda_0$.  
Typically, for continuous lasers, $I_0$ ranges from $10^3$ to $10^6 \, \mathrm{W/m^2}$, while for ultrafast laser pulses, $I_0$ can be as high as $10^{12} $ to $ 10^{14} \, \mathrm{W/m^2}$ \cite{Liph18, Katsantonis2025, Shao:2024dbo, cheng2020}. Using these typical values, we can estimate that the driving force $F_0$ is $0.001\sim 1 \, \mathrm{pN}$, which depends on the intensity of the light fields.

We consider the optical harmonic driving $\boldsymbol{F}(t) = \boldsymbol{F}_0 e^{-i\omega t}$, and therefore assume ${\boldsymbol v}(t) = {\boldsymbol v}_0 e^{-i\omega t}$ for the solution of the Thiele Eq. \ref{Thiele}. We can have the Thiele equation under the light, which is  
\begin{equation}
-i\omega m {\boldsymbol v}_0 + \boldsymbol{G} \times {\boldsymbol v}_0 - \alpha D {\boldsymbol v}_0  = \boldsymbol{F}_0. 
\label{harmonic}
\end{equation}  
For a magnetic skyrmion, we expand Eq. \ref{harmonic} in Cartesian coordinates with $v_z = 0$, and then  
\begin{equation}
\begin{cases} 
(-i\omega m- \alpha D) v_x + G_z v_y = F_x \\ 
-G_z v_x + (-i\omega m - \alpha D) v_y = F_y. 
\end{cases}
\end{equation}  
Here, the matrix representation for both equations can be expressed as follows,
\begin{equation}
\begin{bmatrix}
-i\omega m - \alpha D & G_z \\
-G_z & -i\omega m - \alpha D
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
v_x \\
v_y
\end{bmatrix}
= 
\begin{bmatrix}
F_x \\
F_y
\end{bmatrix}.
\end{equation}  
The elements along the matrix's main diagonal represent the system's inertial and damping characteristics, while the elements away from the diagonal represent the gyroscopic coupling effects. The determinant of the coefficient matrix is   
\begin{equation}
\Delta = (\alpha D + i\omega m)^2 + G_z^2.
\end{equation}  
The velocity amplitude is obtained via matrix inversion, which reads  
\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{v}_0 = \frac{1}{\Delta}
\begin{bmatrix}
-i\omega m - \alpha D & G_z \\
-G_z & -i\omega m - \alpha D
\end{bmatrix}
\boldsymbol{F}_0.   \label{velo-am}
\end{equation}  
In magnetic systems, the external light field acts as a driving force that interacts with the system's magnetic properties. This interaction can induce oscillations or changes in the material's magnetic moments, leading to energy absorption or emission. 

When the gyroscopic force, dissipative force, and optical field force of the skyrmion are precisely balanced, e. g. $\boldsymbol{G} \times \boldsymbol{v} - \alpha D \boldsymbol{v} = \boldsymbol{F}(t)$, the skyrmion experiences no acceleration. According to the Thiele equation, we can determine the velocity of the skyrmion, 
\begin{equation}
v_{x} = \frac{- \alpha D F_x-G_z F_y}{ (\alpha D)^2 + G_z^2 }, \quad v_{y} = \frac{ G_z F_x - \alpha D F_y }{ (\alpha D)^2 + G_z^2 }, \label{vxvy}
\end{equation}
and similar to the skyrmion Hall angle, the deflection angle of skyrmions can be written as
\begin{equation}
\tan\theta_{\text{IFE}}=\frac{v_{y}}{v_{x}}=\frac{\alpha D-G_z}{\alpha D+G_z} \label{ifet}
\end{equation} 
for circularly polarized light $(F_x=F_y)$. When a spin-polarized current drives the motion of skyrmions, the skyrmion Hall effect (SHE) occurs \cite{Chen2017}. The skyrmion Hall angle $\theta_{\text{SkH}}$ is defined as the angle between the motion direction of the skyrmion and the direction of the current \cite{Brearton2021, Wang2022c, du2022}, which is
\begin{equation}
\tan\theta_{\text{SkH}}=\frac{v_y}{v_x}=\frac{(\alpha-\beta)DG_z}{G_z^2+ \alpha\beta D^2}, \label{halla}
\end{equation}
according to the Thiele equation. When skyrmions are driven by a spin-polarized current with the nonadiabatic coefficient $\beta$, the driving force acts in only one direction (e.g., $F_y = 0$). However, due to the Magnus force, a velocity component is generated in the direction perpendicular to the driving force. The Hall angle depends not only on the topological term $G$ and the dissipation term $D$, but also on the value of $\alpha$ and $\beta$, as illustrated in equation \ref{halla}. When an optical field force is used for driving, the driving force has components in both the $x$ and $y$ directions. The angle between both velocity components is similar to the Hall angle due to the spin-polarized current, as shown in equation \ref{ifet}. The velocity components depend not only on the topological term $G_z$ and the dissipation term $\alpha D$, but also on the driving forces in both directions.

Although the "Hall angle" derived by the optical field force is similar to that by spin-polarized current, the physical interpretations and factors affecting these parameters differ. In the case of the optical field force, $\alpha D$ represents the dissipation effect due to the optical field. On the other hand, in the context of spin-polarized current, $D$ refers to the dissipation effect associated with current flow. While the term $G_z$ is related to topological properties in both scenarios, the specific values and influencing mechanisms can vary significantly. This work primarily focuses on driving skyrmion motion through the interaction of light and spins, for instance, via the IFE. This interaction involves coupling light characteristics, such as polarization and angular momentum, with skyrmions' topological and magnetic properties. Although there are significant differences in aspects like velocity components, Hall angles, and physical mechanisms between driving skyrmion motion with spin-polarized current and optical field force, both methods reflect the nontrivial topological properties of skyrmions under external driving conditions. 

The internal dissipation mechanism within the magnetic system plays a crucial role in the energy consumption. This dissipation mechanism, often referred to as damping or relaxation, arises from various interactions and processes within the material. The energy consumed in the magnetic system is dissipated by heat, which the internal dissipation mechanism generates. The time-averaged dissipation power is derived from Joule heating,   
\begin{equation}
P(\omega) = \frac{1}{T} \int_0^T \alpha D |\boldsymbol{v}(t)|^2 dt = \frac{\alpha D}{2} |\boldsymbol{v}_0|^2,
\end{equation}  
where $|\boldsymbol{v}_0|^2 = \frac{(\alpha D)^2 + (\omega m)^2 + G_z^2}{|\Delta|^2} |\boldsymbol{F}_0|^2 $. 

When light interacts with a magnetic skyrmion, it generates a driving force through spin-photon interactions, which excites the motion of the skyrmion. Due to damping effects, the kinetic energy from this motion is ultimately dissipated as heat or other forms of energy, corresponding to the energy absorbed from the light. This heat generation can be influenced by factors such as the strength and frequency of the external light field. Consequently, we can obtain the optical absorption spectrum by the time-averaged power of the dissipation, which is 
\begin{equation}
P(\omega) = \frac{|\boldsymbol{F}_0|^2}{2} \cdot \frac{\alpha D [(\alpha D)^2 + (\omega m)^2 + G_z^2]}{\left[ (\alpha D)^2 + G_z^2 - (\omega m)^2 \right]^2 + (2\alpha D \omega m)^2}. \label{pomeg}
\end{equation}  

The optical absorption spectrum $P(\omega)$ with equation \ref{pomeg} can be rewritten as
\begin{equation}
P(\omega) = \frac{|\boldsymbol{F}_0|^2}{ D}\times\mathcal{R}(\omega),
\end{equation} 
with the dimensionless response function 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}(\omega)=\frac{1 + \tilde{\omega}_{_D}^2 + \tilde{\omega}_{_G}^2}{2\alpha[( 1+ \tilde{\omega}_{_G}^2 - \tilde{\omega}_{_D}^2 )^2 + 4\tilde{\omega}_{_D}^2]}.
\end{equation} 
Here $\tilde{\omega}_{_D}=\omega m/\alpha D$, and $\tilde{\omega}_{_G}=G_z/\alpha D$.

Moreover, the resonance frequency (absorption peak) $\omega_0$ can be calculated by  
\begin{equation}
\frac{d}{d\omega} \Big\{\left[ (\alpha D)^2 + G_z^2 - (\omega m)^2 \right]^2 + (2\alpha D \omega m)^2 \Big\}= 0.
\end{equation}  
We can obtain the resonance frequency  
\begin{equation}
\omega_0 = \frac{1}{m}\sqrt{G_z^2 - (\alpha D)^2} \quad (\text{for } |G_z| > \alpha D). \label{omeg0}
\end{equation}
Additionally, when the damping is small enough ($\alpha << 1$) \cite{Qinyig2018}, we can obtain the simplified approximate expression for $\omega_0$, which is
\begin{equation}
\omega_0 = \frac{1}{m}\Big(|G_z|-\frac{\alpha^2D}{2|G_z|}\Big).
\end{equation}
This result indicates that two terms dominate the resonance frequency $\omega_0$ driven by the chiral optical field. The principal value of the resonance frequency is expressed as $|G_z|/m$, which is determined by the gyroscopic coupling term $G_z\propto Q$ and the inertial mass $m$. The subsequent term serves as a correction factor attributable to damping effects. This damping influences the resonance frequency by causing a shift in its value, with the magnitude of this shift being proportional to $\alpha^2$. Consequently, there is a reduction in the effective resonance frequency. Notably, the resonance frequency exhibits a high sensitivity to the topological charge $Q$, establishing a foundation for the optical detection of the topological states of skyrmions. For example, by manipulating $\alpha$ through doping and interface engineering, one can precisely adjust the resonance frequency for applications within the THz communication frequency band \cite{kumar2022, AZADIAN2017960, Weber_2019}.

When the parameter $G_z$ is less than or equal to $\alpha D$ ($G_z \leq \alpha D$) in equation \ref{omeg0}, the resonance frequency becomes imaginary. This condition indicates that the system is in an overdamped regime, where the motion of the system does not exhibit any resonance peak. In the overdamped regime, the system's response to an input or disturbance is characterized by non-oscillatory decay. The parameter $G_z$ represents the topological gain of the skyrmions, while $\alpha$ and $D$ are damping-related parameters. When the gain is relatively low compared to the damping, the system is driven into the overdamped regime, leading to a non-oscillatory response. This behavior is very similar to particles moving in viscous liquids. The critical damping coefficient is 
\begin{equation}
\alpha_c = G_z/D = |Q|/\eta,  \label{alc} 
\end{equation}
and the linewidth 
\begin{equation}\Gamma = 2\alpha D/m.
\end{equation} 
The optical peak is absent when $\alpha \geq \alpha_c = G_z/D$. When the damping factor $\alpha \geq \alpha_c$, the system enters an overdamped state, and the gyroscopic dynamics are completely suppressed. The motion of the skyrmion loses its periodicity, revealing the competitive relationship among the topological charge, damping, and inertial effects in the dynamics of magnetic skyrmions. The linewidth $\Gamma$, defined as the full width at half maximum (FWHM) of the resonance peak in the absorption spectrum of magnetic skyrmions driven by a THz optical field, is an important characteristic of the system \cite{Heoptic18}. The linewidth $\Gamma$, defined as the full width at half maximum (FWHM) of the resonance peak in the absorption spectrum of magnetic skyrmions driven by a THz optical field, is an important characteristic of the system \cite{Heoptic18}. A larger $\alpha$ indicates faster energy loss, which leads to a wider resonance peak.

The parameter $D$ depends on material properties such as saturation magnetization $M_s$, shape factor $\eta$, as well as the size of the skyrmion. A larger $D$ signifies a greater capacity for energy dissipation, resulting in a wider linewidth $\Gamma$. Conversely, an increase in the parameter $m$ slows the system's response, leading to a sharper resonance peak and more concentrated energy at a specific frequency. The linewidth $\Gamma$ indicates how quickly the system returns to its equilibrium state from the resonance state under the influence of the optical field. When $\Gamma$ is large, energy dissipates rapidly, producing a wide and weak resonance peak. In contrast, a small $\Gamma$ allows the system to maintain resonance for extended periods, resulting in a sharp and strong absorption peak. A low $\alpha$ and a small $m$ can decrease $\Gamma$ and enhance resonance intensity, making them suitable for high-sensitivity THz detectors. Additionally, materials with a high quality factor $Q$ can extend the range of critical damping values $\alpha_c$, enabling the tuning of resonance characteristics across a broader range of parameters. 

\section{Results and Discussions}
\begin{figure}[!t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=1.0\linewidth]{Fig-1.pdf}
\caption{The skyrmion trajectories with $Q=+1$ (a) and (b) under the right- and left-handed circularly polarized light respectively, $Q=-1$ (c) and (d) under the right- and left-handed circularly polarized light respectively, for the frequency $\omega=0.55$ THz, $\alpha=0.01$, and the time $t=20$ ps.}
\label{fig-skyrmion}
\end{figure}

Fig. \ref{fig-skyrmion} shows the helical motion trajectories of magnetic skyrmions with topological charges $Q = \pm 1$ driven under $\omega=0.55$ THz circularly polarized lasers within $t= 20$ ps, revealing the combined regulatory effect of the topological charge and the chirality of the light field on the skyrmion dynamics. The topological charge $Q$ determines the direction of helical motion. When driven by the same chiral circularly polarized light, skyrmions with $Q = +1$ spiral in a counterclockwise direction, whereas those with $Q = -1$ spiral in a clockwise direction. In contrast to topological charges, the chirality of circularly polarized light changes the phase of the spiral motion of the skyrmion. This is because the topological charge $Q$ of the skyrmion couples with the spin angular momentum of the circularly polarized light, generating a topology-dependent gyroscopic torque, which determines the direction of the helical rotation. When the direction of $Q$ is consistent with that of the angular momentum of the light field, the gyroscopic torque is maximized, driving a more significant helical motion. The helical radius of the skyrmion trajectory gradually increases with time but does not diverge, indicating that the system is in a steady-state forced motion. The laser frequency $\omega=0.55$ THz is very close to the intrinsic resonance frequency of the skyrmion, which is determined by the topological stiffness $G_z\propto Q$ and the mass $m$, leading to efficient energy absorption and conversion into the kinetic energy of the helical motion. According to the improved Thiele equation \ref{Thiele}, the circularly polarized light component drives the helical motion, and the sign of $G_z \propto Q$ determines the direction of the gyroscopic torque, competing or collaborating with the direction of the angular momentum of the light field, ultimately resulting in differences in the direction of the trajectory rotation.

By comparing the trajectories of skyrmions with topological charges $Q = +1$ and $Q = -1$ (refer to Fig. \ref{fig-skyrmion}a and c), it can be observed that, when subjected to the same polarization, their helical rotation directions are oppositely aligned. This observation underscores the chirality-flipping effect associated with the varying values of $Q$. Furthermore, the contrasting trajectory directions elicited by right-handed and left-handed polarization (illustrated in Fig. \ref{fig-skyrmion}a and b) demonstrate the non-equilibrium chiral dynamics initiated by the angular momentum of the light field. In experimental applications, the precise selection of the topological charge $Q$ and the polarization direction of incident light facilitates meticulous control over the motion direction of the skyrmion. This capability provides a viable method for directional transfers in topological devices, such as track memories, and informs the development of optically controlled logic gates.
The helical motion of the skyrmion is influenced by the coupling between the topological charge $Q$ and the angular momentum of the light field, demonstrating optically controlled topological dynamics of skyrmions. By choosing $Q$ and the light polarization, the switching between the counterclockwise/clockwise motion of the skyrmion can be achieved, laying the foundation for reconfigurable devices in the THz frequency band. Matching the laser frequency with the intrinsic frequency of the skyrmion can maximize the energy conversion efficiency and guide the design of low-power optical drive devices. Our theoretical predictions are consistent with recent experimental results on ultrafast optical excitation, which provide key experimental evidence for regulating light-magnetic coupling in topological spintronics \cite{Rouzegar2024}. Additionally, similar rotations of skyrmions induced by spin currents, magnetic fields, or temperature gradients have been observed through neutron scattering experiments \cite{jonietz2010, ever2012}.

Furthermore, the topological charge $Q$ results in a Coriolis-like force due to the gyroscopic coupling term $\boldsymbol{G} \times \boldsymbol{v}$, which compels the skyrmion to move in a circular motion, creating a helical trajectory. This phenomenon closely resembles the behavior of electrons with various spin orientations in a magnetic field, such as the well-known Stern-Gerlach experiment \cite{Gerlach1922dv}. Moreover, the similar gyrotropic trajectory of a skyrmion has been observed under an external magnetic field pulse, using time-resolved pump-probe X-ray holography \cite{Buttner2015}. This outcome suggests that the inertial term  $m\boldsymbol{\dot{v}}$ cannot be disregarded in the THz frequency range. The velocity of the skyrmion lags behind the driving force, leading to a phase difference and helical deformation. When circularly polarized light is applied, it generates a spin-polarized driving field through the IFE. The specific chirality of the light and the skyrmion's topological charges jointly control the trajectory of the skyrmion's motion. Experimentally, the topological charge $Q$ of the skyrmion can be determined by measuring the helical trajectory's rotation direction and the resonance frequency. Applying an optical pulse at this resonance frequency can effectively control the skyrmion's movement. This provides a theoretical foundation for the design of all-optical topological devices \cite{Kazemi2024}. 

