\centering
\includegraphics[width=1.0\linewidth]{Fig-2.pdf}
\caption{The skyrmion velocities with $Q=+1$ (a) and (b) under the right- and left-handed circularly polarized light respectively, $Q=-1$ (c) and (d) under the right- and left-handed circularly polarized light respectively, for the frequency $\omega=1.0$ THz, and the time $t=20$ ps, according to equation \ref{vxvy}.}
\label{velocity}
\end{figure}
Fig. \ref{velocity} illustrates the variations in the velocity components $v_x$ (in red) and $v_y$ (in blue) of magnetic skyrmions with topological charges $Q=\pm1$ within the balance of the gyroscopic force, dissipative force, and optical field force acting on the skyrmion, as described in equation \ref{vxvy}, driven by circularly polarized lasers operating at a frequency of $\omega = 1.0$ THz. This result highlights the interplay between the topological charge $Q$ and the chirality of circularly polarized light in tuning skyrmions' velocity dynamics. The phase difference between the optical driving forces and the sign of the topological charge determines the velocity components $v_x$ and $v_y$. When subjected to left-handed circularly polarized light, the phase difference between the forces $F_x$ and $F_y$ is $+\pi/2$. Conversely, the phase difference becomes $-\pi/2$ with right-handed circularly polarized light. This results in a consistent phase difference of $\pi$ between the components $v_x$ and $v_y$.

The gyroscopic term $G_z\propto Q$ indicates the direction of the velocity components. When $Q$ aligns with the angular momentum of the optical field, the gyroscopic torque is enhanced, leading to an increase in velocity amplitude. For instance, if $Q$ is in the same direction as the angular momentum of the optical field, the velocity amplitude reaches its maximum, which can be approximately $3~\mathrm{m/s}$. Conversely, the amplitudes decrease when the signs of $Q$ and the angular momentum do not match. Reversing the sign of $Q$ results in a simultaneous reversal of the directions of $v_x$ and $v_y$, as illustrated in Fig. \ref{velocity}c compared to Fig. \ref{velocity}a, demonstrating the chiral control of the topological charge over the dynamic direction. The topological dependence of the optical driving force $\boldsymbol{F} \propto Q (\hat{x} \pm i\hat{y})$ allows for direct regulation of the amplitudes and directions of the velocity components $v_x$ and $v_y$ by $Q$. According to equation \ref{vxvy}, when $G_z \gg \alpha D$ in conditions of low damping, the velocity amplitude is primarily determined by $G_z$ and is positively correlated with $Q$.

The driving laser frequency is in the THz regime, e.g. $\omega = 1.0 \, \mathrm{THz}$. At this frequency, the system operates in a non-resonant driving state. The velocity amplitude of the skyrmion remains stable over time, indicating that the system is in a steady-state forced vibration, with the energy input from optical fields balancing out dissipation. When comparing the trajectories of skyrmions with topological charges $Q = +1$ and $Q = -1$ (see Fig. \ref{velocity}a vs. c), we observe that the directions of their velocities are opposite under the same polarization driving. This reflects the chiral flipping effect associated with the topological charge. Furthermore, the differences in velocity directions caused by left-hand and right-hand circularly polarized light for the same $Q$ (illustrated in Fig. \ref{velocity}a vs. b) highlight the non-equilibrium dynamics resulting from the angular momentum injection of the optical field. By selecting the value of $Q$ and the light's polarization direction, the skyrmion's motion direction can be programmable. This capability lays the foundation for designing optical logic gates and racetrack memories. For instance, when a skyrmion with $Q = +1$ is driven by left-hand circularly polarized light, it moves counterclockwise (representing logic "1"). Conversely, the same skyrmion driven by right-hand circularly polarized light moves clockwise (representing logic "0"). The damping term $\alpha D$ in the governing equation serves to reduce the velocity amplitude. Thus, both the direction and amplitude of the skyrmion's velocity are jointly influenced by the topological charge $Q$ and the chirality of the light polarizations, confirming the programmability of the optically controlled topological dynamics. At the THz regime, the velocity response remains stable, providing a suitable parameter range for the design of high-frequency THz devices \cite{Phys033006, zhang014102}.

