\label{fig-spectrum}
\end{figure}

We show the dependence of the normalized THz absorption spectrum $\mathcal{R}(\omega)$ on the damping factor $\alpha$ and the topological charge $Q$, revealing the topological resonance characteristics and regulation mechanism of magnetic skyrmions in Fig. \ref{fig-spectrum}.
The results demonstrate the dependence of the resonance peak on the damping factor $\alpha$ [see Fig. \ref{fig-spectrum}(a)]. When $Q = 1$ and $m = 10^{-25}\, \mathrm{kg}$, the absorption peaks for all values of $\alpha$ appear at $\omega_0 \approx 0.55\, \mathrm{THz}$, which is determined by $\omega_0 = \sqrt{G_z^2 - (\alpha D)^2}/m$. The smaller the $\alpha$, the sharper the absorption peak. The higher the peak intensity, the FWHM $\Gamma = 2\alpha D/m$ decreases. The larger the $\alpha$ is, the more the damping dissipation increases, and the system approaches the overdamped state ($\alpha \to \alpha_c$), with the peak width increasing and the peak height decreasing. The results indicate that, under low-damping conditions, the gyroscopic dynamics of the skyrmion are predominant. Consequently, energy becomes more concentrated at the resonance frequency, enhancing absorption efficiency.

Fig. \ref{fig-spectrum}(b) demonstrates how the resonance characteristics are tuned through the topological charge $Q$. It is observed that as $Q$ increases, the resonance frequency shifts to higher values. For $\alpha = 0.01$, the approximate locations of the absorption peaks for $Q = 0.5, 1, 2, $ and $3$ are $0.28, 0.55, 1.10, $ and $1.65 \, \mathrm{THz}$, respectively. This conforms to the relationship $\omega_0\propto Q$. The underlying principle is that $G_z$ is directly proportional to $Q$, and since $\omega_0$ is given by the expression $\omega_0 \approx G_z/m$, it follows that $\omega_0$ is also proportional to $Q$. Furthermore, an increase in $Q$ enhances the resonance's intensity and sharpness. Thus, larger values of $Q$ result in higher and sharper absorption peaks. The elevation of $Q$ also strengthens the gyroscopic coupling term $G_z$, thereby improving the topological protection of the system and concentrating the resonance energy more effectively.

The THz absorption spectrum of magnetic skyrmions is controlled by the topological charge $Q$, the mass $m$, and the damping coefficient $\alpha$. This illustrates the fundamental relationship between topological dynamics and optomagnetic coupling \cite{Dorney2019}. By measuring the resonance frequency $\omega_0\propto Q$, we can directly determine the topological charge of the skyrmion, offering a new approach for characterizing chiral magnets. Applying a THz optical pulse near $\omega_0$ makes it possible to achieve low-energy consumption and high-precision manipulation of skyrmions, including their displacement and rotation. Optimizing $\alpha$ and $Q$ can enhance the resonance intensity, improving the device's sensitivity and speed. These findings provide fundamental theoretical support for designing topological optoelectronic devices, such as THz detectors and topological memories. They can be used to develop non-invasive techniques for topological characterization \cite{Mad2024110750}.

\begin{figure}[!t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=1.0\linewidth]{Fig-4.pdf}
\caption{The THz resonance frequency $\omega_0$ vs. the topological charge $Q$ for the different $\alpha$.}
\label{fig-peak-Q}
\end{figure}
Fig. \ref{fig-peak-Q} demonstrates the relationship between the THz resonance frequency $\omega_0$ and the topological charge $Q$ across various damping factors. This observation provides valuable insights into the fundamental principles underlying topological dynamics and the impact of damping on the THz resonance characteristics of magnetic skyrmions. The findings reveal a quasi-linear correlation between the resonance frequency and the topological charge, namely $\omega_0\propto Q$. Moreover, an increase in topological charge $Q$ is associated with a corresponding increase in the resonance frequency, attributed to the amplification of the gyroscopic coupling term $G_z$. This underscores the significant influence of the topological properties of skyrmions as the primary driving factor behind the THz response.

The damping factor $\alpha$ exerts an inhibitory effect on the resonance frequency. As $\alpha$ increases, the resonance frequency $\omega_0$ decreases. For the same topological charge $Q$ of skyrmions, the resonance frequency $\omega_0$ with $\alpha = 0.01$ is significantly higher than that with $\alpha = 0.10$. The damping term $\alpha D$ can diminish the effectiveness of the gyroscopic coupling, based on the formula $\omega_0 = \sqrt{G_z^2 -(\alpha D)^2}/m$. A lower value of $\alpha$ in magnetic materials can increase resonance frequencies, making it suitable for designing highly sensitive THz detection devices \cite{Shen_2012, Zhang2024b}. By measuring the linear relationship between $\omega_0$ and $Q$, one can directly determine the topological charge of the skyrmion, providing an optical method for characterizing chiral magnets. Applying a THz optical pulse near $\omega_0$ can selectively excite skyrmions with a specific $Q$, allowing for low-energy manipulation of topological states. By choosing $\alpha$ and $Q$, the resonance frequency and bandwidth can be tailored to meet the needs of applications such as THz communication and sensing.

\begin{figure}[!t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=1.0\linewidth]{Fig-5.pdf}
\caption{(a) The THz absorption spectrum $\mathcal{R}(\omega)$ and (b) the THz resonance frequency $\omega_0$ as a function of the damping factor $\alpha$ for the different topological charges $Q$. The dotted lines show the critical damping at $\alpha_c = G_z/D$. For $ \alpha > \alpha_c $, resonance vanishes.}
\label{fig-Romega0}
\end{figure}
Fig. \ref{fig-Romega0} illustrates how the topological charge $Q$ and the damping effect influence the THz response of magnetic skyrmions by examining the THz absorption spectrum (see Fig. \ref{fig-Romega0} (a)) and the resonance frequency (see Fig. \ref{fig-Romega0} (b)) concerning the damping factor $\alpha$. The absorption intensity decreases as $\alpha$ increases for all values of $Q = 0.5, 1, 2$, with the peak of the normalized THz absorption $\mathcal{R}(\omega)$ showing a significant decline as $\alpha$ rises. When $\alpha$ exceeds the critical value $\alpha_c = |Q|/\eta$ (indicated by the dashed lines), the absorption spectrum loses its resonance peak and transitions into an overdamped state. Additionally, the resonance frequency $\omega_0$ decreases with increasing $\alpha$. For a constant $Q$, $\omega_0$ decreases nonlinearly as $\alpha$ increases, approaching zero when $\alpha \to \alpha_c$.

When $\alpha = \alpha_c$, resonance disappears ($\omega_0 = 0$), and the curve terminates at the dashed line. An increase in $Q$ enhances the absorption and resonance frequency for the exact value of $\alpha$. The larger the magnitude of $Q$, the higher the peak absorption value $\mathcal{R}(\omega)$ and the greater the resonance frequency $\omega_0$. The value of $Q$ directly enhances the topological dynamic response of the system through the gyroscopic coupling term $G_z \propto Q$. As $|Q|$ increases, the critical damping $\alpha_c = |Q|/\eta$ also increases, enabling the material to maintain resonance at a higher $\alpha$. The critical damping value $\alpha_c$ precisely corresponds to the position of the dashed line in the figure, as shown for the theoretical model. When $\alpha \geq \alpha_c$, $\omega_0$ becomes an imaginary value, indicating that the complex frequencies can be achieved. Recent advances in complex frequencies and their wave-matter interactions present new opportunities for metamaterials and wave control, especially regarding non-Hermitian wave phenomena \cite{Kim2025a, Kim041024, Jiang2024}.

By selecting the topological charge $Q$ and the damping factor $\alpha$, it is possible to precisely control the intensity of THz absorption and the resonance frequency. It has been demonstrated that the topological charge $Q$ can be controlled by the perpendicular magnetic fields in magnetic nanostructures \cite{fu2025}. This control provides the necessary design flexibility for custom opto-magnetic devices \cite{AZADIAN2017960, Weber_2019}. Analyzing the relationship between the resonance frequency $\omega_0$ and the damping factor $\alpha$ enables the determination of $Q$, facilitating the non-destructive optical characterization of the topological charge associated with skyrmions. Materials with lower $\alpha$ values and higher $Q$ values can improve THz absorption efficiency, making them ideal for sensitive detection applications and low-energy consumption memory systems. Fig. \ref{fig-Romega0} illustrates the sensitivity of THz absorption and the resonance frequency of magnetic skyrmions to the topological charge and damping factor. Identifying a critical damping boundary and understanding the dominant influence of $Q$ on resonance characteristics provides essential physical foundations for the design and optimization of topological optoelectronic devices. This understanding paves the way for advancements in THz technologies based on skyrmions \cite{Barati2014}.

The larger the topological charge $Q$, the higher the critical damping parameter $\alpha_c$ becomes, making it more challenging for the system to transition into the overdamped state. In contrast, as the shape factor $ \eta $ increases, $\alpha_c$ decreases, making the system more likely to enter the overdamped state. The shape factor $\eta$ represents the geometric dissipation capability of the skyrmion. A higher $Q$ enhances topological protection, requiring greater damping to disrupt the resonance. To achieve highly sensitive THz detection, it is essential to select a low $\alpha$ in low-damping materials, a small $D$ to optimize the shape factor $\eta$, or a large effective mass $m$. By manipulating $Q$ and $\eta$, the critical damping $\alpha_c$ can be controlled, allowing for the design of THz devices suited for various damping environments. In the range where $\alpha < \alpha_c$, choosing optimal values for $\alpha$ and $m$ can enhance the linewidth $\Gamma$, leading to efficient energy absorption and low-power manipulation.

\section{Conclusions}  
In this work, we have developed a theoretical framework to elucidate the THz optical response of magnetic skyrmions by extending the Thiele equation to include inertial effects. The derived analytical model reveals a topological resonance in the absorption spectrum, governed by the skyrmion's topological charge $Q$, effective mass $m$, and damping factor $\alpha$. The resonance frequency $\omega_0 \propto Q/m$ enables optical detection of topological charge and mass, with critical damping $\alpha_c = |Q|/\eta$ dictating overdamped dynamics. Skyrmion trajectories and velocities exhibit helicity-dependent motion under circularly polarized THz fields, which are controlled via $Q$ and light polarization. The $Q$-dependent absorption spectrum and linewidth $\Gamma = 2\alpha D/m$ provide design methods for tunable THz modulators and sensors.  

Our results bridge Thiele dynamics with optical responses, offering a pathway to integrate skyrmions into high-frequency opto-spintronic devices. We hope our theoretical results can be verified experimentally via the inertial effects and explore material systems with tailored skyrmions' topological charge, effective mass, and damping factor for optimized THz performance. This study enhances the fundamental understanding of topological light-matter interactions and emphasizes the potential of skyrmions as adaptable components in next-generation photonic technologies.

