\subsection{Gromov-Witten invariants}

A CohFT, then consists of a system 

\begin{equation} 
\Omega\ =\ (\Omega_{_{g,n}})_{_{2g-2+n>0}}  
\end{equation}   
of tensors  

\begin{equation}  
\Omega_{_{g,n}}\ \in\ H^{^{\bullet}}\left(\overline{\mathfrak{M}}_{_{g,n}},\mathbb{Q}\right)\otimes(V^{\bullet})^{\otimes n},  
\end{equation}   
associating a cohomology in $H^{^{\bullet}}\left(\overline{\mathfrak{M}}_{_{g,n}},\mathbb{Q}\right)$ to vectors $v_{_1}, ..., v_{_n}\in V$ assigned to the $n$ markings.



In the context of HMS, they are particularly useful for calculations involving the A-side, as explained in what follows. 

To define Gromov-Witten invariants of a symplectic manifold, ${\cal M}$, consider moduli spaces of stable homolorphic maps,   

\begin{equation}  
\overline{\cal M}_{_{g,n,\beta}}(M)\ \overset{def.}{=}\ \left\{u:\Sigma_{_g}\rightarrow X \ \text{holomorphic},\  u[\Sigma_{_g}]=\beta\right\}\ / \sim,  
\end{equation}  
there are maps  

\begin{equation}  
\overline{\cal M}_{_{g,n}}\ \longleftarrow\ \overline{\cal M}_{_{g,n,\beta}}\ \longrightarrow\ M^{^n}  
\end{equation}  

\begin{equation}  
\Rightarrow \ \ \ I_{_{g,n,\beta}}:\ \ H^{^{\bullet}}(M)^{^{\otimes n}}\ \rightarrow\ H^{^{\bullet}}\left(\overline{\cal M}_{_{g,n,\beta}}\right).    
\end{equation}    


Then, the maps  

\begin{equation}  
I_{_{g,n}}\ =\ \sum_{_{\beta\in H_{_2}(M)}}r^{^{\omega(\beta)}}\ I_{_{g,n,\beta}}   
\end{equation}  
form a $\mathbb{C}[[r]]$-linear cohomological field theory.  








\section{Fukaya categories}  

\subsection{Lagrangian submanifolds}   

A Lagrangian submanifold of a symplectic manifold is a maximally isotropic submanifold, hence one on which the symplectic form vanishes, and which is maximal with this property. 

\underline{Example} For $X\equiv \mathbb{R}^{^{2n}}$ equipped with a canonical symplectic form, \cite{1},

\begin{equation}  
\omega\ =\ \sum_{_{i=1}^{^n}}dq_{_i}\wedge dp^{^i} 
\end{equation} 
standard Lagrangian submanifolds are submanifolds $\mathbb{R}^{^n}\ \hookrightarrow\ \mathbb{R}^{^{2n}}$ of fixed values of the $\left\{q_{_i}\right\}_{_{i=1}}^{^n}$ coordinates. Locally, every Lagrangian submanifold looks like this.

\underline{def.} A Lagrangian submanifold of a symplectic manifold $(X,\omega)$ is a submanifold $L\hookrightarrow X$ such that the following equivalent conditions hold:

\begin{enumerate}  
\item At each point $\ell\in L$, the tangent space  

\begin{equation}  
T_{_{\ell}}L\ \hookrightarrow\ T_{_{\ell}}X  
\end{equation}  
is a Lagrangian subspace of $T_{_{\ell}}X$ equipped with the symplectic form $\omega_{_X}$. 

\item $L\hookrightarrow X$ is a maximal isotropic submanifold.  

\end{enumerate}  

\underline{Remark (more advanced):} More generally, one can consider Lagrangian subamnifolds of symplectic structures in higher geometry, such as symplectic Lie n-algebroids equipped with their canonical invariant polynomials, thought of as dg-manifolds via their CE-algebra, and equipped with graded symplectic forms. Such structures are also referred to as $\Lambda$-structures, and a foliation by such leaves constitutes a Lagrangian foliation of a Lie algebroid. 

\subsection*{Types of Lagrangian submanifolds}  

Lagrangian submanifolds come into four main types, namely: minimal, calibrated, special, and graded. 

\begin{itemize}  

\item \underline{Minimal:} A minimal submanifold is a submanifold such that small deformations of its embedding do not affect its volume.

\item \underline{Calibrated:} Let $M$ be a Riemann manifold, and vol$_{_V}$ denote the induced volume for any given subspace $V\subset T_{_z} M$. A $k$-form is said to be calibrated on $M$ if it is closed and for all oriented $k$-dimensional subspaces $V\subset T_{_z}M, \eta|_{_V}=\lambda\text{vol}_{_V}$ for some $\lambda\le1$. A submanifold $N\hookrightarrow M$ is $\eta$-calibrated if $\forall z\in N$, $\eta|_{_{T_{_z}N}}=\text{vol}_{_V}$.  
\medskip 

\underline {Proposition} Let $(M,g)$ be a Riemannian manifold, $\eta$ a calibration on $M$, and $N$ a compact $\eta$-calibrated submanifold of $M$. Then $N$ is volume minimising in its homology class.
This tells us that the volume of calibrated submanifolds is unchanged by small variations of the embedding, thereby implying that calibrated submanifolds are minimal.



\item \underline{Special:} Let $M$ be a complex $n$-dimensional CY manifold with CY form $\Omega$ and K$\ddot{\text{a}}$hler form $\omega$. The volume form induced by the K$\ddot{\text{a}}$hler metric is then   

\begin{equation}  
\frac{\omega^{^n}}{n!}\ =\ \frac{1}{n!}\sum dz 
\end{equation} 
with $\omega|_{_L}=0$ (from the condition of a submanifold to be Lagrangian).  

CYs are compact by definition. Therefore, all holomorphic volume forms on them are locally constant. We can certainly scale $\Omega$ (uniquely up to a phase), such that    

\begin{equation}  
\frac{\omega^{^n}}{n!}\ =\ (-1)^{^{n(n-2)/2}}\left(\frac{i}{2}\right)^{^n}\Omega\wedge\overline\Omega.    
\end{equation}

Then, the scaled $\Omega$ is the unique CY form which is a tivial calibration on $M$. For any Lagrangian submanifold $L\hookrightarrow M$,  

\begin{equation}  
\text{vol}_{_L}\ =\ e^{^{i\pi\theta}}\Omega_{_L} 
\end{equation}  
for some $\theta:L\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$, referred to as the phase of $L$. If $\theta$=const., then the given Lagrangian submanifold is termed \emph{special}. 

Likewise, we could define them as the submanifolds of $M$ calibrated by Re$(\Omega)$ under some choice of a phase (i.e. the phase of $L$).    


\underline{Example} All special Lagrangian submanidfolds of a complex 1D CY manifold (i.e. an elliptic curve) are geodesics. However, the converse is also true, since geodesics are submanifolds of constant phase. 



\item \underline{Graded:}  $\theta$ is unique up to an integer shift. Given a fixed $\Omega$, if $\theta$=const. implies $L$ is special, this choice is called grading of $L$. 

A graded special Lagrangian submanifold therefore corresponds to a pair $(L,\theta)$. This corresponds to the concept of grading used later on in the context of HMS for elliptic curves.   

The shift  

\begin{equation}  
(L,\theta)[n]\ \overset{def.}{=}\ (L,\theta+n)  
\end{equation}  
mirrors the $n^{^{th}}$-shift functor on complexes of coherent sheaves. 

The shift  

\begin{equation}  
(L,\theta)\ \mapsto\ (L,\theta+1)  
\end{equation}   
corresponds to reversing the orientation of $L$ induced by vol$_{_L}$, since  

\begin{equation}  
\text{vol}_{_L}\ \mapsto \ -\text{vol}_{_L}\ \ \ \ \ \Rightarrow\ \ \ \ e^{^{i\pi\theta}}\ \mapsto\ e^{^{-i\pi\theta}}. 
\end{equation}

The notion of grading can be generalised to other Lagrangian submanifolds by defining the \emph{average phase} of $L$, $\phi(L)$, given modulo $\mathbb{Z}$ by the holomorphic ratio  

\begin{equation}  
\frac{\int_{_L}\Omega}{\int_{_L}\text{vol}_{_L}}\ =\ e^{^{i\pi\phi(L)}}, 
\end{equation}
which is invariant under Hamiltonian deformations of $L$. Assuming $L$ is homologous to a special Lagrangian submanifold, matches to the aforementioned restricted concept of grading.


\end{itemize}


\subsection{Lagrangian Floer cohomology}     

\subsubsection{Floer differential}  

The Floer differential is defined by the pseudo-holomorphic curves bounded by $L_{_0}, L_{_1}$, representing the worldsheet instantons in Physics. Specifically, let $J$ be an $\omega$-compatible almost complex structure on $M$, namely, an almost complex structure such that 

\begin{equation} 
g_{_J}(.,.)\ \equiv\ \omega(.,J.)  
\end{equation}  
is a Riemannian metric.     

Consider the space of pseudoholomorphic curves     

\begin{equation}  
u:\ \Sigma=\mathbb{R}\times[0,1]\ \rightarrow\ M  
\end{equation}  
meaning the curve $u$ satisfies the complex linear condition  

\begin{equation}  
J\circ du\ =\ du\ \circ J_{_o},   
\end{equation}   
with $J_{_o}$ denoting the almost complex structure of $\Sigma\subseteq\mathbb{C}$. The complex linear condition is equivalent to the Cauchy-Riemann equation  

\begin{equation}  
\overline{\partial}_{_J}\ =\ 0\ \ \ \ \Rightarrow\ \ \ \ \frac{\partial u}{\partial s}+J(u)\frac{\partial u}{\partial t}\ =\ 0  
\end{equation}   
with $(s,t)$ denoting real coordinates of $\Sigma\subseteq\mathbb{C}$.

\underline{def.} Let $\hat{\cal M}(p,q;[u],J)$ denote the \emph{moduli space} of pseudoholomorphic strips  

\begin{equation}  
u:\ \mathbb{R}\times[0,1]\ \rightarrow\ (M,J) 
\end{equation}  
in the homotopy class $[u]\in \pi_{_2}(M, L_{_0}\cup L_{_1})$ with Lagrangian boundary conditions  

\begin{equation}  
\begin{cases}  
&u(s,0)\ \in\ L_{_0}, \ u(s,1)\ \in\ L_{_1}\ \forall s\ \in \ \mathbb{R}\\
&\underset{s\rightarrow\infty}{\lim}\ u(s,t)\ =\ p, \ \underset{s\rightarrow-\infty}{\lim}\ u(s,t)\ =\ q  
\end{cases}  
\end{equation}
and finite energy condition  

\begin{equation}  
E(u)\ =\ \omega([u])\ =\ \int\ u^{^*}\omega\ =\ \int\left|\frac{\partial u}{\partial s}\right|^{^2}dt\ ds\ <\ \infty.  
\end{equation}

We can further simplify the moduli space by quotienting the action of $\mathbb{R}$-translations on it. This can be readily checked by taking $a\in\mathbb{R}$ and noticing that 

\begin{equation}  
(a\cdot u)(s,t)\ =\ u(s-a,t).  
\end{equation}

Then, the orbit action of $\mathbb{R}$-translations provide the quotiented moduli space, that we will be denoting as ${\cal M}(p,q;[u],J)$.  

\medskip 

\underline{Remark:}  
Let $D_{_{\overline\partial_{_{J}},u}}$ be the linearisation of $\overline\partial_{_{J}}$ at a given strip $u$. The linearised operator $D_{_{\overline\partial_{_{J}},u}}$ is a 1$^{^{st}}$-order differential operator, meaning that it maps to a section of $u^{^*}TM$ satisfying the Lagrangian boundary conditions specified above to a $u^{^*}TM$-valued $(0,1)$-form on $\Sigma$, namely a section of the bundle $u^{^*}TM\otimes\Lambda^{^{(0,1)}}T\Sigma$. 

The lienarised operator is also a Fredholm operator, whose index is defined by  

\begin{equation}  
\text{ind}_{_{\mathbb{R}}}D_{_{\overline\partial_{_{J}},u}}\ \overset{def.}{=}\ \text{dim\ Ker}D_{_{\overline\partial_{_{J}},u}}-\text{dim\ coKer}D_{_{\overline\partial_{_{J}},u}}.  
\end{equation}

If its action on $\hat{\cal M}$ is surjective, then, 

\begin{equation}  
\text{dim}\hat{\cal M}\ =\ \text{ind}_{_{\mathbb{R}}}D_{_{\overline\partial_{_{J}},u}}  
\end{equation}  
as a Fredholm operator\footnote{This condition is ensured by performing Hamiltonian perturbation, if not transversally-intersecting.}.



The Floer differential 


\begin{equation}  
\partial:\ CF(L_{_o}, L_{_1})\ \rightarrow\ CF(L_{_o},L_{_1})  
\end{equation}    
is the $\Lambda$-linear map defined by 

\begin{equation}  
\partial(p)\ =\ \underset{[u]:\text{ind}([u])=1}{\sum_{_{q\in\chi(L_{_o},L_{_1})}}}\left(n.{\cal M}(p,q;[u],J)\right)T^{^{\omega([u])}}q,    
\end{equation}   
where $n.{\cal M}(p,q;[u],J)$ denotes the number of points in ${\cal M}(p,q;[u],J)$. The physical meaning of implementing the Floer differential is the tunnelling of an open string at one point to another, as shown in the Figure.  

The most important theorem in Floer cohomology is $\partial^2=0$. The proof of this identity relies on Gromov's compactness theorem. 

\subsubsection{Relation to Morse Theory}  

Let $X$ be a complete Riemannian manifold, not necessarily of finite dimension. Consider a smooth function $f$ on $X$, i.e. $f\in C^{^{\infty}}(X,\mathbb{R})$. The essential feature of Morse Theory and its generalisations is the relationship between the structure of the critical set of $f$, 

\begin{equation}    
C(f)\ \equiv\ \{x\in X: df(x)=0\}  
\end{equation}  
and the topology of $X$. Morse theory yields very interesting results for functions on finite-dimensional Riemann manifolds. 

However, it can also be extended to infinite-dimensional situations.   

\medskip     

\underline{Examples}  

\medskip       

Given a Riemannian manifold, $X$, diffeomorphic to the 2-sphere, $S^{^2}$, and studying smooth functions on $X$, more specifically, taking two functions $f_{_1}, f_{_2}$ whose leve; set graphs are exhibited in the following figure, with the vertical axis describing the value of the functions.  


The idea of Morse Theory is that of extracting information about the global topology of $X$ from the critical points of $f$, i.e. from the points $p\in X$ with $df(p)=0$. This is not invariant: for $f_{_1}$, there are two critical points, for $f_{_2}$ there are four. The local geometry of the function in the vicinity of a critical point requires assigning a Morse index $\mu(p)$ to each critical point $p$ as the number of linearly-independent directions on which the second derivative $d^{^2}f(p)$ is negative-definite. Equivalently, this is the dimension of the \emph{unstable manifold}, $W^{^u}(p)$.  

Such unstable manifold is defined as follows: consider the solutions of  


\begin{equation}       
x:\ \mathbb{R}\ \longrightarrow\ M  
\ \ \ , \ \ \ \ \dot x(t)\ =\ -\text{grad}\ f(x(t))\ \ \ ,\  \ \ \forall t\in \mathbb{R}.  
\end{equation}   

At this point, define the gradient of $f$ as the vector field dual to the 1-form $df$. The flow lines $x(t)$ are curves of steepest descent for $f$. For $t\rightarrow\pm\infty$, each flow line $x(t)$ converges to some critical points $p=x(-\infty), q=x(\infty)$ of $f$. The unstable manifold $W^{^u}(p)$ of a critical point $p$ then simply consists of all flow lines $x(t)$ with $x(-\infty)=p$, i.e. those flow lines that emanate from $p$.  

In these examples, 

\begin{equation}  
\mu_{_{f_{_1}}}\left(p_{_1}\right)\ =\ 2\ \ \ ,\ \ \ \mu_{_{f_{_1}}}\left(p_{_2}\right)\ =\ 0,   
\end{equation}  
and for $f_{_2}$    

\begin{equation}  
\mu_{_{f_{_2}}}\left(p_{_1}\right)\ =\ 2\ \ \ ,\ \ \ \mu_{_{f_{_2}}}\left(p_{_2}\right)\ =\ 2\ \ \ ,\ \ \   \mu_{_{f_{_2}}}\left(p_{_3}\right)\ =\ 1\ \ \ ,\ \ \ \mu_{_{f_{_2}}}\left(p_{_4}\right)\ =\ 0.
\end{equation}     


This follows from the fact that $f_{_1}$ has a maximum point, $p_{_1}$, and a minimum, $p_{_2}$, as its only critical points, whereas $f_{_2}$ has two local maxima, $p_{_1}, p_{_2}$, a saddle point, $p_{_3}$, and a minimum, $p_{_4}$.    

The unstable manifold is therefore topologically a cell (i.e. homeomorphic to an open ball), $W^{^u}(p)$, of dimensions $\mu(p)$, and the manifold $X$ is the union of the unstable manifolds of the critical points of the function. Thus, we get a decomposition of $X$ into cells.  

To see the local effects of critical points, we need to intersect $W^{^u}(p)$ with a small ball around $p$, and contract the boundary of that intersection to a point. We then obtain a pointed sphere $\left(S^{^{\mu(p)}},\text{pt.}\right)$ of dimension $\mu(p)$. These local constructions already yield an important topological invariant, namely the Euler characteristic $\chi(X)$, as the alternating sum of these dimensions  

\begin{equation}  
\chi(X)\ =\ \sum_{_{p}}(-1)^{^{\mu(p)}}\mu(p),    
\end{equation}   
where $p$ are the critical points of $f$. The signs $(-1)^{^{\mu(p)}}$ are introduced to get some cancellations between the contributions from the individual critical points. This issue is handled in more generality by introducing the boundary operator $\partial$. From the point of view of Floer theory, consider pairs $(p,q)$ of critical points with $\mu(q)=\mu(p)-1$, namely of index difference 1. We then count the number of trajectories from $p$ to $q$ mod 2    

\begin{equation}    
\partial p\ =\ \sum_{_q}\ \left(\text{number of lines from}\  p\  \text{to}\  q \ \text{mod 2}\right)\ q.    
\end{equation}  

This way, we can get an automorphism from the vector space over $\mathbb{Z}_{_2}$ generated by the critical points of $f$ to itself, $C_{_*}\left(f, \mathbb{Z}_{_2}\right)$. The crucial point is then to show that 

\begin{equation}    
\partial\ \circ \ \partial \ =\ 0.   
\end{equation}  

On this basis, one can define the homology groups 


\begin{equation}  
H_{_k}(X, f,\mathbb{Z})\ \overset{def.}{=}\ \text{ker}(\partial)_{_{C_{_k}(f,\mathbb{Z}_{_2})}}\bigg/\text{im}(\partial)_{_{C_{_{k+1}}(f,\mathbb{Z}_{_2})}}, 
\end{equation}
where $C_{_k}(f,\mathbb{Z}_{_2})$ is generated by the critical points of Morse index $k$.

\subsection{Gromov's compactness theorem} 

Any sequence of pseudo holomorphic curves with uniformly bounded energy has a subsequence that converges to nodal configurations up to reparametrisation.   

For pseudoholomorphic strips satisfying Lagrangian boundary conditions, there are three types of admissible configurations:  

\begin{enumerate}  

\item   Broken strips. 

\begin{figure}[ht!]  
\begin{center}  
\includegraphics[scale=0.6]{gromov.pdf}  
\caption{\small}  
\end{center}  
\end{figure}

Energy concentrates at either end, $s\rightarrow\pm\infty$, meaning that there is a sequence $a_{_n}\rightarrow\pm\infty$ such that the translated strips $u_{_n}(s-a_{_n},t)$ converge to a non-constant limit strip. 


\item The disk bubble.     

\begin{figure}[ht!]  
\begin{center}  
\includegraphics[scale=0.6]{gromov1.pdf}  
\caption{\small}  
\end{center}  
\end{figure}

Energy concentrates at a point on the boundary of the strips, where suitable rescalings of $u_{_n}$ converge to a pseudoholomorphic disk in ${\cal M}$ with boundary entirely contained in, either, $L_{_o}$ or $L_{_1}$.  



\item  The sphere bubble.   

Energy concentrates at an interior point of the strip, where suitable rescalings of $u_{_n}$ converge to a pseudoholomorphic sphere in $M$.   


\begin{figure}[ht!]  
\begin{center}  
\includegraphics[scale=0.6]{gromov2.pdf}  
\caption{\small}  
\end{center}  
\end{figure}

\end{enumerate}   

The broken strip constitutes the nontrivial contribution to the proof of the identity $\partial^{^2}=0$. This is because, both, the disk bubbles and the sphere bubbles can be regarded as elements in $\pi_{_2}(M,L_{_i})$. As such, the symplectic area of all bubbles vanishes.  

Considering only homotopy class $[u]$ of Maslov index 2, according to Gromov's compactness theorem, the boundaries of the compactified moduli space $\overline{\cal M}(p,q;[u],J)$ consists of broken strips connecting $p$ to $q$ in the class $[u]$. The components of the broken strips correspond to the moduli spaces ${\cal M}(p,r;[u^{^{\prime}}],J)$ and ${\cal M}(r,q;[u^{^{\prime\prime}}],J)$ where $r$ denotes any generator of the Floer complex, and the relevant homotopy classes satisfy the following relation   

\begin{equation}  
\left[u^{^{\prime}}\right]+\left[u^{^{\prime\prime}}\right]\ =\ \left[u\right].  
\end{equation}  

Since the Maslov index is additive under the addition of homotopy classes,   

\begin{equation}  
\text{dim}{\cal M}(p,q;[u],J)\ =\ \text{ind}([u])-1  
\end{equation}  
the Maslov index of a non-compact strip must be at least 1. Thus, the only possibility is a broken strip consisting of two components of Maslov index 1. Furthermore, the gluing theorem states that every broken strip is locally the limit of a unique family of index 2 strips.  

Hence, the boundaries of the compactified moduli space is precisely the case of broken strips, and each broken strip consists of two strips of Maslov index 1. 

Overall, we get  

\begin{equation}  
\partial\overline{\cal M}(p,q;[u],J)\ =\ \underset{\left[u^{^{\prime}}\right]+\left[u^{^{\prime\prime}}\right]\ =\ \left[u\right]}{\bigsqcup_{_{r\in\chi(L_{_o},L_{_1})}}} \left({\cal M}(p,r;[u^{^{\prime}}],J)\ \times\ {\cal M}(p,q;[u^{^{\prime\prime}}],J)\right),
\end{equation}    
with unit indices  

\begin{equation}  
\text{ind}\left(\left[u^{^{\prime}}\right]\right)\ =\ \text{ind}\left(\left[u^{^{\prime\prime}}\right]\right)\ =\ 1.   
\end{equation}  

The total number of boundary points of a compact 1-manifold is always zero mod 2, hence, 

\begin{equation}  
\begin{aligned}
\partial^{^2}p\ &=\ \sum_{_{...}}\left(n.{\cal M}\left(p,r;\left[u^{^{\prime}}\right],J\right)\right)T^{^{\omega\left(\left[u^{^{\prime}}\right]\right)}}\partial r  \\
&=\sum_{_{r\in\chi\left(L_{_o},\chi_{_1}\right)}}\sum_{_{q\in\chi\left(L_{_o},\chi_{_1}\right)}}\left(n.{\cal M}\left(p,r;\left[u^{^{\prime}}\right],J\right)\right)\left(n.{\cal M}\left(p,r;\left[u^{^{\prime}}\right],J\right)\right)T^{^{\omega\left(\left[u^{^{\prime}}\right]\right)+\omega\left(\left[u^{^{\prime\prime}}\right]\right)}}q\\
&=0.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}



