\subsection{The wrapped Fukaya category}     

\subsubsection{Hamiltonian perturbation}   

As already mentioned when introducing Floer cohomology, Hamiltonian perturbation is required when one wishes to calculate the Floer complex $CF\left(L_{_0}, L_{_1}\right)$ for the case of non-transversally intersecting Lagrangians, especially for the case $L_{_0}\ \equiv\ L_{_1}$. We now turn to briefly overview how this affects the calculation of the Floer differential.

Let $H_{_t}\ \in\ C^{^{\infty}}(M\times[0,1])$ be a time-dependent Hamiltonian, $X_{_{H_{_t}}}$ be the Hamiltonian vector field generated by $H_{_t}$ and the 1-parameter flow\footnote{Also referred to as Hamiltonian isotopy.}, $\phi_{_t}$, generated by $X_{_{H_{_t}}}$.   

Let     


\begin{equation}  
J\ \in\ C^{^{\infty}}\left([0,1], {\cal J}(M,\omega)\right)  
\end{equation}
be a generic smooth family of $\omega$-compatible almost complex structures.   
The Floer data, $\left(H_{_t}, J\right)$, should ensure the regularity condition\footnote{Namely $D_{_{\overline{\partial}_{_{J,u}}}}$ is surjective at any strip $u$.} and $L_{_0}$ and $\phi_{_1}^{^{-1}}L_{_1}$ intersect transversally. Then, the Cauchy-Riemann equation becomes  

\begin{equation} 
\frac{\partial\tilde u}{\partial s}\ +\ \tilde{J}(\tilde{u})\frac{\partial\tilde u}{\partial t}\ =\ 0 ,    
\end{equation}   
where    


\begin{equation}   
\tilde{J}= (\phi_{_t})^{^{-1}}{_{_*}}\ \ \ J\ \ \ \ \ \ , \ \ \ \ \ \ \tilde u(s,t)\ =\ \left(\phi_{_t}\right)^{^{-1}}\left(u(s,t)\right).     
\end{equation}   

From such data, the Floer complex in presence of a Hamiltoninan perturbation can be redefined as follows  

\begin{equation}  
CF\left(L_{_0}, L_{_1}; H_{_t}, J\right)\ \overset{def.}{=}\ CF\left(L_{_o}, \left(\phi_{_t}\right)^{^{-1}}(L_{_1}); J_{_t}\right)  .    
\end{equation}    


\medskip 

\medskip 


\underline{Theorem} [D. Auroux]  

\medskip    

Given $L_{_0}, L_{_1}$, then the Floer complex $(CF(L_{_0}, L_{_1}),\partial)$ does not depend on the choice of the time-dependent almost-complex structure, and the Hamiltonian isotopy, up to equivalence.   


\subsubsection{The wrapped Fukaya category}

The last thing to find is the mirror of ${\cal O}$.  Note that 


\begin{equation}  
\text{Hom}^{^{\bullet}}({\cal O},{\cal O})\ =\ \mathbb{C}[z^{^{\pm 1}}]  
\end{equation}  
is infinite dimensional. Therefore, we need $\mathbb{C}<L\cap \varphi(L)>$ to be infinite dimensional as well. For this to occur, $L$ must be non-compact. Require these non-compact objects to be well-behaved at infinity. By this we mean that the Lagrangians will radially wrap the cylinder as approaching infinity.  

The wrapped Fukaya category, ${\cal W}Fuk(X)$, is therefore characterised as follows  

\begin{itemize}  
\item  Objects are exact Lagrangian submanifolds such that they are invariant under Liouville flow.

\item   Morphisms (invariance of intersection at infinity)  

\begin{equation}  
\text{Hom}(L,\varphi(L))\ =\ \bigoplus_{_{p\in L\cap \varphi(L)}}\mathbb{C}\cdot p. 
\end{equation}     



\item   Product: the composition 
\begin{equation}  
\text{Hom}(L_{_o},L_{_1})\ \otimes\ \text{Hom}(L_{_1},L_{_2})\ \rightarrow\ \text{Hom}(L_{_o},L_{_2})\ 
\end{equation}     
is given by   


\begin{equation}  
(p,q)\ \mapsto\ \sum_{_r}\eta_{_{pqr}}\cdot r, 
\end{equation}    
where $\eta_{_{pqr}}$ is the count of holomorphic disks with boundary.


\end{itemize}    


