Figure \ref{fig:tr} shows a holomorphic triangle as would arise in $C^*$ when acting with a Hamiltonian perturbation to evaluate cohomology of noncompact Lagrangian self-intersections. In particular, in the figure reproduced above we have that $\varphi^2_{_H}$ is a perturbation of $L$ by double the amount. $\varphi_{_H}(p)\equiv\varphi_{_H}(L\cap \varphi_{_H}L)$, and $r^{^{\prime}}\in L\cap \varphi(L)$.   

For the case of the cylinder, the noncompact Lagrangian in question is 

\begin{equation}  
L\ \equiv\ \mathbb{R}\ \simeq\ \mathbb{R}\times\{\text{pt}\}\ =\ \{\theta=0\}.   
\end{equation} 

We need to calculate $End(L)$. In order to do so, we need $L$ to wrap the cylinder. Note that  

\begin{equation}  
\text{Hom}(L,L)\ =\ \bigoplus_{_{n\in\mathbb{Z}}}\mathbb{C}\cdot x_{_n}.    
\end{equation}   

This has infinite rank, but, to get a true correspondence, we need to compute the multiplication structure $x_{_n}\cdot x_{_n}$, and this, in turn, requires introducing a second perturbation, so that we can get a third Lagrangian, resulting in the holographic triangle pictured above.   

\underline{Remark:} We would like 



\begin{equation}   
x_{_n}\ \in\ \text{Hom}(L,L)  
\end{equation}   
to correspond to the power   

\begin{equation}   
z^{^n}\ \in \mathbb{C}\left[z^{^{\pm1}}\right].     
\end{equation}


The way to define the product equation is as follows: take $L\cap \varphi_{_H}(L)$ and an intersection point of $\varphi_{_H}(L)\cap\varphi^2(L)$. These points determine a third point $L\cap \varphi^2_{_H}(L)$ which is the product we want to define.  

