\begin{center} 
\includegraphics[scale=1]{commutative-diagram.pdf}    
\end{center}  
\end{figure}    
where $\rho$ and $p$ denote the restriction and quotient projection functor, respectively.
$\rho$ singles-out the ends of a Lagrangian submanifold of $\left(\mathbb{C}^{^*}\right)^{^n}\backslash H$ which lie on the missing divisor $H$. $\rho$ is the composition of restriction to a neighbourhood of $H$ isomorphic to the product of $H$ with a punctured disk $\mathbb{D}^{^*}$, and projection from $H\times \mathbb{D}^{^*}$ to $H$.  

From the description of the commutative diagram we have just provided, some additional remarks are in order:  

\begin{itemize}  

\item  The top row in the diagram above fits with the general philosophy that removing a divisor from a symplectic manifold $\left(\left(\mathbb{C}^{^*}\right)^{^n}\right)$ should correspond to a degeneartion of its mirror to a singular space (namely $Z$). The level set of ${\cal W}$ provides exactly such degeneration.

\item   $\left(\mathbb{C}^{^*}\right)^{^n}\backslash H$ can itself be viewed as a hypersurface in $\left(\mathbb{C}^{^*}\right)^{^{n+1}}$, defined by  

\begin{equation}  
\hat f \left(x_{_1},...,x_{_{n+1}}\right)\ =\ f \left(x_{_1},...,x_{_{n+1}}\right)+x_{_{n+1}}\ =\ 0.  
\label{eq:hatw}   
