\section{$\mathbb{CP}^{^1}$} \label{sec:3}  

We now switch gears to a slightly more general manifestation of HMS, namely $\mathbb{CP}^{^1}$. In such case, HMS ceases to be marely a correspondence among manifolds, since one side involves a Fano variety. The idea that quantum cohomology ring is equivalent to the Jacobian ring begins from one of Givental's Theorem, stating that the D-module defined by an oscillatory integral with the superpotential as its kernel is isomorphic to $S^{^1}$-equivariant Floer cohomology pf periodic Hamiltonian systems.  
The main idea in this example is that of combining such information to extract information on Lagrangian submanifolds.  

\subsection{Equivariant cohomology}  

Equivariant cohomology, a.k.a. Borel cohomology, is a cohomology theory from algebraic topology that applies to topological spaces equipped with a group action. It can be viewed as a common generalisation of group cohomology and an ordinary cohomology theory.  


The equivariant cohomology ring of a space $X$ with actionn of a topological group $G$ is defined as the ordinary cohomology wing with coefficient ring $\Lambda$ of the homotopy quotient 

\begin{equation}   
H^{^{\bullet}}_{_G}\ (X;\Lambda)\ =\ H^{^{\bullet}}\left(EG\times_{_G}X;\Lambda\right).   
\label{eq:eqiv}   
\end{equation}   

If $G$ is the trivial group, then \eqref{eq:eqiv} reduces to the ordinary idempotent ring of $X$, wheras, if $X$ is contractible, it reduces to the cohomology ring of the classifying space $BG$. If $G$ acts freely on $X$, then the canonical map 

\begin{equation}  
EG\times_{_G}X\ \rightarrow\ X/G   
\end{equation}  
is a homotopy equivalence, and \eqref{eq:eqiv} reduces to   

\begin{equation}    
H^{^{\bullet}}_{_G}\ (X;\Lambda)\ =\ H^{^{\bullet}}(X/G;\Lambda).   
\label{eq:eqiv1}   
\end{equation}



\subsection{A-side on $\mathbb{CP}^{^1}$}  

Given a symplectic manifold, $M$, and an $S^{^1}$-invariant symplectic form, $\omega$, we have 

\begin{equation}  
\omega(\mathbb{CP}^{^1})\ =\ 2\pi.   
