\subsubsection{Adic Topology}   

A linear topology of a ring $R$ in which the fundamental system of neighbourhoods of zero consists of powers $\mathfrak{m}^{^n}$ of some 2-sided ideal $\mathfrak{m}$. The topology is then said to be $\mathfrak{m}$-adic, and the ideal $\mathfrak{m}$ is said to be the defining ideal of the topology. 

The closure of any set $F\subset A$ in the $\mathfrak{m}$-adic topology is equal to   

\begin{equation}   
\bigcap_{_{n\ge 0}}\left(F+\mathfrak{m}^{^n}\right)  
\end{equation}  

In particular, the topology is said to be separable if and only if  

\begin{equation}  
\bigcap_{_{n\ge 0}}\mathfrak{m}^{^n}\ = \ 0.     
\end{equation}   

The separable completion $\hat R$ of the ring $R$ in an $\mathfrak{m}$-adic topology is isomorphic to the projective limit   

\begin{equation}  
\underset{\longleftarrow}{\lim}\left(R/\mathfrak{m}^{^n}\right).  
\end{equation}  

The $\mathfrak{m}$-adic topology of an $R$-module, $P$, is defined in a similar manner: its fundamental system of neighbourhoods of zero is given by the submodules $\mathfrak{m}^{^n}P$; in the $\mathfrak{m}$-adic topology, $P$ becomes a topological $R$-module.    

Let $R$ be a commutative ring with identity with an $\mathfrak{m}$-adic topology, and let $\hat R$ be its completion; if $\mathfrak{m}$ is an ideal of finite type, the topology in $\hat R$ is $\hat{\mathfrak{m}}$-adic, and 

\begin{equation}
    \hat{\mathfrak{m}}^{^n}\ = \ \mathfrak{m}^{^n}\hat R.    
    \end{equation}  

    If $\mathfrak{m}$ is a maximal ideal, the $\hat R$ is the local ring with maximal ideal $\hat{\mathfrak{m}}$.  

A local ring topology is an adic topology defined by its maximal ideal (an $\mathfrak{m}$-adic topology).    


A fundamental tool in the study of adic topologies of rings is the following Lemma  

\medskip 

\medskip 

\underline{Lemma}\ [Artin, Rees]   

\medskip   
Let $R$ be a commutative Noetherian ring, let $\mathbb{m}$ be an ideal in $R$, let $E$ be an $R$-module of finite type, and let $F$ be a submodule of $E$. Then there is a $k$ such that, $\forall n\ge 0$,   



\begin{equation}  
\mathfrak{m}^{^n}\left(\mathfrak{m}^{^k} E\cap F\right)\ =\ \mathfrak{m}^{^{k+n}} E\cap F  
\end{equation}   

The topological interpretation of the ARtin-Rees Lemma shows that the $\mathfrak{m}$-adic topology of $F$ is induced by the $\mathfrak{m}$-adic topology of $E$. It follows that:  

\begin{enumerate}  

\item   The completion $\hat R$ of a ring $R$ in the $\mathfrak{m}$-adic topology is a flat $R$-module.  

\item  The completion $\hat E$ of the $R$-module $E$ of finite type is identical with  


\begin{equation}  
E\ \underset{R}{\otimes}\ \hat R.    
\end{equation}

\item Krull's Theorem holds. 

\end{enumerate}    

\medskip 

\medskip 

\underline{Theorem}\ [Krull]   

\medskip  


The $\mathfrak{m}$-adic topology of a Noetherian\footnote{A ring, $R$, is said to be Noetherian if any ideal of $R$ is finitely-generated.} ring is separable if and only if the set $1+\mathfrak{m}$ contains no zero divisors. In particular, the topology is separable if $\mathfrak{m}$ is contained in the (Jacobson) radical\footnote{The Jacobson radical $J(R)$ of a ring $R$ is the intersection of the maximal ideals of $R$. Since all maximal ideals are prime, the nilradical is contained in the Jacobson radical.} of the ring.     

\subsubsection{Matrix factorisation (MF)}    


Let $R$ be a commutative regular $\mathbb{k}$-algebra. Take ${\cal W}\in R\backslash K$.A matrix factorisation of ${\cal W}$ is defined by the pair $(P, dP)$, where 

\begin{equation}  
P\ =\ P^{^0}\ \oplus\ P^{^1}  
\end{equation}
is a $\mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}$-graded, finitely-generated projective $R$-module, and $d_{_P}\ \in\ \text{End}^{^{odd}}(P)$ is an $R$-linear morphism of odd degree with 

\begin{equation}  
d_{_P}^{^2}\ =\ {\cal W}\circ\ \text{id}_{_P}.
\end{equation}

Then, $d_{_P}$ consists of $\varphi\in \text{Hom}_{_R}(P^{^1}, P^{^0})$ and $\psi\in\text{Hom}_{_R}(P^{^0},P^{^1})$ with 

\begin{equation}  
\varphi\ \circ\ \psi\ =\ {\cal W}\ \circ \text{id}_{_{P^{^0}}}\ \ \ ,\ \ \ \psi\ \circ \ \varphi\ =\ {\cal W}\ \circ\ \text{id}_{_{P^{^1}}} 
\end{equation}

For MF $(P, d_{_P})$ and $(P^{^{\prime}}, d_{_{P^{^{\prime}}}})$, the $\mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}$-graded module of $R$-linear morphisms from $P$ to $P^{^{\prime}}$ with a differential 

\begin{equation}  
d(f)\ =\ d_{_{P^{^{\prime}}}}\circ f -(-1)^{^{|f|}}f\circ d_{_P}  
\end{equation}
is denoted by MF($P,P^{^{\prime}}$). Here $f$ is a homogeneous $R$-linear morphisms and $|f|\ \in\ \mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}$ is the degree of $f$. This data defines a dg-category MF(${\cal W}$), where composition of morphisms is naturally defined. Then, $[MF({\cal W})]$ is a triangulated category. 

