\subsection{Categories of branes for Givental type potential functions}  
Given a commutative ring, $R$, and $f, g_{_1}, ..., g_{_r}\in R$, $\lambda_{_1}, ..., \lambda_{_r}\in \mathfrak{k}$, with $\mathfrak{k}$ a regular algebra. Assume $g_{_i}$ are invertible for every $i$. This data is usually encoded in the Givental potential 

\begin{equation} 
{\cal F}\ =\ f-\lambda_{_1}\log g_{1}-...-\lambda_{r}\log g_{_r}.  
\end{equation}

Although ${\cal F}$ is not well-defined as a single-valued function, its differential can be defined as follows  

\begin{equation}
d{\cal F}\ =\ df-\lambda_{_1}\frac{dg_{_1}}{g_{_1}}-...-\lambda_{_r}\frac{dg_{_r}}{g_{_r}}, 
\end{equation}
with Crit$({\cal F})$ defining the scheme-theoretic 0-locus of $d{\cal F}$. Assume that ${\cal F}$ has isolated singularities, then 

\begin{equation} 
\text{dim}(\text{Crit}({\cal F}))\ =\ 0.  
\end{equation}

Let $\mathfrak{m}\in\text{Span}(R)$ be a maximal ideal of $R$ and $(\hat R_{_{\mathfrak{m}}},\hat{\mathfrak{m}})$ be the completion w.r.t. the m-adic topology, with $\hat{\mathfrak{m}}$ the maximal ideal. For $\mathfrak{h}\in R$, the corresponding element in $\hat R_{_{\mathfrak{m}}}$ is denoted by $\hat{\mathfrak{h}}_{_{\mathfrak{m}}}$.  

For an invertible element $h\in\hat R_{_{\mathfrak{m}}}\backslash\hat{\mathfrak{m}}$, defining 

\begin{equation}  
\log\mathfrak{h}\ =\ \sum_{_{k=1}}^{^{\infty}}(-1)^{^{k-1}}\frac{1}{k}\left(\frac{\mathfrak{h}-\mathfrak{h}_{_o}}{\mathfrak{h}_{_o}}\right)^{^k} 
\end{equation}  
where $\mathfrak{h}(0)\neq 0\in k$ is the value of $\mathfrak{h}$ at $\hat{\mathfrak{m}}$. Then, $\hat{\cal F}_{_{\mathfrak{m}}}$ at ${\mathfrak{m}}\in\text{Span}(R)$ can be defined as 

\begin{equation}
  \hat{\cal F}_{_{\mathfrak{m}}}\ \overset{def.}{=}\ \hat f_{_{\mathfrak{m}}}- \hat f_{_{\mathfrak{m}}}(0)-\lambda_{_1}\log\hat g_{_{1,m}}-...-\lambda_{_r}\hat g_{_{r,m}} \ \ \ \in\ \ \ \hat{\mathfrak{m}}.
\end{equation}

Then, the dg-category of MF of Givental type potential ${\cal F}$ is defined as  

\begin{equation} 
\text{Br}({\cal F})\ \overset{def.}{=}\ \prod_{_{\mathfrak{m}\in \text{Crit}({\cal F})}}\text{MF}(\hat{\cal F}_{_{\mathfrak{m}}}).  
\end{equation}


\section{Kapustin-Orlov branes}        


\subsection{Coisotropic branes} 

We start by introducing the notion of \emph{coisotropic branes}, namely coisotropic objects in Fuk$(X,\omega)$,  \cite{4},

\begin{equation}
Y\ \subset\ (X,\omega).  
\end{equation}     

The simplest example is 

\begin{equation}  
\mathbb{C}^{^n}\ =\ \mathbb{R}^{^n}\oplus i\mathbb{R}^{^n}\ \ \ , \ \ \ \omega  
\end{equation}   
with standard isotropic structure.  

Take  

\begin{equation}  
\mathbb{R}^{^n}\oplus i\mathbb{R}^{^k}\ \ \ \subset \ \ \ \mathbb{C}^{^n}.    
\end{equation} 

If $k=0$, it is half-dimensional, thereby implying it is actually Lagrangian. Hence, a co-isotropic one is such that it can take intermediate values in dimension, satisfying the following condition:


\begin{itemize}  

\item  The restriction of $\omega$ to $Y$ has a constant rank.

\item  The commutator of any two vector fields in ${\cal L} Y$ also belongs to ${\cal L} Y$, with ${\cal L} Y$ denoting the tangent bundle of the foliation.

\end{itemize} 

From the data above, one can define the normal bundle of the foliation as the following quotient  


\begin{equation}  
{\cal F}\ = \ T_{_Y}/{\cal L} Y.  
\end{equation}  

Interpreting $Y$ as a first-class constraint surface in a phase space of a mechanical system, the meaning of the above foliation can be understood as follows: First-class constraints lead to gauge symmetries. A leaf in $Y$ is precisely an orbit of a point under all gauge transformations. 

Formally, the reduced phase space, $Y_{_{\text{red}}}$, describing gauge-invariant degrees of freedom, is the quotient of $Y$ by the aforementioned gauge transformations. Equivalently, $Y_{_{\text{red}}}$ is the space of leaves of the foliation\footnote{However, this space might not be a manifold, in particular, it might not even be a Hausdorff topological space. In general, it is unclear how to define the dynamics on $Y_{_{\text{red}}}$.}.

\underline{Additional structure:}  
$\nabla$ is a U(1)-connection on $Y$, whose curvature 2-form is  

\begin{equation}  
F\ =\ \frac{1}{2\pi i}\text{Curv}(\nabla),      
\end{equation}   
such that 

\begin{equation}  
F^{^b}:\ T_{_Y}\ \longrightarrow\ T_{_Y}^{^*}   
\end{equation}   
is required to vanish on the characteristic foliation, implying it is automatically integrable as a foliation. The characteristic foliation will be  

\begin{equation}  
\text{Char}\ =\ \mathbb{R}^{^{n+k}}\ \ \ \subset\ \ \ \mathbb{R}^{^n}\oplus i\mathbb{R}^{^k}.    
\end{equation}   

On the complement part, 

\begin{equation}  
\omega^{^{-1}}F:\ T_{_Y}/\text{Char}\ \longrightarrow\ T_{_Y}/\text{Char}.  
\end{equation}

This is an almost complex structure, meaning  

\begin{equation}  
\left(\omega^{^{-1}}F\right)^{^2}\ =\ -1.  
\end{equation} 

There are two conditions imposed on the 2-form. The underlying geometric structure is a coisotropic submanifold. On the character part (the Lagrangian part) it is a flat connection.

\subsection{Fukaya category with a B-field}  

The effect of a $B$-field on $D^{^b}(X)$ results in the fact that the sheaf ${\cal O}_{_X}$ is replaced by a certain sheaf of noncommutative algebras. Importantly, in String Theory, the $B$-field makes the D-brane worldvolume noncommutative.  

However, the effect of the $B$-field on the Fukaya category is quite different. For a 2D cymplectic manifold $(X,\omega)$, fix the almost complex structure $I$ on $X$ to be compatible with $\omega$, and thereby obtain a Hermitian metric on $X$. An object in the Fukaya category is a triple $(L,E,\nabla)$, where $L$ is a Lagrangian submanifold, $E$ is a trivial unitary vector bundle on $L$, $\nabla$ is a flat connection on $E$.    

Physically, this data is enough to specify the A-type boundary conditions for the classical $\sigma$-model, defining an A-type D-brane.

In this case, an object in the Fukaya category is defined as follows  

\begin{equation}  
\left(L,i,E,\nabla\right),  
\end{equation}
where $E$ is a complex vector bundle on $L$, $\nabla$ is a connection on $E$ such that 

\begin{equation}  
F_{_{\nabla}}\ =\ 2\pi i\ d_{_E}B\bigg|_{_L}.     
\end{equation}   

If $B=0$, then $F_{_{\nabla}}=0$. In the absence of the $B$-field, any oair $(L,i)$ can be extended to an object of the Fukaya category. The situation is more complicated for $B\neq 0$. The original HMS correspondence required $E$ to be a unitary connection. Nevertheless, this condition is too strong. Even in the case of the elliptic curve, one has to allow for non-unitary connections on the A-side if one wants to account for all bundles on the B-side.

\subsubsection*{Morphisms in the modified Fukaya category}  

Let    

\begin{equation}  
{\cal U}_{_1}\ = \ \left(L_{_1},i_{_1}, E_{_1},\nabla_{_1}\right)\ \ \ \ \ \ ,\ \ \ \ \ \ {\cal U}_{_2}\ = \ \left(L_{_2},i_{_2}, E_{_1},\nabla_{_1}\right),       
\end{equation}  
be two objects, such that $L_{_1}, L_{_2}$ intersect transversally. Morphisms from ${\cal U}_{_1}$ to ${\cal U}_{_2}$ in $Fuk(X)$ form a complex of vector spaces defined by the rule   

\begin{equation}   
\text{Hom}\left({\cal U}_{_1},{\cal U}_{_2}\right)\ = \ \bigoplus_{_{x\in L_{_1}\cap L_{_2}}}\text{Hom}\left(E_{_1}|_{_x}, E_{_2}|_{_{x}}\right).  
\label{eq:newvectsp}  
\end{equation}

\eqref{eq:newvectsp} is graded in the following way: for each point $x\ \in\ L_{_1}\cap L_{_2}$, there are two points $i_{_1}(x)$ and $i_{_2}(x)$ on the universal cover of the Lagrangian Grassmannian of $T_{_x}X$. To these two points, we can associate an integer

\begin{equation}  
\mu\left(i_{_1}(x), i_{_2}(x)\right),  
\end{equation}   
constituting the Maslov index. By definition, ${Hom}^{^i}\left(E_{_1}|_{_x}, E_{_2}|_{_x}\right)$ has a $\mu\left(i_{_1}(x), i_{_2}(x)\right)$-grading.     


The differential on \eqref{eq:newvectsp} is defined by the rule  

\begin{equation}  
d(u)\ =\ \sum_{_{z\in L_{_1}\cap L_{_2}}} m_{_1}(u;z),      
\end{equation}  
where   

\begin{equation}  
u\ \in\ \text{Hom}\left(E_{_1}|_{_x}, E_{_2}|_{_x}\right),
\end{equation}  
and  

\begin{equation}  
 m_{_1}(u;z)\ \in\ \text{Hom}\left(E_{_1}|_{_z}, E_{_2}|_{_z}\right)
 \end{equation}  
 is given by    

 \begin{equation}  
 m_{_1}(u;z)\ =\ \sum_{_{\phi: D\rightarrow X}}\pm \exp\left(2\pi i\int_{_D}\phi^{^*}(-B+i\omega)\right)\ {\cal P}\ \exp\left(\oint_{_{\partial D}}\phi^{^*}\nabla\right), 
 \label{eq:pathoorder}    
 \end{equation}  
 where $\phi$ is an (anti)-holomorphic map from the disk  

 \begin{equation}  
 D\ =\ \{|w|\le 1, w\in\mathbb{C}\}  
 \end{equation}   
 to $X$, such that   

 \begin{equation}  
 \phi(-1)\ =\ x \ \ \ \ , \ \ \ \ \phi(1)\ = \ z  
 \end{equation}   
 and    

  \begin{equation}  
 \phi([x,z])\ \subset\ L_{_2} \ \ \ \ , \ \ \ \ \phi([z,x])\ \subset \ L_{_1}.  
 \end{equation}    

 The path-ordered integral featuring in \eqref{eq:pathoorder} is defined by the following rule  

 \begin{equation}  
 {\cal P}\  \exp\left(\oint_{_{\partial D}}\phi^{^*}\nabla\right)\ =\  {\cal P}\  \exp\left(\int_{_x}^{^z}\phi^{^*}\nabla_{_2}\right)\ u\  {\cal P}\  \exp\left(\int_{_z}^{^x}\phi^{^*}\nabla_{_1}\right).  
 \end{equation}     



   \section{Diffeomorphism classes of CY varieties}    

A longstanding problem in Geometry is the classification of geometric objects up to isomorphisms. 

The minimal model (explain what this is). 

In 1D, CY curves are genus-1 curves. In 2D, CY curves are K3-surfaces (not projective). In either case are they isomorphic. K3-surfaces are particularly important for Algebraic and Complex Geometry. 

In higher D, the classification of CY manifolds is quite hard and many questions in the topological setting are still to be addressed.  For example, the topology of CY manifolds of complex dimension greater or equal to 3 is not uniquely determined. 

In this section, we will be focusing on simply-connected CY threefolds. Importantly, their diffeomorphism type is completely determined by:  

\begin{itemize}  

\item The Hodge data.

\item The triple intersection in cohomology.

\item The 2$^{^{nd}}$ Chern class.



\end{itemize}

We will be explaining each one of them in turn. 

\underline{Example} The best example of a CY threefold is the smooth \emph{quintic} hypersurface in projective space $\mathbb{P}^{^4}$, which is defined by a homogeneous polynomial of degree 5\footnote{$\mathbb{P}^{^4}$ is a particular example of a smooth toric Fano variety, playing a fundamental role in the construction of examples of CY manfolds. As we shall see, a toric Fano manifold ensures there always exists a codimension-1 submanifold that is CY.}.

     \subsection{Preliminary definitions}   

   \underline{Homeomorphic:} An isomorphism in the category of topological spaces, $\textbf{Top}$, namely a homeomorphism 

   \begin{equation}   
   f:X\ \rightarrow\ Y  
   \end{equation}
is a continuous map of topological spaces such that there is an inverse 

\begin{equation}  
f^{^{-1}}:\ Y\ \rightarrow\ X  
\end{equation}  
that is also a continuous map of topological spaces.   

   \underline{Diffeomorphism:} Given two $k$-times differentiable manifolds (or smooth manifolds), a diffeomorphism 

   \begin{equation}  
   f:\ X\ \rightarrow\ Y  
   \end{equation}

is a differentiable function such that there exists an inverse differentiable function $f^{^{-1}}$. 

Relation to homeomorphisms: Every differomorphism is a homeomorphism, but the converse, in general, is not true.

   \underline{Isomorphism:} Generalising the concept of bijection. It is an invertible morphism, hence a morphism with an inverse morphism.   

      \underline{Examples of isomorphisms:} 
      \begin{enumerate} 

      \item A bijection is an isomorphism in \textbf{Set}.

      \item A homeomorphism is an isomorphism in \textbf{Top}.

      \item A diffeomorphism is an isomorphism in \textbf{Diff}.

      \end{enumerate}


   \underline{Divisor:} In Algebraic Geometry, a divisor in a given variety is a formal linear combination of sub-varieties of codimension-1   

   \begin{equation}  
   D\ =\ \sum_{_i}n_{_i}D_{_i}  
   \end{equation}
   on $X$ induces a short exact sequence of abelian sheaves on $X$ of the form 

   \begin{equation}  
   0\ \rightarrow\ {\cal O}_{_X}^{^{\times}}\ \rightarrow\ {\cal O}_{_X}\left(*Y\right)^{^{\times}}\ \overset{\nu_{_Y}}{\rightarrow}\ (\nu_{_Y})_{_*}\mathbb{Z}_{_{\tilde Y}}\ \rightarrow\ 0,
   \end{equation}
   where $Y$ is the \emph{support} of $D$, namely  


   \begin{equation}    
   Y\ \overset{def.}{=}\ \bigcup_{_{i|n_{_i}\neq0}}D_{_i}  
   \end{equation}  
   and    

   \begin{equation}  
   \nu_{_Y}:\ \tilde Y\ \longrightarrow\ Y  
   \end{equation}
   is its \emph{normalisation} in $Y$, corresponding to the map 

   \begin{equation}  
   \delta:\ H^{^0}\left(\tilde Y,\mathbb{Z}\right)\ \longrightarrow\ H^{^1}\left(X,{\cal O}_{_X}^{^{\times}}\right). 
   \end{equation}

 \underline{Canonical Divisor:} The divisor corresponding to the canonical line bundle of an algebraic variety. Similarly, its \emph{anticanonical divisor} is the additive inverse, corresponding to the anticanonical line bundle.

 \underline{Canonical Line Bundle:} For $X$ a complex manifold over the complex numbers, the K$\ddot{\text{a}}$hler differential forms are holomorphic forms. For example, the canonical bundle for dim$_{_{\mathbb{C}}}(X)\ \equiv n$ is $\Omega^{^{n,0}}$, a complex line bundle.  

\underline{(Very) ample divisor:} An integral divisor $D$ is very ample if $\phi: X\ \rightarrow \ \mathbb{P}^{^n}$ defines an embedding of $X$. $D$ is ample if $mD$, for $m\in\mathbb{N}$, is very ample.

 \underline{Toric varieties:}  Irreducible varieties containing an algebraic torus as an open dense subset. An $n$-dimensional toroc variety is a veriety, $X$, containing an open dense subset isomorphic to 

 \begin{equation}  
 T\ =\ \text{Spec}_{_{\mathbb{C}}}\left[T_{_1}^{^{\pm 1}},...,T_{_n}^{^{\pm 1}}\right],
 \end{equation}
an affine variety equipped with an algebraic action  

\begin{equation}  
T\ \times\ X\ \rightarrow\ X  
\end{equation} 
restricting to the multiplication action  

\begin{equation} 
T\ \times\ T\ \rightarrow\ T  
\end{equation} 
implying a toric variety is essentially an equivariant enlargement of a torus.

      \subsection{CY}  


      Let $X$ be a complex manifold, $T_{_X}$ its holomorphic tangent bundle. $X$ is a CY of dimension $n$ if it is a projective manifold with trivial canonical bundle and without holomorphic $p$-forms, i.e.  

      \begin{equation}  
      K_{_X}\ \equiv\ \Omega_{_X}^{^p}\ \simeq\ {\cal O}_{_X}\ \ \ \ ,\ \ \ \ H^{^0}(X,\Omega_{_X}^{^p})\ =\ 0\ \ \ \ , \ \ \ \ p\ \in\ [0,n].
      \end{equation}

      If dim$_{_{\mathbb{C}}}X=3\ \Rightarrow\ \Omega_{_X}^{^3}\ \simeq\ {\cal O}_{_X}$. 
      The \emph{Hodge numbers} are defined as follows  

      \begin{equation}  
      h^{^{i,j}}(X)\ \overset{def.}{=}\ \text{dim}_{_{\mathbb C}}H^{^j}\left(X, \Omega_{_X}^{^i}\right).  
      \end{equation}

      By renaming 
 \begin{equation}  
      h^{^{1,1}}(X)\ \overset{def.}{=}\ a\ \ \ \ , \ \ \ \ h^{^{1,2}}(X)\ \overset{def.}{=}\ b
      \end{equation}  
      from which to build a \emph{Hodge diamond}  

      \begin{equation}  
      \begin{aligned}   
      &\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ 1\\
      &\ \ \ \ \ \ 0\ \ \ \ \ 0\\
      &\ \ \ 0\ \ \ \ \ a\ \ \ \ \ 0\\
      & 1\ \ \ \ \ b\ \ \ \ \ b\ \ \ \ \ 1\\
      &\ \ \ 0\ \ \ \ \ a\ \ \ \ \ 0\\
      &\ \ \ \ \ \ 0\ \ \ \ \ \ 0\\
      &\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ 1\\
      \end{aligned} 
      \label{eq:HD}   
      \end{equation}

      \eqref{eq:HD} shows that the topological type of CY manifold is not uniquely determined for 3 complex-dimensional manifolds. If $X_{_1}, X_{_2}$ are CY threefolds with different $a, b$, they are not homeomorphic. If they are the same, they are diffeomorphic. 


         \subsection{Toric Fano Manifolds}  

        A Fano manifold is a projective manifold, $F$, such that $-K_{_X}$ is ample. If $F$ is toric, then $-K_{_X}$ is very ample. From Bertini's Theorem, this implies that the generic section of ${\cal O}_{_F}(-K_{_F})$ gives a smooth connected hypersurface $X\ \subseteq\ F$, such that $X\ \in\ |-K_{_F}|$, implying $X$ is a smooth CY variety. 

        Importantly, this implies that, once we have a toric Fano manifold, then there always exists a submanifold of codimension-1 that is a CY manifold. 
        Hence, if $F$ is 4 (complex)-dimensional, once can construct a 3D CY by means of the procedure outlined above.

        \underline{Example}  
        The projective space $\mathbb{P}^{^4}$ is a smooth toric Fano manifold of dimension 4. The general quintic hypersurface is a smooth CY threefold. In such case, $a=1, b=101$.    


