               \subsection{Diffeomorphic 3D CY varieties} 

               In 1966, C.T.C. Wall described the invariants that determine the diffeomorphism type of closed, smooth, simply-connected 6-manifolds with torsion-free cohomology.  
               \medskip 

               \underline{Theorem [Wall]} 
               \medskip 

               Diffeomorphism classes of simply-connected, spin, oriented, closed 6-manifolds, $X$, with torsion-free cohomology correspond bijectively to isomorphism classes of systems of invariants consisting of  

               \begin{enumerate} 

               \item Free abelian groups, $H^{^2}(X,\mathbb{Z}), H^{^3}(X,\mathbb{Z})$.

               \item A symmetric trilinear form 

               \begin{equation}  
               \mu:\ H^{^2}(X,\mathbb{Z})^{^{\otimes 3}}\ \rightarrow\ H^{^6}(X,\mathbb{Z})\ \simeq\ \mathbb{Z},  
               \end{equation}
               defined as follows  

                \begin{equation}  
               \mu(x,y,z)\ \overset{def.}{=}\ x\ \cup\ y\ \cup z,  
               \label{eq:mumap}
               \end{equation}   
               with $x, y, z\ \in \ H^{^2}(X,\mathbb{Z})$.

               \item  A linear map    


                \begin{equation}  
               p_{_1}:\ H^{^2}(X,\mathbb{Z})\ \rightarrow\ H^{^6}(X,\mathbb{Z})\ \simeq\ \mathbb{Z},  
               \end{equation} 
               where   

                \begin{equation}  
                p_{_1}(x)\ \overset{def.}{=}\ p_{_1}(X)\ \cup\ x.  
                \label{eq:p1}
               \end{equation}

               \end{enumerate}  


               \eqref{eq:mumap} and \eqref{eq:p1} are subject to the following constraints  

               \begin{equation}  
               \boxed{\ \ \ \ \mu(x,x,y)\ +\ \mu(x,y,y)\ =\ 0\ \ \ (\text{mod}\ 2)\ \ \ \ , \ \ \ \ 4\mu(x,x,x)\ -\ p_{_1}(X)\ =\ 0\ \ \ (\text{mod}\ 24).\  \color{white}\bigg]\ \color{black}}  
               \label{eq:constr}
               \end{equation}  

               \medskip   

               \underline{Theorem}  

               \medskip  

               Let $(X,H)$ be a very ample polarised CY threefold, i.e. $x=H$ is a very ample divisor on $X$. Then, the following inequalities hold: 

               \begin{equation}    
               -36\mu(x,x,x)\ -\ 80\ \le\ \frac{c_{_3}(X)}{2}\ =\ h^{^{1,1}}(X)- h^{^{1,2}}(X)\ \le\ 6\mu(x,x,x)\ +\ 40.  
               \label{eq:constraintmp}
               \end{equation}  

               If $X$ is a CY threefold, then 

               \begin{equation}  
               p_{_1}(X)\ =\ -2c_{_2}(X)\ \in\ H^{^4}(X,\mathbb{Z})  
               \end{equation}  
               and  

               \begin{equation}   
               \int_{_X}c_{_3}(X)\ =\ \chi(X)\ =\ \sum_{_{i=0}}^{^6}\ \text{dim}\ H^{^i}(X, \mathbb{R})\ =\ 2h^{^{1,1}}(X)-2 h^{^{1,2}}.  
               \end{equation}  

               \medskip    

               \underline{Remark}  

               \medskip  

               By Wall's Theorem, if $X$ is simply-connected, spin, oriented, closed 6-manifold with torsion-free cohomology, then the diffeomorphism class is determined by the free abelian groups $H^{^2}(X,\mathbb{Z}), H^{^3}(X,\mathbb{Z})$ and the form $\mu$ and $p_{_1}$.  

               If $X$ is CY, $\mu$ and $p_{_1}$ need to satisfy \eqref{eq:constraintmp} outlined above. This is a necessary, but not sufficient condition for $X$ to be CY.

               In particular, let $X_{_1}, X_{_2}$ be simply-connected CY threefolds with torsion-free cohomology, and the same Hodge numbers  

               \begin{equation}  
               h^{^{1,1}}(X)\ =\ a\ \ \ \ ,\ \ \ \ h^{^{1,2}}(X)\ =\ b.  
               \end{equation}   
               To be diffeomorphic, $\mu, p_{_1}$ should be the same, and satisfy \eqref{eq:constraintmp}.  
\medskip

               \underline{Corollary}  
               \medskip  

               Let $X$ be a CY threefold, with torsion-free cohomology, and  

               \begin{equation}  
               h^{^{1,1}}(X)\ =\ 1\ \ \ \ , \ \ \ \ h^{^{1,2}}(X)\ =\ h^{^{2,1}}(X)\ =\ b\ \ \ ,\ \ \ b\ \in \ \mathbb{N}.  
               \end{equation}


               Then, 

               \begin{equation}   
               H^{^2}(X,\mathbb{Z})\ \simeq\ \mathbb{Z}\ \ \ \ ,\ \ \ \ H^{^3}(X,\mathbb{Z})\ \simeq\ \mathbb{Z}^{^{2+b}}.  
               \end{equation} 

               Fix a generator, $H\ \in H^{^2}(X,\mathbb{Z})$, and $\mu(H,H,H)=m\ \in\ \mathbb{Z}$, then  

               \begin{equation}  
               m\ \ge\ \frac{b-81}{36}.  
               \end{equation}  

               \underline{Example}  
               If $m=5$, then $b\ \le\ 261$. For $b=101$, we recover the generic quintic threefold in $\mathbb{P}^{^4}$.


