\subsection{Generalised Theta functions}    

$\theta$-functions are particularly useful as a bases for the B-side homsets. They descend to holomorphic sections of line bundles on $E_{_{\tau}}$, and provide a bases for our B-side homsets.  

The generalised $\theta$-function reads as follows  


\begin{equation}    
\Theta[a,z_{_o}](\tau,z)\ = \ \sum_{_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}}e^{^{\pi i[(m+a)^{^2}\tau+2(m+a)(z+z_{_o})]}}.     
\end{equation}   

Thinking of $\theta$ functions as as line bundle homomorphisms, we can define compositions on the B-side by making use of the following addition formula:  


\begin{equation}   
\begin{aligned}    
&\Theta\left[\frac{a}{n_{_1}},0\right]\left(n_{_1}\tau,z_{_1}\right)\ \Theta\left[\frac{b}{n_{_2}},0\right]\left(n_{_2}\tau,z_{_2}\right)\ =\\   
&=\ \sum_{_{j\in\mathbb{Z}/k\mathbb{Z}}}\Theta\left[\frac{c_{_j}}{k},0\right]\left(k\tau,z_{_1}+z_{_2}\right)\ \Theta\left[\frac{n_{_2}c_{_j}-kb}{n_{_1}n_{_2}k},0\right]\left(n_{_1}n_{_2}k\tau,n_{_2}z_{_1}-n_{_1}z_{_2}\right),       
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}  
for $a,b\in\mathbb{Q}, n_{_1},n_{_2}\in\mathbb{Z}_{_{\ge 1}}, k=n_{_1}+n_{_2}, c_{_j}=j n_{_1}+a+b$.


