\subsubsection{The Fukaya-Kontsevich category}  

Given two Lagrangians and a morphism in between them,   

\begin{equation}  
(L,\alpha, M)\ \longrightarrow\ \left(L^{^{\prime}},\alpha^{^{\prime}}, M^{^{\prime}}\right)   
\end{equation}   
the morphisms in the graded Fukaya category read as follows    

\begin{equation}
    \text{Hom}_{_{{\text{Fuk}}^{^0}\left(E^{^{\tau}}\right)}}\left({\cal L},{\cal L}^{^{\prime}}\right)\ = \ \begin{cases}  
    &\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ 0\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \  \ \  \ \text{if}\ \ \alpha^{^{\prime}}-\alpha\ \notin\ [0,1)\\   
    &\\    
    &\underset{p\in L\cap L^{^{\prime}}}{\bigoplus}\text{Hom}\left(M_{_p},M_{_p}^{^{\prime}}\right)\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \text{if}\ \ \alpha^{^{\prime}}-\alpha\ \in\ [0,1).\\    
    \end{cases}    
\end{equation}


\medskip 


\underline{Lemma}    


\medskip    

For ${\cal L}_{_1}, {\cal L}_{_2}$ objects in $\text{Fuk}^{^0}\left(E^{^{\tau}}\right)$, there exists a canonical isomorphism  


\begin{equation}   
\text{Hom}\left({\cal L}_{_1},{\cal L}_{_2}[1]\right)\ \simeq \ \text{Hom}\left({\cal L}_{_2}, {\cal L}_{_1}\right)^{^*}.  
\end{equation}    

$\text{Fuk}^{^0}\left(E^{^{\tau}}\right)$ is a pre-additive category, however, $D^{^b}\left(E_{_{\tau}}\right)$ is additive. 

To recover a categorical equivalence with the B-side, we need to introduce the Fukaya-Kontsevich category, ${\cal FK}^{^0}\left(E^{^{\tau}}\right)$, defined as the 0$^{^{th}}$-cohomology of the Fukaya category on the elliptic curve  

\begin{equation}   
{\cal FK}^{^0}\left(E^{^{\tau}}\right)\ \overset{def.}{=}\ H^{^0}\left(\text{Fuk}\left(E^{^{\tau}}\right) \right).  
\end{equation}    

Unlike previous examples we have discussed so far, the elliptic curve provides a first example where the $A_{_{\infty}}$-structure can be relaxed for the correspondence to hold.   
Notice that the HMS correspondence might have been recovered in an alternative way, namely by equipping $D^{^b}\left(E_{_{\tau}}\right)$ with an $A_{_{\infty}}$-structure. Albeit such direction has been explored by other authors in the literature, it is still far from complete due to their analysis being restricted to the transversally-intersecting cases alone.

For the case of the torus, $M=E_{_{\tau}}$, is an elliptic curve with modular parameter $\tau$, and $\tilde M=\tilde E^{^{\rho}}$ a torus with complexified K$\ddot{\text{a}}$hler parameter    

\begin{equation}   
\rho\ = \ iA+b,     
\end{equation}  
where $A$ denotes the area, and $b$ defines a class in  

\begin{equation}  
H^{^2}\left(\tilde M;\mathbb{R}\right)\ /\ H^{^2}\left(\tilde M;\mathbb{Z}\right).     
\end{equation}  

The mirror map therefore reads   

\begin{equation}  
\rho\ \longleftrightarrow\ \tau.  
\end{equation}   

Generalising this result to higher-dimensional CY manifolds is still an open question, the reason being that the Fukaya category composition depends on the K$\ddot{\text{a}}$hler form, i.e. on the unique CY metric.  

The exact form on this metric is yet unkown. Abelian varieties are simplest extension of the case of the elliptic curve.


\subsection{The mirror dual}  

Local sections of a vector bundle are an example of a coherent sheaf.  


\medskip   


\underline{Lemma}  

\medskip  

Let ${\cal F},{\cal G}$ be coherent sheaves on $E_{_{\tau}}$; then there exists an isomorphism   


\begin{equation}   
\text{Hom}({\cal F},{\cal G})\ \simeq\ \text{Ext}^{^1} ({\cal G},{\cal F})^{^*}.     
\end{equation}   

For any pair objects of an abelian category, $A, B$,   


\begin{equation}   
\text{Hom}_{_{\cal DA}}(A[m], B[n])\ \simeq\ \text{Hom}_{_{\cal DA}}(A, B[m-n])\ \simeq\ \text{Ext}^{^{m-n}}(A,B)   
\end{equation}     


\begin{equation}   
\text{Hom}_{_{D^{^b}(E_{_{\tau}})}}(A, B[1])\ =\ \text{Ext}^{^1}(A,B)   \ =\ \text{Hom}(B,A)^{^*}
\label{eq:ext1}
\end{equation}    

\begin{equation}   
\text{Hom}_{_{D^{^b}(E_{_{\tau}})}}(A, B)\ =\ \text{Ext}^{^0}(A,B)   \ =\ \text{Hom}(A,B).
\label{eq:ext0}
\end{equation}   

The last equality in \eqref{eq:ext1} and \eqref{eq:ext0} follows from the following

\medskip   


\underline{Lemma}  

\medskip    

Let $A_{_1},A_{_2}$ be two coherent sheaves on the elliptic curve, $E_{_{\tau}}$, then  

\begin{equation}   
\text{Ext}^{^1}\left(A_{_2},A_{_1}\right)\ \simeq\ \text{Hom}\left(A_{_1},A_{_2}\right)^{^*}.  
\end{equation}   

\medskip   


\underline{Proof}  

\medskip    

Let $A_{_2}$ be an arbitrary coherent sheaf. $E_{_{\tau}}$ being smooth projective implies there exists a short-exact sequence   

\begin{equation}  
0\ \longrightarrow\ F^{^{\prime}}\ \longrightarrow\ A_{_2}\ \longrightarrow\ 0  
\end{equation}   
with locally-free sheaves $F$ and $F^{^{\prime}}$. By applying the covariant functors Ext$^{^1}(A_{_1},.)$ and Hom$(.,A_{_1})^{^*}$ we obtain the following diagram   

\begin{equation}   
\begin{aligned}  
&\text{Ext}^{^1}\left(A_{_1},F^{^{\prime}}\right)\ \longrightarrow\ \text{Ext}^{^1}\left(A_{_1},F\right)\ \longrightarrow\ \text{Ext}^{^1}\left(A_{_1},A_{_2}\right)\ \longrightarrow\ 0   \\
&\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \simeq\ \ \downarrow\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \simeq\ \ \downarrow \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \downarrow\\
&\text{Hom}\left(F^{^{\prime}},A_{_1}\right)^{^*}\ \longrightarrow\ \text{Hom}\left(F,A_{_1}\right)^{^*}\ \longrightarrow\ \text{Hom}\left(A_{_2},A_{_1}\right)^{^*}\ \longrightarrow\ 0   \\    
\end{aligned}  
\label{eq:diagramlemma}
\end{equation}  

The Lemma follows from commutativity of the diagram \eqref{eq:diagramlemma}, in turn, due to Serre duality.   

\medskip     


\underline{Lemma}  

\medskip 

\begin{equation}   
\text{Ext}^{^k}(A,B)\ = \ 0  \ \ \  \ \ \ \ \ \text{if}\ \ k\neq 0,1  
\end{equation}  
for $A,B$ coherent sheaves on a Riemann surface.     

\medskip 

\medskip    


In general, for a complex $n$-dimensional manifold, the Ext$^{^k}$-functor is zero for $k>n$.

\subsection{The equivalence}

We now turn to outlining the proof of the main theorem in [Polishchuk, Zaslow] and [Kreussler], namely that   

\begin{equation}  
\boxed{\ \ \ \Phi_{_{\tau}}:\ D^{^b}\left(E_{_{\tau}}\right)\ \longrightarrow\ {\cal FK}^{^0}\left(E^{^{\tau}}\right)   \color{white}\bigg]\ \ }
\end{equation}    
is an equivalence of additive categories compatible with the shift functor.   


\medskip   

\underline{Proof}  

\medskip   

Replacing $D^{^b}\left(E_{_{\tau}}\right)$ by the equivalent subcategory whose objects are finite direct sums of shifted indecomposable coherent sheaves on $E_{_{\tau}}$.    


$\Phi_{_{\tau}}$ can be defined on objects by knowing how it acts on $A=A[0]$ (an indecomposable coherent sheaf on $E_{_{\tau}}$). Defining $A$ as a tensor product of line bundles with unipotent bundle on $E_{_{\tau}}$. By means of Theorem 2.6, they prove that $\Phi_{_{r\tau}}(A)$ can be, in turn, extended to all indecomposable vector bundles. The definition of $\Phi_{_{\tau}}(A)$ thereby involves elements common to the SYZ description, with   

\begin{equation}  
