\subsubsection{The Chekarov Torus}    

Taken from "An introduction to the Chekarov torus" (JOe' Brendel), 2021

The Chekarov torus in $\mathbb{C}^{^2}$ is the first example of a Lagrangian torus in $(\mathbb{R}^{^4},\omega_{_o})$ that cannot be mapped to a product torus by symplectomorphisms. Highlighting the relationship with Hamiltonian torus actions and symplectic reduction. 

\subsubsection*{Construction of the Chekarov Torus in $\mathbb{R}^{^4}$}    

One key element is the Hamiltonian   

\begin{equation}  
\begin{aligned}
&F:\ \ \ \ \mathbb{C}^{^2}\ \ \ \longrightarrow\ \ \ \ \ \ \mathbb{R} \\   
&\\ 
&\ \  \ \ \ \ (z_{_1},z_{_2})\ \mapsto\ \pi\left(|z_{_1}|^{^2}-|z_{_1}|^{^2}\right)    
\end{aligned}  
\end{equation}  
inducing the following Hamiltonian circle action  

\begin{equation}  
\psi_{_{\theta}}(z_{_1},z_{_2})\ = \ \left(e^{^{2\pi i\theta}}z_{_1}, e^{^{-2\pi i\theta}}z_{_2}\right)  \ \ \ \ ,\ \ \ \ \theta\ \in\ S^{^1}\ =\ \mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}
\end{equation}    

Level sets of $F$ are  

\begin{equation}  
Z_{_C}\ \overset{def.}{=}\ F^{^{-1}}(c)\  \ \ ,\ \ \ \forall\ c\ \in\ \mathbb{R}.  
\end{equation}   

For $c\neq0$, $Z_{_C}$ is smooth, implying it is diffeomorphic to $S^{^1}\times\mathbb{C}$, the only critical value being $c=0$, with $0\in\mathbb{C}^{^2}$ being the corresponding critical point.     


