The moment map of the standard toric structure on $\mathbb{C}^{^2}$ is  

\begin{equation}  
\begin{aligned}
\mu:\ &\ \ \ \mathbb{C}^{^2}\ \ \ \longrightarrow\ \ \ \ \ \ \mathbb{R}_{_{\ge 0}}^{^2} \\  
&(z_{_1},z_{_2})\ \mapsto\ \left(\pi|z_{_1}|^{^2},\pi|z_{-2}|^{^2}\right)
\end{aligned}  
\end{equation}   

The Chekarov torus is obtained by lifting circles from the quotient spaces $M_{_C}$ to tori in $\mathbb{C}^{^2}$ via a 1-parameter family of quotients\footnote{That is not well-defined for $c=0$.}  

\begin{equation}  
(\mathbb{C},\omega_{_o})\ \simeq\ (M_{_c},\omega_{_c})\ \xleftarrow{\ \ \pi_{_c}\ \ }\ Z_{_c}\ =\ F^{^{-1}}(c)\ \hookrightarrow\ \left(\mathbb{C}^{^2}, \omega_{_o}\right) . 
\end{equation}  

Circles in the plane are Lagrangian, and therefore, their corresponding tori are automatically Lagrangian. The Chekarov torus of area parameter $a>0$ is defined as the lift  

\begin{equation}  
T_{_{\text{Ch}}}(a)\ =\ \pi_{_o}^{^{-1}}\left(S_{_{\text{Ch}}}^{^1}(a)\right)\ \subset\ \mathbb{C}^{^2}  
\end{equation}    
importantly, there are two kinds of circles in the reduced space $M_{_o}=\mathbb{C}$, those of standard type $S^{^1}(a)$ (enclosing the origin) and those of Chekarov type $S_{_{\text{Ch}}}^{^1}(a)$ (not enclosing the origin). Both types come in a 1-parameter family indexed by the area they contain. A smooth circle that passes through the origin lifts to a pinched Lagrangian torus in $\mathbb{C}^{^2}$.    

