\section{Mirror Symmetry for Abelian Varieties}    



In physical terms, HMS is a correspondence in between A- and B-type D-branes. These are equipped with an $A_{_{\infty}}$-category structure, encoding the properties of correlation functions in Topological Open String Theory.  

For  

\begin{equation}  
\text{dim}_{_{\mathbb{C}}}X\ =\ \text{dim}_{_{\mathbb{C}}}\hat X\ =\ 1,  
\end{equation}  
namely for the elliptic curve, the corresponding ${\cal N}=2$ SCVA is known, and therefore one knows the precise conditions under which $(X, G, B)$ is mirror to $(X^{^{\prime}},G^{^{\prime}}, B^{^{\prime}})$. Furthermore, all objects and morphisms in the Fukaya category can be explicitly described. 

For the case of algebraic tori of $\text{dim}_{_{\mathbb{C}}}>1$, the original HMS correspondence by Kontsevich does not hold in general anymore. The main reason is that, both, $D^{^b}$Coh and $D^{^b}$Fuk do not depend on the $B$-field, whereas for Physicists' formulation of Mirror Symmetry, the $B$-field plays an essential role. This requires a certain modification of the HMS correspondence.    







\subsection{Functors between categories of coherent sheaves}   

Take $X$ an algebraic variety over an arbitrary field $\mathbb{k}$. ${\cal O}_{_X}$ the structure sheaf on $X$. Coh$(X)$ the category of (quasi)-coherent sheaves on $X$, namely admitting a finite presentation in terms of ${\cal O}_{_X}$-modules.     

Turning to derived categories of Coh$(X)$, $D^{^b}(X)$, they admit a triangulated categorical structure, equipped with the following  properties:  

\begin{enumerate}   

\item  There is a translation functor  

\begin{equation}  
[1]:\ D\ \longrightarrow\ D    
\end{equation}  
that is an additive autoequivalence.  

\item  A class of distinguished (or exact) triangles  

\begin{equation}  
X\ \xrightarrow{u}\ Y\ \xrightarrow{v}\ Z\ \xrightarrow{w}\ X[1]    
\end{equation}   
such that certain axioms are satisfied.    

\end{enumerate}    

An additive functor between categories   

\begin{equation}  
F:\ D\ \longrightarrow\ D^{^{\prime}}  
\end{equation}   
is exact if it commutes with the translation functors, and takes every distinguished triangle in $D$ to a distinguished triangle in $D^{^{\prime}}$.  One such example is provided by algebraic varieies  

\begin{equation}  
f:\ X\ \longrightarrow\ Y  
\end{equation}  


\begin{equation}  
\Rightarrow\ \ f_{_*}:\ \text{QCoh}(X)\ \longrightarrow\ \text{QCoh}(Y)  
\end{equation}  

A category $\text{QCoh}(X)$ admits enough injectives, implying there is a right derived functor  

\begin{equation}  
Rf_{_*}:\ D^{^b}\left(\text{Coh}(X)\right)\ \longrightarrow\ D^{^b}\left(\text{Coh}(Y)\right).   
\end{equation}    

Focusing on smooth projective varieties, every map  

\begin{equation}  
f:\ X\ \longrightarrow\ Y  
\end{equation}   
induces two functors  

\begin{equation}   
Rf_{_*}:\ D^{^b}(X)\ \longrightarrow\ D^{^b}(Y)  
\end{equation}  
and  

\begin{equation}  
Lf^{^*}:\ D^{^b}(Y)\ \longrightarrow\ D^{^b}(X).    
\end{equation}  

Furthermore, each complex  

\begin{equation}  
{\cal F} \ \in\ D^{^b}(X)  
\end{equation}  
induces an exact functor  

\begin{equation}  
\overset{L}{\bigotimes} \ {\cal F}:\ D^{^b}(X)\ \longrightarrow\ D^{^b}(X)  
\end{equation}    

Making use of these functors, we can introduce a larger class of exact functors.  Let $X$ and $Y$ be smooth projective varieties of dimension $n$ and $m$, respectively.   

Consider the projections  

\begin{equation}  
X\ \xleftarrow{p}\ X\times Y\ \xrightarrow{q}\ Y  
\end{equation}   

Every object   

\begin{equation}  
{\cal E}\ \in\ D^{^b}(X\times Y)  
\end{equation}   
defines an exact functor  

\begin{equation}  
\Phi_{_{\cal E}}:\ D^{^b}(X)\ \longrightarrow\ D^{^b}(Y)  
\end{equation}  
by the following formula  

\begin{equation}  
\Phi_{_{\cal E}}(.)\ \overset{def.}{=}\ Rq_{_*}\left({\cal E}\ \overset{L}{\otimes}\ p^{^*}(.)\right)  
\end{equation}    

The same object defines another functor  

\begin{equation}  
\Psi_{_{\cal E}}:\ D^{^b}(Y)\ \longrightarrow\ D^{^b}(X)  
\end{equation}  
by the similar formula  

\begin{equation}  
\Psi_{_{\cal E}}(.)\ \equiv\ Rp_{_*}\left({\cal E}\ \overset{L}{\otimes}\ q^{^*}(.)\right). 
\end{equation}    

The functor $\Phi_{_{\cal E}}$ admits left- and right-adjoint functors $\Phi_{_{\cal E}}^{^*}$ and $\Phi_{_{\cal E}}^{^!}$, respectively, defined as follows:  

\begin{equation}  
\Phi^{^*}_{_{\cal E}}\ \simeq\ \Psi_{_{{\cal E}^{^V}\otimes\ q^{^*}K_{_Y}[m]}}\ \ \ \ \ , \ \ \ \ \Phi^{^!}_{_{\cal E}}\ \simeq\ \Psi_{_{{\cal E}^{^V}\otimes\ p^{^*}K_{_X}[m]}}   
\end{equation}  
where $K_{_X}$ and $K_{_Y}$ are canonical sheaves on $X$ and $Y$, respectively, and ${\cal E}^{^V}$ denotes  RHom$\left({\cal E},{\cal O}_{_{X\times Y}}\right)$.   


\medskip   

\medskip   

\underline{Example 1}   

\medskip   

\medskip  

Let    

\begin{equation}    
f:\ X\longrightarrow\ Y  
\end{equation}  
be a morphism of varieties.   

$\Gamma(f)$ defines a subvariety of $X\times Y$ that is a graph of $f$. It is clear that the functor  

\begin{equation}  
Rf_{_*}:\ D^{^b}(X)\ \longrightarrow\ D^{^b}(Y)  
\end{equation}  
is isomorphic to $\Phi_{_{{\cal O}_{_{\Gamma(f)}}}}$, where ${\cal O}_{_{\Gamma(f)}}$ is the structure sheaf of the graph $\Gamma(f)$. In the same way, the functor  

\begin{equation}   
Lf^{^*}:\ D^{^b}(Y)\ \longrightarrow\ D^{^b}(X)  
\end{equation}   
is isomorphic to $\Psi_{_{{\cal O}_{_{\Gamma(f)}}}}$.   



\medskip   

\medskip   

\underline{Example 2}   

\medskip   

\medskip  

Let  

\begin{equation}   
\delta:\ \Delta\ \hookrightarrow\ X\ \times\ X  
\end{equation}  
be the diagonal embedding. $\Delta$ is the graph of the identity morphism  

\begin{equation}  
\text{Id}:\ D^{^b}(X)\ \longrightarrow\ D^{^b}(X)  
\end{equation}    
is represented by the structure sheaf ${\cal O}_{_{\Delta}}$. For any object ${\cal F}\in D^{^b}(X)$, the functor 

\begin{equation}  
\overset{L}{\bigotimes} \ {\cal F}:\ D^{^b}(X)\ \longrightarrow\ D^{^b}(X)  
\end{equation}    
is isomorphic to $\Phi_{_{\delta_{_*}{\cal F}}}$.

There is a large class of exact functors between derived categories of smooth projective varieties consisting of functors admitting the form $\Phi_{_{\cal E}}$ for some complex ${\cal E}$ pn the product. This class is closed under the composition of functors. Let $X, Y, Z$ be three smooth projective varieties, and let  

\begin{equation}  
\Phi_{_I}:\ D^{^b}(X)\ \longrightarrow\ D^{^b}(Y)  \ \ \ \ , \ \ \ \ \Phi_{_J}:\ D^{^b}(Y)\ \longrightarrow\ D^{^b}(Z)
\end{equation}    
be two functors, where $I$ and $J$ are objects of $D^{^b}(X\times Y)$ and $D^{^b}(Y\times Z)$, respectively.   

By further denoting with   

\begin{equation}  
p_{_{XY}}\ , \ p_{_{YZ}}\ , \  p_{_{XZ}}   
\end{equation}   
the projections of $X\times Y\times Z$ on the corresponding pair of factors. 




\medskip   

\medskip   

\underline{Lemma}   

\medskip   

\medskip  

The composition 

\begin{equation}  
\Phi_{_J}\ \circ\ \Phi_{_I}\ \simeq\ \Phi_{_K} 
\end{equation}   


\begin{equation}   
K\ \simeq\ R_{_{p_{_{XZ*}}}}\left(p^{^*}_{_{YZ}}(J)\ \otimes\ p^{^*}_{_{XY}}(I)\right).  
\end{equation}   


This is conjectured to encode all exact functors between $D^{^b}$Coh on smooth projective varieties.   





\medskip   

\medskip   

\underline{Theorem}   

\medskip   

\medskip 



Let $X$ and $Y$ be smooth projective varieties. Suppose   

\begin{equation}  
F:\ D^{^b}(X)\ \xrightarrow{\ \ \ \sim\ \ \ }\ D^{^b}(Y)  
\end{equation}   
is an exact equivalence. Then, there is a unique object (up t oisomorphisms) 

\begin{equation}  
{\cal E}\ \in\ D^{^b}(X\times Y)  
\end{equation}  
such that the functors $R$ and $\Phi_{_{\cal E}}$ are isomorphic.    

Defining the group of isomorphism classes of exact equivalences  

\begin{equation}  
F:\ D^{^b}(X)\ \xrightarrow{\ \ \ \sim\ \ \ }\ D^{^b}(X)  
\end{equation}  
is called the group of autoequivalences of $D^{^b}(X)$, denoted by  Auteq$\left(D^{^b}(X)\right)$.   

Assume that $\mathbb{k}\equiv\mathbb{C}$. For every element   


\begin{equation}   
\xi\ \in\ H^{^{\bullet}}(X\times Y, \mathbb{Q})  
\end{equation}  
define the following maps  

\begin{equation}  
v_{_{\xi}}:\ H^{^{\bullet}}(X, \mathbb{Q})\ \longrightarrow\ H^{^{\bullet}}(Y, \mathbb{Q})  \ \ \ \ \ , \ \ \ \ \ w_{_{\xi}}:\ H^{^{\bullet}}(Y, \mathbb{Q})\ \longrightarrow\ H^{^{\bullet}}(X, \mathbb{Q})  
\end{equation}  

\begin{equation}  
v_{_{\xi}}(.)\ =\ q_{_*}\left(\xi\cup p^{^*}(.)\right)  \ \ \ \ \ , \ \ \ \ \ w_{_{\xi}}(.)\ =\   p_{_*}\left(\xi\cup q^{^*}(.)\right).   
\end{equation}     

From the K$\ddot{\text{u}}$nneth formula, it follows that any linear map between cohomologies has this form for some $\xi$. The composition formula for cohomological correspondence is well-known and similar to the composition of functors.   Given three varieties, $X, Y, Z$, $\xi, \eta$ elements of $H^{^{\bullet}}(X\times Y)$ and $H^{^{\bullet}}(Y\times Z)$, respectively.   




\medskip   

\medskip   

\underline{Lemma}   

\medskip   

\medskip 

The composition   


\begin{equation}    
v_{_{\eta}}\ \circ\ v_{_{\xi}}  
\end{equation}  
is isomorphic to $v_{_{\zeta}}$, where $\zeta\ \in\ H^{^{\bullet}}(X\times Z)$ is given by the formula  

\begin{equation}  
\zeta\ = \ p_{_{XZ*}}\left(p^{^*}_{_{YZ}}(\eta)\ \cup\ p^{^*}_{_{XY}}(\xi)\right).  
\end{equation}   

There is a natural correspondence that attaches to a functor   

\begin{equation}  
\Phi_{_{\xi}}:\ D^{^b}(X)\ \longrightarrow\ D^{^b}(Y)  
\end{equation}    
a linear map of vector spaces  

\begin{equation}  
\phi_{_{\cal E}}:\ H^{^{\bullet}}(X,\mathbb{Q})\ \longrightarrow\ H^{^{\bullet}}(Y,\mathbb{Q}).    
\end{equation}   

Each exact functor  

\begin{equation}  
F:\ D^{^b}(X)\ \longrightarrow\ D^{^b}(Y)   
\end{equation}   
induces a homomorphism   

\begin{equation}  
\overline{F}:\ K(X)\ \longrightarrow\ K(Y)  
\end{equation}      
where $K(X)$ and $K(Y)$ are the Grothendieck groups of the categories $D^{^b}(X)$ and $D^{^b}(Y)$. In addition to this, there is also the Chern character map  

\begin{equation}  
\text{ch}: \ K(X)\ \longrightarrow\ H^{^{\bullet}}(X,\mathbb{Q}).  
\end{equation}  

For every object  

\begin{equation}  
{\cal E}\ \in\ D^{^b}(X\ \times\ Y)  
\end{equation}   

\begin{equation}  
\begin{aligned}
\phi_{_{\cal E}}\ &\overset{def.}{=}\ v_{_{\text{ch}({\cal E})\ \cup\ p^{^*}\left(\text{td}_{_X}\right)}}(.)\\  
&\ =\ q_{_*}\left(\text{ch}({\cal E})\ \cup\ p^{^*}\left(\text{td}_{_X}\right)\ \cup\ p^{^*}(.)\right)  
\end{aligned}   
\end{equation}   



\begin{equation}  
\begin{aligned}
\psi_{_{\cal E}}\ &\overset{def.}{=}\ w_{_{\text{ch}({\cal E})\ \cup\ q^{^*}\left(\text{td}_{_X}\right)}}(.)\\  
&\ =\ p_{_*}\left(\text{ch}({\cal E})\ \cup\ q^{^*}\left(\text{td}_{_X}\right)\ \cup\ q^{^*}(.)\right)  
\end{aligned}   
\end{equation}
with td$_{_X}$, td$_{_Y}$ defining the Todd classes of $X$ and $Y$, respectively. 

\medskip   

\medskip   

\underline{Lemma}   

\medskip   

\medskip     


For given ${\cal E}\ in\ D^{^b}(X\times Y)$, the following diagram is commutative  


\begin{equation}   
\begin{aligned}  
&K(X)\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \xrightarrow{\overline{\Phi}_{_{\cal E}}}\ \ \ \ \ \ K(Y) \\ 
&\\
&\text{ch}\ \downarrow\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \  \downarrow\text{ch}\ \\  
&H^{^{\bullet}}(X,\mathbb{Q})\ \ \ \ \xrightarrow{\overline{\phi}_{_{\cal E}}}\ \ \ \ \  H^{^{\bullet}}(Y,\mathbb{Q})  
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}   

Assume that a functor  

\begin{equation}  
\Phi_{_k}:\ D^{^b}(X)\ \longrightarrow\ D^{^b}(Z)  
\end{equation}  
is isomorphic to   

\begin{equation}   
\Phi_{_J}\ \circ\ \Phi_{_I}  
\end{equation}  
for  

\begin{equation}  
\Phi_{_I}:\ D^{^b}(X)\ \longrightarrow\ D^{^b}(Y)  \ \ \ \ , \ \ \ \ \Phi_{_J}:\ D^{^b}(Y)\ \longrightarrow\ D^{^b}(Z).
\end{equation}  

Then,   

\begin{equation}  
\phi_{_K}\ = \ \phi_{_J}\ \circ\ \phi_{_I}  
\end{equation}  
implying the correspondence  

\begin{equation}  
\Phi_{_{\cal E}}\ \mapsto\ \phi_{_{\cal E}}  
\end{equation}  
induces a representation  

\begin{equation}  
\rho_{_X}:\ \text{Auteq}\left(D^{^b}(X)\right)\ \longrightarrow\ GL\left(H^{^{\bullet}}(X,\mathbb{Q})\right).     
\end{equation}     

Both Lemmas follow from the Riemann-Roch-Grothendieck Theorem.    

\medskip   

\medskip   

\underline{Example 3}   

\medskip   

\medskip     


Let 

\begin{equation}   
f:\ X\ \longrightarrow\ Y  
\end{equation}   
be a morphism. The functor  

\begin{equation}  
Lf^{^*}\ \simeq\ \Psi_{_{{\cal O}_{_{\Gamma(f)}}}}\ \ \ \ , \ \ \ \ Rf_{_*}\ \simeq\ \Phi_{_{{\cal O}_{_{\Gamma(f)}}}}  
\end{equation} 
give maps $\psi_{_{{\cal O}_{_{\Gamma(f)}}}}$ and $\phi_{_{{\cal O}_{_{\Gamma(f)}}}}$ between cohomologies. Furthermore, the morphism $f$ induces the map  

\begin{equation}  
