\item CHL a formulation using weakly-unobstructed deformations of a Lagrangian immersion to construct the mirror geometry, which is a LG model.  This is particularly important when the Lagrangian fibration is not known, or is too complicated in parctice.  This way, we can always construct a natural functor from the Fukaya category t othe mirror B-model (namely MF for a LG model).    The Theory can be applied to the orbifold $\mathbb{P}_{_{a,b,c}}^{^1}$ to construct its mirror by using an immersed Lagrangian [Seidel], succesfully covering HMS for elliptic and hyperbolic orbifolds. 

\end{enumerate}   

Essentially, the technique adopted by [CHL] consists in fixing a reference Lagrangian, $\mathbb{L}$, and a space of odd-degree weakly unobstructed formal deformations $b$ of $\mathbb{L}$.  The mirror functor corresponds to a family of curved Yoneda embedding, constructed by using curved Floer theory between the family $(\mathbb{L}, b)$ and the other Lagrangians in the Fukaya category. For Lagrangian fibrations, the singular fibers and wall-corossing phenomenons make it very difficult to construct the family Floer functor in an explicit way.   

On the other hand, [CHL] allows to start from an immersed Lagrangian, with no wall-crossing taking place.   

[Khovanov-Seidel] introduced the idea of applying the Yoneda functor to the study of Fukaya categories. From Fukaya's construction, there is an $A_{_{\infty}}$-algebra, ${|cal A}$, associated to a fixed collection of Lagrangians    

\begin{equation}  
\mathbb{L}\ \equiv\ \left\{L_{_1},...,L_{_N}\right\}  
\label{eq:coll}  
\end{equation}        
and there is a functor   

\begin{equation}  
D^{^b}\text{Fuk}(X)\ \longrightarrow\ D({\cal A}).  
\end{equation}    


[CHL], instead: the non-commutative algebras in this paper are obtained from Maurer-Cartan solutions rather than the whole Hom$(\mathbb{L, L})$ space, \cite{11}.  

Furthermore, this formalism extends to non-compact Lagrangians. This occurs in the case of punctured Riemann surfaces. Importantly, to achieve mirrors i sto consider weakly-unobstructed odd-degree deformations. Then, need to consider curved $\mathbb{Z}_{_2}$-graded Fukaya categories and categories of Matrix Factorisations in the mirror. 

In this section, we will be dealing with the extension of the [CHL] formalism to the deformation space by noncommutative deformations. The reason to do so is two-fold:  

\begin{itemize}  


\item  First of all, the fact that the solution space of the weak MAurer-Cartan equations associated to $\mathbb{L}$ in \cite{12} might be trivial (the 0-vector space), as is needed in the case for a generic Lagrangian, $\mathbb{L}$. Nontriviality requires a certain symmetry on the moduli of holomorphic disks bounded by $\mathbb{L}$. Specifically, it requires that the counting of holomorphic disks through a point $p\in\mathbb{L}$ is independent of the choice of $p$. Nontrivial solutions are thereby achieved by using noncommutative deformations.    Foor example, the Seidel Lagrangian in a 3-punctured sphere is generically non-symmetric about the equator. There is no non-zero commutative solution to the Maurer-Cartan equation. On the other hand, the noncommutative solutions form the space of Sklyanin algebras  


\begin{equation}  
\frac{\mathbb{C}<x,y,z>}{<xy-ayx,yz-azy,zx-axz>}   
\end{equation}    
as mirrors to the elliptic orbifold $\mathbb{P}^{^1}_{_{3,3,3}}$ using noncommutative deformations.  

\item   The second reason is that of globalising the mirror construction: the mirror functor is localised to the Lagrangian immersion, $\mathbb{L}$, fixed in the beginning. Applying the mirror construction to a family of Lagrangian immersions, one can capture more about the global geometry. For the collection \eqref{eq:coll} , the End-algebra is a path algebra of a directed graph, and is naturally noncommutative.

Collections of Lagrangians can be achieved by means of dimer models in 1D, or skeletons of coamoebas in arbitrary dimensions. 

\end{itemize}    

\subsection{The construction}  

The odd-degree Floer-theoretical endomorphisms of $\mathbb{L}$ are described by a directed graph $Q$ (a.k.a. quiver). The path algebra $\Lambda Q$ is regarded as  the noncommutative space of formal deformations of $\mathbb{L}$. Each edge $e$ of $Q$ corresponds to an odd-degree Floer generator $X_{_e}$ and a formal dual variable $x_{_e}\in\Lambda Q$. Consider the deformation  

\begin{equation}  
b\ =\ \sum_{_e}x_{_e}X_{_e}  
\end{equation}  

The obstruction is given by   

\begin{equation}  
\mu_{_0}^{^b}\ = \ \mu\left(e^{^b}\right)\ =\ \sum_{_{k\ge 0}}\mu_{_k}(b,...,b)    
\end{equation}     


The new thing is that extending the notion of weakly-unobstructedness by FOOO to the noncommutative setting; the corresponding weak Maurer-Cartan equation is   

\begin{equation}   
\mu_{_0}^{^b}\ =\ \sum_{_{i=1}}{\cal W}_{_i}\mathbf{1}_{_{L_{_i}}}  
\end{equation}    
where $\mathbf{1}_{_{L_{_i}}}$ denotes the Floer-theoretical unit corresponding to the fundamental class of $L_{_i}$, assuming the Fukaya $A_{_{\infty}}$-category is unital.   

The solution space is given by a quiver algebra with relations  

\begin{equation}  
{\cal A}\ = \ \Lambda Q/R  
\end{equation}  
where $R$ is the 2-sided ideal generated by weakly-unobstructed relations.  

The end product is a noncommutative LG model  

\begin{equation}   
\left({\cal A}\ =\ {\cal W}\ = \ \sum_{_i}{\cal W}_{_i}\right)
\end{equation} 

This is referred to as a generalised mirror of $X$, in the sense that there exists a natural functor    


\begin{equation}  
\text{Fuk}(X)\ \longrightarrow\ \text{NCMF}({\cal A, W})   
\end{equation}  

By \emph{generalised} here, we really mean in the following sense:  

\begin{enumerate}  


\item  It is a generalisation of the SYZ program, where Lagrangian tori are replaced by immersions.  

\item   The functor needs not be an equivalence, so $({\cal A, W})$ needs not be a mirror of $X$ in the original sense.   

\end{enumerate}    


\underline{Theorem 1.1}  

There exists an $A_{_{\infty}}$-functor   

\begin{equation}  
{\cal F}^{^{\mathbb{L}}}:\ \text{Fuk}(X)\ \longrightarrow\ \text{MF}({\cal A, W})   
\end{equation}   
which is injective on $H^{^{\bullet}}(\text{Hom}(\mathbb{L},U))\ \forall U$. An important feature is that the LG superpotential, ${\cal W}$, constructed this way is automatically a central element in ${\cal A}$. In particular, we can make sense of ${\cal A}/<{\cal W}>$ as a hypersurface singularity defined by the zero set of ${\cal W}$. 

\underline{Theorem 1.2}  

${\cal W}\ \in\ {\cal A}$ is a central element.     

The noncommutative formal space dual to an $A_{_{\infty}}$-algebra uses constructions by [Kontsevich, Soibelman]. The quiver algebra with relations, ${\cal A}$ constructed by [CHL] can be regarded as a subvariety in the formal space (restricting to weakly-unobstructed odd-degree deformations). Moreover, obtaining a LG model instead of a CY algebra. Weakly-unobstructedness is crucial for the construction of the mirror functor. The resulting mirror is a curved dg-algebra. However, it is very difficult to compute in practice, since it involves all higher $\mu_{_k}$ operations.    An interesting feature of this setup is that we can achieve multiple mirrors to a single symplectic geometry, $X$. The construction of a LG mirror $({\cal A}_{_{\mathbb{L}}},{\cal W}_{_{\mathbb{L}}})$ and the associated mirror functor   


\begin{equation}  
{\cal F}^{^{\mathbb{L}}}:\ \text{Fuk}(X)\ \longrightarrow\ \text{MF}({\cal A}_{_{\mathbb{L}}},{\cal W}_{_{\mathbb{L}}})  
\end{equation}  
depends on the choice of $\mathbb{L}\ \subset\ X$. 

For a different choice of $\mathbb{L}^{^{\prime}}$, the mirror  $\left({\cal A}_{_{\mathbb{L}}}^{^{\prime}},{\cal W}_{_{\mathbb{L}}}^{^{\prime}}\right)$ would be different, even though their B-model categories  MF$({\cal A}_{_{\mathbb{L}}},{\cal W}_{_{\mathbb{L}}})$ and MF$\left({\cal A}_{_{\mathbb{L}}}^{^{\prime}},{\cal W}_{_{\mathbb{L}}}^{^{\prime}}\right)$ can remain equivalent. A similar phenomenon occurs in SYZ mirror symmetry for multiple Lagrangian fibrations.  


Later on, we will see how to construct a continuous family of LG models $\left({\cal A}_{_t},{\cal W}_{_t}\right)$ that are mirror to a single elliptic orbifold (with a fixed K$\ddot{\text{a}}$hler structure). \footnote{Albeit there being applications beyond Mirror Symmetry, they will not be dealt with in this course.}  Most importantly, their construction provides an interesting link between Lagrangian Floer theory and deformation quantisation in the sense of Kontsevich: take $\mathbb{L}$. The mirror 
quiver algebra ${\cal A}$ can be obtained from an  exceptional collection of a variety $\tilde X$. Then, a commutative mirror, $(\hat X, {\cal W})$ is equivalent to the noncommutative model $({\cal A, W})$.   


Take a defomation family, $\mathbb{L}_{_t}$, with $\mathbb{L}_{_0}\ \equiv\ \mathbb{L}$. The deformations can be obtained by equipping $\mathbb{L}$ with flat $U(1)$-connection, or by deforming $\mathbb{L}$using non-exact closed 1-forms on $\mathbb{L}$. The mirror construction gives a family of non-commutative LG models $({\cal A}_{_t}, {\cal W}_{_t})$. The NC hypersurface ${\cal A}_{_t}/{\cal W}_{_t}$ should give deformation-quantisation of the hypersurface   

\begin{equation}  
\left\{{\cal W}=0\right\}\ \subset\ \hat X  
\end{equation}  

Examples of this are for mirrors of elliptic orbifolds  $\mathbb{P}^{^1}_{_{3,3,3}}$ and $\mathbb{P}^{^1}_{_{2,2,2,2}}$. 

The elliptic orbifold (discussed last time),   

\begin{equation}  
X\ =\ E/\mathbb{Z}_{_3}\ = \mathbb{P}^{^1}_{_{3,3,3}}   
\end{equation}     
has a noncommutative LG mirror $(\mathbb{C}^{^3}, {\cal W})$ constructed by [CHL] by using the immersed Lagrangian, $\mathbb{L}$ given by Seidel. $\mathbb{L}$ is symmetric under any anti-symplectic involution on $\mathbb{P}^{^1}_{_{3,3,3}}$. This symmetry can be broken, and one could consider a deformation family of immersed Lagrangians  $\mathbb{L}_{_h}\subset X$, obtaining $({\cal A}_{_h},{\cal W}_{_h})$ as the mirror, where ${\cal A}_{_h}$ is a Sklyanin algebra, and ${\cal W}_{_h}$ is its central element.   


For $h=0$


\begin{equation}  
\left({\cal A}_{_0}/{\cal W}_{_0}\right)\ =\ \left(\mathbb{C}[x,y,z]/{\cal W}\right)  
\end{equation}  
we recover the commutative mirror.  

The hypersurface ${\cal A}_{_h}/{\cal W}_{_h}$ gives a deformation quantisation of the affine del Pezzo surface  

\begin{equation}  
\mathbb{C}[x,y,z]/{\cal W}  
\end{equation}  

$h$ here denotes a deformation parameter belonging to a compact manifold $(S^{^1})^{^2}$.  

One of the most extensively-used NC algebras is the Sklyanin algebra  

\begin{equation}  
{\cal A}\ \overset{def.}{=}\ \frac{\Lambda<x,y,z>}{(axy+byz+cz^{^2},ayz+bzy+cx^{^2},azx+bxz+cy^{^2})}  
\end{equation}    
for fixed $a,b,c,\ \in\ \mathbb{C}$. For $a=-b, c=0$,  ${\cal A}=\mathbb{C}[x,y,z]$. As a graded algebra, ${\cal A}$ gives a NC deformation of $\mathbb{P}^{^2}$. 




\subsection{Noncommutative Mirror from a single Lagrangian and the Centrality Theorem}     


The first step involves extending the base of an $A_{_{\infty}}$-algebra, $A$, to a noncommutative space. A key theorem, by [CHL], is that the potential function ${\cal W}$ is automatically a central element.       


For the mirror construction, the $A_{_{\infty}}$-algebra is taken to be the Floer chain complex of a single (immersed) Lagrabgian in a K$\ddot{\text{a}}$hler manifold, $X$. Then, the base space together with the potential ${\cal W}$ they construct is regarded as a NCLG model for $X$ in a generalised sense. Such construction, therefore relates symplectic geometry of $X$ with a NC AG pf ${|cal W}$. However, such functor is not necessarily a categorical equivalence. The above construction is algebraic, implying it can be applied to any $A_{_{|infty}}$-caategory, not necessarily Fukaya categories. 

An upgrade of this is by using finitely many Lagrangians (rather than a single one), and the corresponding base space will form a quiver algebra with relations. This procedure makes the generated mirror more global, and thereby capable of capturing more general information of $X$.    

Take an immersed Lagrangian, $\mathbb{L}$, in a K$\ddot{\text{a}}$hler manifold, $X$,   


\begin{equation}  
\iota:\ \mathbb{L}\ \longrightarrow\  X    
\end{equation}  

For $\mathbb{L}$ a compact, spin oriented, unobstructed Lagrangian immersion with self-intersection points. \cite{12} is associated with an $A_{_{\infty}}$-algebra whose underlying vector space is the space of cochains   

\begin{equation}    
\begin{aligned}   
CF^{^{\bullet}}(\mathbb{L,L})\ &\overset{def.}{=}\ CF^{^{\bullet}}(\mathbb{L}\underset{\iota}{\times}\mathbb{L})\\   
&=\ C^{^{\bullet}}(\mathbb{L})\oplus\bigoplus_{_p}\text{Span}\left\{(p_{_-},p_{_+}),(p_{_+},p_{_-})\right\}  
\end{aligned}  
\end{equation}  
where $p$ runs over immersed points, $p_{_{\pm}}$ are its preimages under $\iota$, whereas $(p_{_-},p_{_+}),(p_{_+},p_{_-})$ constitute complementary immersed generators.  

The vector space is $\mathbb{Z}_{_2}$-graded ($\mathbb{Z}$-graded when $X$ is CY, and $\mathbb{L}\ \subset\ X$ has Maslov class equal to $0$). Consider the NC deformation by the immersed generators; let $\{X_{_e}\}$ be the set of all odd-degree immersed generators in $CF^{^{\bullet}}(\mathbb{L,L})$, $e$ running in a finite label set $E$ of immersed points.    

One can also include odd degree generators other than the immersed generator, but in this course we will not be dealing with it. 


$\{x_{_e}\}$ denote formal dual variables. Defining   

\begin{equation}   
K\ \overset{def.}{=}\ \Lambda_{_0}<<x_{_e}:\ e\ \in\ E>>\ \ \ \Rightarrow\ \ \ \ \tilde A\ =\ K\ \underset{\Lambda_{_0}}{\hat{\bigotimes}}\ A  
\end{equation}   
with $\tilde A$ defining an $A_{_{\infty}}$-algebra.   

The formal bounding cochain  


\begin{equation}  
b\ = \ \sum_{_e} x_{_e} X_{_e}  
\end{equation}    
represents a formal smoothing deformation of $\mathbb{L}$at each intersection point, $e$. This $A_{_{\infty}}$-algebra can be defined with $A_{_{\infty}}$-operations $\{\mu_{_k}^{^b}\}$, where $\mu_{_k}^{^b}$ denotes the deformed $A_{_{\infty}}$-operation defined as follows  

\begin{equation}  
\begin{aligned}
\mu_{_K}^{^b}(v_{_1},...,v_{_k})\ &=\ \sum_{_{l_{_o},...,l_{_k}\ge 0}}\mu_{_{k+l_{_0}+...+l_{_k}}}\left(b^{^{l_{_o}}}v_{_1},b^{^{l_{_1}}},v_{_2},...,v_{_k}, b^{^{l_{_k}}}\right) \\   
&= \mu\left(e^{^b}, v_{_1}, e^{^b}, v_{_2},...,e^{^b},v_{_k},e^{^b}\right)
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}  
\begin{aligned}
\Rightarrow\ \ \ \mu_{_K}^{^{b_{_0},...,b_{_k}}}(v_{_1},...,v_{_k})\ &=\ \sum_{_{l_{_o},...,l_{_k}\ge 0}}\mu_{_{k+l_{_0}+...+l_{_k}}}\left(b^{^{l_{_o}}}v_{_1},b^{^{l_{_1}}},v_{_2},...,v_{_k}, b^{^{l_{_k}}}\right) \\   
&= \mu\left(e^{^b}, v_{_1}, e^{^b}, v_{-2},...,e^{^b},v_{_k},e^{^b}\right)
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}  
for $b$ a Maurer-Cartan element.    


The worldsheet potential  

\begin{equation}  
{\cal W}\ =\ {\cal W}_{_{\cal L}}\ \subset\ {\cal A}  
\end{equation}  
is a generalisation of $X$ w.r.t. the choice of an immersed Lagrangian, $\mathbb{L}$. $\mu_{_0}$ counts the holomorphic disks bounded by $\mathbb{L}$ passing through a generic point. ${\cal W}_{_{\mathbb{L}}}$ counts holomorphic disks bounded in $\mathbb{L}$ passing through a generic point with $b$-boundary insertions.  Holomorphic sisks are allowed to turn corners at $X_{_e}$, and the names of the corners are recorded in the variables $x_{_e}$.  

\medskip   

\medskip 

\underline{Theorem} [CHL] (Centrality of the potential)  

The noncommutative potential ${\cal W}_{_{\mathbb{L}}}\ \in\ {\cal A}$ is central, meaning  

\begin{equation}  
{\cal W}_{_{\mathbb{L}}}\ \cdot\ x_{_e}\ = \ x_{_e}\ \cdot\ {\cal W}_{_{\mathbb{L}}}  \ \ \  \ \ \ \ , \ \ \ \ \ \ \forall\ x_{_e}
\end{equation}   

\underline{Proof}  

\begin{equation}  
\mu_{_0}^{^b}\ =\ {\cal W}_{_{\mathbb{L}}}\mathbf{1}_{_{\mathbb{L}}}  
\end{equation}   
over ${\cal A}$.   

$\hat\mu$ denotes the coderivation induced from $\mu$, implying  


\begin{equation}    
\begin{aligned}
0\ &=\ \mu\ \circ\ \hat\mu\left(e^{^b}\right)\ \\ 
&=\ \mu\left(e^{^b}, \hat\mu_{_e}^{^{(b)}}, e^{^b}\right)\\
&=\ \mu_{_2}\left(b,{\cal W}_{_{\mathbb{L}}}\mathbf{1}_{_{\mathbb{L}}}\right)+\mu_{_2}\left({\cal W}_{_{\mathbb{L}}}\mathbb{1}_{_{\mathbb{L}}}, b\right)  \\  
&=\ \sum_{_e}\left({\cal W}_{_{\mathbb{L}}}\cdot x_{_e}-x_{_e}\cdot {\cal W}_{_{\mathbb{L}}}\right)\ X_{_e}
\end{aligned}  
\end{equation}  
following from the property of $\mathbb{1}_{_{\mathbb{L}}}$.  

Centrality is also required to ensure that   

\begin{equation}   
\left(\mu_{_1}^{^{b,b}}\right)^{^2}\ =\ 0  
\end{equation}  

The \emph{spacetime superpotential} is defined as follows  

\begin{equation}   
\Phi\ =\ \Phi_{_{\mathbb{L}}}\ \overset{def.}{=}\ \sum_{_k}\ \frac{1}{k+1}<\mu_{_k}(b,...,b),b>\ \in\ K/[K,K]  
\end{equation}    
such that 

\begin{equation}    
{\cal A}\ =\ \text{Jac}(\Phi)\ =\ \Lambda_{_0}\left<\left<x_{_e}:\ e\in E\right>\right>\bigg/\left(\partial_{_{x_{_e}}}\Phi:\ e\in E\right)  
\end{equation}   

A triple  

\begin{equation}  
\left(\Lambda_{_0}\left<\left<x_{_e}:\ e\in E\right>\right>, \Phi, {\cal W}\right)  
\end{equation}  
is referred to as a \emph{noncommutative} LG model if the noncommutative Jacobi algebra  


\begin{equation}    
{\cal A}\ =\ \text{Jac}(\Phi)\ \overset{def.}{=}\ \Lambda_{_0}\left<\left<x_{_e}:\ e\in E\right>\right>\bigg/\left(\partial_{_{x_{_e}}}\Phi\right)  
\end{equation}    
has ${\cal W}$ in its center. 


\medskip  

\medskip  

\underline{Corollary}    

Under the assumption that $\partial_{_{x_{_e}}}\Phi,\  \forall e\ \in\ E$ are weakly-unobstructed relations, implying  

\begin{equation}  
{\cal A}\ =\ \text{Jac}(\Phi)  
\end{equation}  
the generalised mirror   


\begin{equation}    
\left(\Lambda_{_0}\left<\left<x_{_e}:\ e\in E\right>\right>, \Phi, {\cal W}\right)  
\end{equation}   
associated to $\mathbb{L}$ is a NC LG model.    

Next step is to transform Lagrangian submanifolds in $X$ to MF$({\cal W})$. Symplectic geometry corresponds to NCAG in the generalised mirror.  

\subsubsection{The mirror functor from a single Lagrangian}     

Take MF$({\cal A, W})$ equipped with two projective ${\cal A}$-modules, $M_{_0}, M_{_1}$, with maps  

\begin{equation}  
\delta_{_0}:\ M_{_0}\ \longrightarrow\ M_{_1}  \ \ \ \ \ ,\ \ \ \ \ \delta_{_1}:\ M_{_1}\ \longrightarrow\ M_{_0}  
\end{equation}  
such that   

\begin{equation}  
\delta_{_0}\delta_{_1}\ =\ \delta_{_1}\delta_{_0}\ =\ {\cal W}\cdot\ \text{Id}  \end{equation}  
or as a $\mathbb{Z}_{_2}$-graded projective ${\cal A}$-module  

\begin{equation}   
M\ =\ M_{_0}\ \oplus\ M_{_1}  
\end{equation}    
with an odd endomorphism  

\begin{equation}    
\delta\ = \ \delta_{_0}\ +\ \delta_{_1}  
\end{equation}    
such that   

\begin{equation}   
\delta^{^2}\ = \ {\cal W}\ \cdot\ \text{Id}  
\end{equation}     

The differential graded category of MF$({\cal A, W})$ is defined as follows. 

A morphism between MF$(M,\delta_{_M})$ and MF$(N, \delta_{_N})$ is an ${\cal A}$-module homomorphism  

\begin{equation}  
f:\ M\ \longrightarrow\ N  
\end{equation}  

The morphism space is $\mathbb{Z}_{_2}$-graded and equipped with a differential $mu_{_1}$ or d defined by  

\begin{equation}  
\mu_{_1}(f)\ =\ \delta_{_N}\circ f-(-1)^{^{\text{deg}(f)}}f\circ\delta_{_M}  
\end{equation}  

$\mu_{_2}$ denotes the composition of morphisms. 

Denoting by Fuk$(X)$ the $\mathbb{Z}_{_2}$-graded Fukaya category of unobstructed Lagrangian submanifolds of $X$. For an object $U$ of Fuk$(X)$, its mirror MF$({\cal A, W})$ is defined as follows  

\begin{equation}  
{\cal F}(U)\ \overset{def.}{=}\ \left({\cal A}\ \underset{\Lambda_{_0}}{\hat\bigotimes}\ CF^{^{\bullet}}((\mathbb{L},b),U), \text{d}\ =\ (-1)^{^{\text{deg}(.)}}\mu_{_1}^{^{b,0}}(.)\right)   
\end{equation}   

There are different ways of realising Lagrangian Floer theory and $CF^{^{\bullet}}((\mathbb{L},b),U)$ depends on the choice.     

\cite{12} showed that $U$ can have clean intersections with $\mathbb{L}$, implying $CF^{^{\bullet}}((\mathbb{L},b),U)$ is the direct sum of the spaces of singular chains (or differential forms) in the intersections.     


[Seidel] showed that if $U$ transversally-intersects $\mathbb{L}$, then $CF^{^{\bullet}}((\mathbb{L},b),U)$ is spanned by the intersection points.   

Fixing only one of the choices to carry out the constructions, the operation of $\mu_{_1}^{^{b,0}}$ on   

\begin{equation}  
{\cal A}\ \underset{\Lambda_{_0}}{\otimes}\ CF^{^{\bullet}}((\mathbb{L},b),U)  
\end{equation}  
is   

\begin{equation}   
\mu_{_1}^{^{b,0}}(p)\ =\ \sum_{_{k=0}}^{^{\infty}}\mu_{_{k+1}}(b,...,b,p)  \ \ \ \ , \ \ \ \ p\ \in\ CF^{^{\bullet}}((\mathbb{L},b),U)
\end{equation}   

The mirror MF counts pseudoholomorphic strips bounded by $\mathbb{L}$ and $U$, where an arbitrary number of boundary intersection $b$'s are allowed along the upper boundary mapped in $\mathbb{L}$.    

The correspondence between objects defined above extends to be an $A_{_{\infty}}$-functor 

\begin{equation}  
{\cal F}\ =\ \left({\cal F}_{_k}\right)_{_{k\ge1}}^{^{\infty}}  
\end{equation}  
as follows. Given an intesection point, $q$ between two Lagrangians, $U_{_1}, U_{_2}$, in Fuk$(X)$, which can be regarded as a morphism from $U_{_1}$ to $U_{_2}$, we have the morphism  

\begin{equation}  
(-1)^{^{\text{deg}(q)-1)(\text{deg}(.)-1)}}\mu_{_2}^{^{b,0,0}}(.,q):\ {\cal F}(U_{_1})\ \longrightarrow\ {\cal F}(U_{_2})  
\end{equation}    

Given an intersection point, $p\in\mathbb{L}\cap U_{_1}$, 

\begin{equation}  
(-1)^{^{(\text{deg}(q)-1)(\text{deg}(p)-1)}}\mu_{_2}^{^{b,0,0}}(p,q)\ = \ (-1)^{^{(\text{deg}(q)-1)(\text{deg}(p)-1)}}\sum_{_{k=0}}^{^{\infty}}\mu_{_{k+2}}(b,...,b,p,q)  
\end{equation}  
defining an element in the underlying module of ${\cal F}(U_{_2})$. This defines a map  

\begin{equation}  
{\cal F}_{_1}^{^{\mathbb{L}}}:\ \text{Hom}(U_{_1},U_{_2})\ \longrightarrow\ \text{Hom}\left({\cal F}(U_{_1}, {\cal F}(U_{_2})\right)  
\end{equation}  
where  

\begin{equation}  
\left(q_{_1},...,q_{_l}\right)\ \in\ \text{Hom}\left(U_{_1}, U_{_2}\right)\ \otimes\ ...\ \otimes\   \text{Hom}\left(U_{_l}, U_{_{l+1}}\right)
\end{equation}    
sending   


\begin{equation}   
p\ \in\ \mathbb{L}\cap U_{_1}\ \mapsto\ (-1)^{^{\epsilon}}\mu_{_{l+1}}^{^{b,0,...,0}}\left(p,q_{_1},...,q_{_l}\right)  
\end{equation}  
with    

\begin{equation}  
\epsilon\ =\ \left(\text{deg} p-1\right)\left(\sum_{_{i=1}}^{^l}\left(\text{deg}q_{_i}-1\right)\right)  
\end{equation}   

To prove that ${\cal F}$ is actually an $A_{_{\infty}}$functor, it is essential to take the underlying ring to be   

\begin{equation}  
{\cal A}\ = \ \Lambda_{_0}\left<\left<x_{_e}:\ e\in E\right>\right>\bigg/\left<P_{_f}:\ f\ \in\ F\right>    
\end{equation} 
rather than $\Lambda_{_0}$. Over this ring,  

\begin{equation}  
\mu_{_{0,\mathbb{L}}}^{^b}\ = \ {\cal W}\ \cdot\ \mathbf{1}_{_{\mathbb{L}}}  
\end{equation}     


${\cal F}_{_l}\left(q_{_1},...,q_{_l}\right)$ defined above are truly ${\cal A}$-module homomorphisms, since     

\begin{equation}   
\mu_{_{k+l+1}}\left(b,...,b,g\cdot p, q_{_1},...,q_{_l}\right)\ =\ g\cdot\mu_{_{k+l+1}}\left(b,...,b, p, q_{_1},...,q_{_l}\right)  
\end{equation}



\subsection{Elliptic curves and deformation quantisation}       

Applying the technique outlined above to the elliptic curve quotient  

\begin{equation}  
E\bigg/\mathbb{Z}_{_3}\ =\ \mathbb{P}^{^1}_{_{3,3,3}}    
\end{equation}   
to achieve a family of noncommutative mirrors.   Considering a family of reference Lagrangians, $\left(\mathbb{L}_{_t}, \lambda\right)$ producing the mirror functor whose target is the noncommutative LG model of 3D Skyanin algebras with central elements.    


\medskip   

\medskip    

\underline{Theorem} [CHL]     


There is a $T^{^2}$-family $(\mathbb{L},\lambda)$ of Lagrangians decorated by flat $U(1)$-connections in $\mathbb{P}_{_{3,3,3}}^{^1}$ for $(-1)\ \in\ \mathbb{R}/2\mathbb{Z}$ and $\lambda\in U(1)$ whose corresponding generated mirror 

\begin{equation}   
\left({\cal A}_{_{(\lambda, t)}}, {\cal W}_{_{(\lambda, t)}}\right)   
\end{equation}   
satisfies the following:   

\begin{enumerate}   

\item   The noncommutative algebras are Sklyanin algebras of the form   

\begin{equation}  
{\cal A}_{_{(\lambda,t)}}\ =\ \frac{\Lambda\ <x,y,z>}{(axy+byx+cz^{^2},ayz+bzy+cx^{^2}, azx+bxz+cy^{^2})}  
\end{equation}  

\item   ${\cal W}_{_{(\lambda, t)}}$ lies in the center of ${\cal A}_{_{(\lambda,t)}}\ \ \ \forall(\lambda,t)$.

\item   The coefficients $(a:b:c)$ are given by theta-functions defining an embedding   

\begin{equation}    
E_{_{\tau}}\ \longrightarrow\ \mathbb{P}^{^2}  
\end{equation}  
onto the mirror elliptic curve  

\begin{equation}  
\hat E\ = \ \left\{(a:b:c)\ \in\ \mathbb{P}^{^2}\ \bigg|\ {\cal W}_{_0}(x,y,z)\ =\ 0\right\}  
\end{equation}

\item   $\forall\ (\lambda, t)$, there is a $\mathbb{Z}_{_2}$-graded $A_{_{\infty}}$-functor  

\begin{equation}   
{\cal F}^{^{(\mathbb{L},t)}}:\ \text{Fuk}\left(\mathbb{P}_{_{3,3,3}}^{^1}\right)\ \longrightarrow\ \text{MF}\left(    {\cal A}_{_{(\lambda,t)}}, {\cal W}_{_{(\lambda,t)}}\right)
\end{equation}   

\item  The graded noncommutative algebra   

\begin{equation}  
