and $\cal T$ containing lognormal peaks 
[cf.~Eqs.~\eqref{eq:ps-peak} and~\eqref{eq:trispec-paramet-2}].
We also indicate the regimes where $\ph^V(k)$ and $\ph^I(k)$ turn negative 
(in green and orange respectively).
We set the parameters to be $\alpha=10$, $k_{\rm t}=k_{\rm p}$, 
$\sigma^2_{\rm p}=10^{-2}$ and others as mentioned in the text.
We vary $\sigma^2_{\rm t}=[10^{-4},10^{-2},1]$ to inspect the effect of such 
an intrinsic scale-dependence of $\cal T$ on $\ph^V(k)$ and $\ph^I(k)$.
We learn that for $\sigma^2_{\rm t}=10^{-4}$, the contribution from $\cal T$
to $\ph^I$ and $\ph^V$ is lesser and hence $\Pi_k\leq 10^{-2}$.
For $\sigma^2_{\rm t}=10^{-2}=\sigma^2_{\rm p}$, the contributions from $\cal T$
and $\ps$ to $\ph^I$ compete closely around $k_{\rm p}$, leading to corrugated
features in it. Further, $\ph^V$ becomes comparable and even dominant over 
$\ph^I$ in this brief window.
When $\sigma^2_{\rm t}=1$, the peak just rescales $\gnl$ and $\tgnl$ and
