\label{fig:nematic}
\end{figure} 

On the other hand, Congjun Wu, Kai Sun, Shoucheng Zhang and I showed that a Pomeranchuk instability in the spin triplet channel leads to generalizations of the nematic phase in the spin channel \cite{Wu-2007}. One example is the ``nematic-spin-nematic'' phase, conjectured in Ref.\cite{Kivelson-2003} and shown in Fig.\ref{fig:nematic-spin-nematic}. This state is invariant under a $\pi/2$ spatial rotation followed by a spin flip. This state has the same symmetry of the \textit{altermagnet} states which are being widely discussed in the current literature \cite{Smejkal-2022}. In Ref.\cite{Wu-2007} it is also shown that there is a second class of anisotropic phases in the triplet channel in which the spin polarization \textit{winds} twice (for $\ell=2$) around the Fermi surface with a non-vanishing Berry phase.

