The processed stress and strain data were then used to determine the Young’s modulus ($E$) and yield stress ($\sigma_\mathrm{yield}$) for the different MFAs, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:DIC_prot}. Note that compressive strains are defined as positive. The modulus $E$ was calculated as the slope of a linear fit applied to each unloading segment of the stress-strain curve. For the yield stress $\sigma_\mathrm{yield}$, an envelope with the maximum stress per strain value was computed to exclude the unloading segments. A moving regression with a constant window size ($\approx$ \SI{0.5}{\percent}) was applied, and the segment with maximum slope was used to define the yield stress using the \SI{0.2}{\percent} strain offset criterion as done in previous works~\cite{schwiedrzik2016identification}.

